Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "activity_cliff_geometry_probe",
  "Title": "Smooth Where It Should Jump: Do Chemistry Foundation Models Internally Represent Activity Cliffs as Pharmacophore-Localized Discontinuities, or Just Smooth Structural Similarity?",
  "Short Hypothesis": "Activity cliffs \u2014 pairs of near-identical molecules with large potency differences \u2014 are exactly where molecular property models fail. We hypothesize this failure has a mechanistic signature: chemistry foundation models embed structurally-similar molecules close together regardless of whether a tiny change flips potency, so their internal representation is locally SMOOTH across cliffs (structure-driven) rather than DISCONTINUOUS at the potency-flipping substructure (mechanism-driven). If instead a model represents the underlying pharmacophore mechanism, then (a) cliff-forming matched transformations should produce a larger, localized representational jump than potency-neutral (non-cliff) matched transformations of comparable structural magnitude, and (b) that jump should causally localize to the pharmacophore-relevant substructure via activation patching. The exact matched-molecular-pair cliff-vs-non-cliff counterfactual makes this a mechanism-defining test of WHY cliffs are hard, not another cliff predictor.",
  "Related Work": "A large body of work treats activity cliffs (ACs) as a prediction problem: MoleculeACE (Exposing the Limitations, 2022) shows all ML methods struggle at ACs and descriptor methods beat deep models; ACNet, ACES-GNN, ACANet, ACtriplet, and SCAGE (Nature Comm 2025) build AC-aware predictors or explanation-supervised GNNs. Separately, interpretability of chemistry models (Unveiling Latent Knowledge via SAEs 2025; MoleX 2024; concept-whitening GNNs) recovers concept features aligned with functional groups and pharmacological classes, and roughness/RePRA analyses (ROGI-XD 2023; RePRA 2023) show pretrained representations do NOT produce smoother QSPR surfaces. Crucially, none of these asks the mechanism-defining question: is the AC failure caused by the model's internal representation being locally smooth across cliffs (blind to the discontinuity), and does the representational change at a cliff causally localize to the pharmacophore substructure that actually flips potency? Prior AC work measures output error or trains cliff-aware losses; prior interpretability work matches features to marginal annotations. Our exact cliff-vs-matched-non-cliff counterfactual, representational-smoothness geometry, and causal pharmacophore-localization patching are the novel contribution, explaining WHY cliffs are hard at the representational level rather than engineering around them.",
  "Abstract": "Activity cliffs \u2014 pairs of molecules differing by a minor structural change yet showing large potency differences \u2014 are the single most reliable failure mode of molecular property models, but why they are hard remains a black box. We propose a mechanism-defining test of whether chemistry foundation models represent the pharmacophore mechanism behind cliffs or merely encode structural similarity. Our central hypothesis is that these models embed structurally-similar molecules close together regardless of potency, making their internal representation locally SMOOTH across a cliff \u2014 blind to the very discontinuity that defines it \u2014 which would mechanistically explain the observed failures. We test this with an exact counterfactual built from matched molecular pairs (MMPs): cliff pairs (same transformation, large potency jump) versus matched non-cliff pairs (comparable structural transformation, negligible potency change) against the same targets, drawn from MoleculeACE and ChEMBL. First, we measure the internal representational distance induced by each transformation across layers in models such as ChemBERTa/MoleculeSTM, SMI-TED (FM4M), and Uni-Mol, testing whether cliff transformations produce larger, sharper representational jumps than matched non-cliff transformations \u2014 or whether both are equally smooth (the failure signature). Second, using sparse autoencoders and linear probes we test whether a dedicated feature/direction tracks potency independent of structure. Third, via activation patching we test whether the cliff-induced representational change causally localizes to the pharmacophore-relevant substructure (e.g., the atom whose modification creates a steric clash or breaks a hydrogen bond), versus being diffuse. Metrics quantify a cliff-vs-non-cliff jump ratio, a potency-beyond-structure decoding index, and a pharmacophore-localization score. Either outcome sharpens why deployed chemistry models fail precisely where drug discovery needs them most.",
  "Experiments": [
    "1. Models and data: Load several public chemistry foundation models spanning representation types \u2014 a SMILES transformer (ChemBERTa-2 or MoleculeSTM text encoder), a masked chemistry FM (SMI-TED / FM4M), and a 3D/graph model (Uni-Mol) \u2014 plus an ECFP-fingerprint baseline (known to beat deep models at cliffs). Assemble matched molecular pairs from MoleculeACE (30 targets) and ChEMBL: (a) cliff pairs (Tanimoto similarity high, |\u0394pIC50| large, same target), and (b) matched NON-cliff pairs applying comparable transformation types to the same scaffolds against the same targets but with negligible |\u0394pIC50|. Match cliff and non-cliff sets on structural-transformation magnitude (fingerprint distance, atoms changed) so the only difference is the potency consequence. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.",
    "2. Representational-smoothness test (primary): For each pair, extract per-molecule representations at each layer and compute the transformation-induced representational distance (cosine / Euclidean). Primary metric: Cliff-vs-Non-cliff Jump Ratio = median representational distance(cliff pairs) / median representational distance(matched non-cliff pairs), reported per layer and per model. A mechanism-aware model gives ratio >> 1 (cliffs jump more); the hypothesized failure signature is ratio \u2248 1 (representation is locally smooth, blind to the cliff). Compare against the ECFP baseline (expected ratio \u2248 1 by construction, since it is purely structural) to calibrate.",
    "3. Potency-beyond-structure decoding: Train a linear probe / regress potency (pIC50) from internal representations, then test whether, WITHIN matched pairs (holding structure change fixed), the representation moves in the potency-correct direction. Metric: Potency-Beyond-Structure Index = accuracy of predicting which molecule of a matched pair is more potent, minus the accuracy achievable from fingerprint distance alone. Positive index means the model encodes potency information beyond raw structural similarity.",
    "4. SAE / feature-level analysis: Train sparse autoencoders on the models' activations and search for feature(s) whose activation tracks potency across cliff pairs while remaining stable across matched non-cliff pairs of similar structure. Report whether any monosemantic 'potency-discontinuity' or pharmacophore feature exists, versus potency being smeared across structure features.",
    "5. Causal pharmacophore-localization via patching: For each cliff pair, identify the pharmacophore-relevant substructure (the differing atom/group whose modification flips potency \u2014 from the MMP transformation and, where available, docking/known SAR annotations). Patch the internal representation (token/atom activations) of the low-potency molecule with those of the high-potency molecule at (a) the pharmacophore substructure only versus (b) matched-size off-target substructures, and measure the change in the model's potency prediction. Metric: Pharmacophore-Localization Score = |\u0394prediction from patching pharmacophore region| / |\u0394prediction from patching matched off-target region|. Mechanistic representation => score >> 1 (the cliff effect is causally carried by the pharmacophore substructure); diffuse/structural representation => score \u2248 1.",
    "6. Failure-prediction link: Test whether per-pair representational smoothness (small cliff jump / low pharmacophore-localization) predicts where the model's downstream potency prediction is wrong. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error cliff pairs from the internal jump ratio, compared to output-confidence baselines. This directly connects the representational signature to the documented AC failure mode.",
    "7. Cross-model and layer analysis: Report at which layers cliff-sensitivity (if any) emerges, whether it correlates with downstream AC accuracy across models, and whether 3D/graph models (Uni-Mol) show stronger pharmacophore localization than SMILES models (testing whether geometry-aware pretraining internalizes the steric/H-bond mechanism better).",
    "8. Brackets: Compare against (upper bound) an AC-aware model (SCAGE or ACANet representations) expected to show larger cliff jumps and stronger localization, and (lower bound) the ECFP fingerprint, to place foundation models on the structure-only-to-mechanism spectrum."
  ],
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": [
    "Matching cliff and non-cliff pairs on structural-transformation magnitude is imperfect; residual structural differences could confound the jump ratio. We mitigate with tight fingerprint-distance and atoms-changed matching and report sensitivity to the matching threshold.",
    "Pharmacophore-relevant substructure assignment from MMP transformations may be ambiguous for pairs with multiple simultaneous changes; we restrict causal-patching to single-transformation MMP-cliffs and, where available, use docking/known SAR to confirm the responsible group.",
    "Some FMs pool to a single molecule embedding, limiting token/atom-level patching; we prioritize models with per-atom/per-token activations (Uni-Mol, ChemBERTa) for the localization test and use whole-molecule patching as a coarser fallback.",
    "Potency labels (pIC50) are assay-dependent and noisy across ChEMBL sources; we use MoleculeACE's curated targets and report robustness to potency-difference thresholds defining a cliff.",
    "A finding that models are locally smooth across cliffs (ratio \u2248 1) is a negative result about mechanism, but it is precisely the mechanistic explanation for the well-documented AC failure mode and is decision-relevant for trusting these models in lead optimization; we frame either outcome as explaining WHY cliffs are hard at the representational level.",
    "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes/SAEs and public benchmark data (MoleculeACE, ChEMBL), keeping compute at academic scale."
  ]
}}
