ions — or whether both are equally smooth (the failure signature). Second, using sparse autoencoders and linear probes we test whether a dedicated feature/direction tracks potency independent of structure. Third, via activation patching we test whether the cliff-induced representational change causally localizes to the pharmacophore-relevant substructure (e.g., the atom whose modification creates a steric clash or breaks a hydrogen bond), versus being diffuse. Metrics quantify a cliff-vs-non-cliff jump ratio, a potency-beyond-structure decoding index, and a pharmacophore-localization score. Either outcome sharpens why deployed chemistry models fail precisely where drug discovery needs them most.', 'Experiments': ['1. Models and data: Load several public chemistry foundation models spanning representation types — a SMILES transformer (ChemBERTa-2 or MoleculeSTM text encoder), a masked chemistry FM (SMI-TED / FM4M), and a 3D/graph model (Uni-Mol) — plus an ECFP-fingerprint baseline (known to beat deep models at cliffs). Assemble matched molecular pairs from MoleculeACE (30 targets) and ChEMBL: (a) cliff pairs (Tanimoto similarity high, |ΔpIC50| large, same target), and (b) matched NON-cliff pairs applying comparable transformation types to the same scaffolds against the same targets but with negligible |ΔpIC50|. Match cliff and non-cliff sets on structural-transformation magnitude (fingerprint distance, atoms changed) so the only difference is the potency consequence. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.', '2. Representational-smoothness test (primary): For each pair, extract per-molecule representations at each layer and compute the transformation-induced representational distance (cosine / Euclidean). Primary metric: Cliff-vs-Non-cliff Jump Ratio = median representational distance(cliff pairs) / median representational distance(matched non-cliff pairs), reported per layer and per model. A mechanism-aware model gives ratio >> 1 (cliffs jump more); the hypothesized failure signature is ratio ≈ 1 (representation is locally smooth, blind to the cliff). Compare against the ECFP baseline (expected ratio ≈ 1 by construction, since it is purely structural) to calibrate.', '3. Potency-beyond-structure decoding: Train a linear probe / regress potency (pIC50) from internal representations, then test whether, WITHIN matched pairs (holding structure change fixed), the representation moves in the potency-correct direction. Metric: Potency-Beyond-Structure Index = accuracy of predicting which molecule of a matched pair is more potent, minus the accuracy achievable from fingerprint distance alone. Positive index means the model encodes potency information beyond raw structural similarity.', "4. SAE / feature-level analysis: Train sparse autoencoders on the models' activations and search for feature(s) whose activation tracks potency across cliff pairs while remaining stable across matched non-cliff pairs of similar structure. Report whether any monosemantic 'potency-discontinuity' or pharmacophore feature exists, versus potency being smeared across structure features.", "5. Causal pharmacophore-localization via patching: For each cliff pair, identify the pharmacophore-relevant substructure (the differing atom/group whose modification flips potency — from the MMP transformation and, where available, docking/known SAR annotations). Patch the internal representation (token/atom activations) of the low-potency molecule with those of the high-potency molecule at (a) the pharmacophore substructure only versus (b) matched-size off-target substructures, and measure the change in the model's potency prediction. Metric: Pharmacophore-Localization Score = |Δprediction from patching pharmacophore region| / |Δprediction from patching matched off-target region|. Mechanistic representation => score >> 1 (the cliff effect is causally carried by the pharmacophore substructure); diffuse/structural representation => score ≈ 1.", "6. Failure-pre