Response Content: {"total": 5, "offset": 0, "data": [{"paperId": "723c88691eb57f7557fe8f43263a60e267b87b52", "title": "Finetuning-Free Diffusion Model with Adaptive Constraint Guidance for Inorganic Crystal Structure Generation", "venue": "arXiv.org", "year": 2026, "citationCount": 0, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.13354, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with thi
Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "convex_hull_competition_probe",
  "Title": "Stable Relative to What? Testing Whether Crystal Foundation Models Represent the Relational Convex-Hull Competition of Thermodynamic Stability, Not Just Pointwise Formation Energy",
  "Short Hypothesis": "Thermodynamic stability is not a pointwise property of a compound but a relational, competitive one: a phase is stable only if no combination of competing phases at the same composition has lower energy (its energy above the convex hull). We hypothesize that crystal foundation models\u2014now routinely conditioned on 'distance to hull' to generate 'stable' materials\u2014internally encode pointwise formation energy (a correlational proxy) rather than the relational competition that actually defines stability. A mechanistic representation must change a compound's internal stability signal when its competing neighbors in composition space change, even though the compound itself is untouched; a pointwise representation will not. This competitor-context counterfactual is a clean, mechanism-defining test that correlation with hull distance on the standard database cannot provide.",
  "Related Work": "Crystal foundation models are explicitly built around convex-hull stability: Matra-Genoa (2026) and UniMat diffusion (2023) condition generation on distance-to-hull; CLOUD (2025) is a physics-informed crystal FM; Matbench Discovery (2025) shows the decision-critical boundary is 0 eV/atom above the hull, where accurate regressors incur high false-positive rates. The 'seven-facet polyhedron' work (2026) shows the formation-energy hull has a compact, interpretable relational structure, and Sun et al. (2016) quantified the thermodynamic scale of metastability. Interpretability of materials models (CGCNN interpretability 2018; 'Comparing latent features of uMLIPs' 2025; 'Beyond Converging Representations' 2025; 'Occupancies vs Embeddings' 2025) recovers site-energy contributions, cross-model feature alignment, and DFT-like internal structure\u2014but all examine POINTWISE energetics or generic latent geometry. None tests whether a crystal FM's internal representation encodes the RELATIONAL, COMPETITIVE convex-hull mechanism that distinguishes formation energy from actual stability, nor whether that internal signal is causally sensitive to the competitor context. Because hull distance and formation energy are highly correlated on the standard database, correlation-based benchmarks cannot separate the two; our competitor-context counterfactual is the novel, mechanism-defining contribution.",
  "Abstract": "Machine-learning models of crystals are now used to discover 'stable' inorganic materials, and stability is defined by the convex hull of formation energies: a compound is stable only if no linear combination of competing phases at the same composition has lower energy. Stability is therefore intrinsically relational\u2014a compound's fate depends on its competitors\u2014yet it is nearly perfectly correlated with pointwise formation energy on the standard Materials Project database, so ordinary benchmarks cannot tell whether models learn the relational competition or merely a pointwise energy proxy. We propose the first mechanism-defining test. Using public crystal foundation models (a generative model conditioned on hull distance such as Matra-Genoa/UniMat, a crystal representation FM such as CLOUD, and a universal MLIP such as MACE-MP-0), we ask whether their internal representations encode the convex-hull competition structure. First, a relational-beyond-pointwise probe: can we linearly decode a compound's energy-above-hull from its representation beyond what its own formation energy predicts, and is a distinct direction dedicated to 'competitive margin'? Second, and most decisively, a competitor-context counterfactual: we present the same target compound in two composition-space contexts that differ only in which competing phases exist (constructed from Materials Project phase diagrams), such that the target is on-hull in one context and above-hull in the other. A mechanistic representation must shift its internal stability signal and its generation/classification decision accordingly; a pointwise representation will not. We validate causally by steering the recovered competition direction. Metrics quantify a relational-beyond-pointwise index, a competitor-context sensitivity ratio, and a hull-boundary failure predictor. Either outcome sharpens what deployed materials-discovery models actually represent.",
  "Experiments": [
    "1. Models and data: Load public crystal FMs spanning uses\u2014a hull-conditioned generative model (Matra-Genoa or UniMat diffusion), a crystal representation FM (CLOUD), and a universal MLIP (MACE-MP-0). From the Materials Project, extract for each compound its DFT formation energy, its energy above the convex hull (E_hull), the phase diagram of its chemical system, and the set of competing (decomposition) phases that define its local hull facet. Extract internal representations (per-structure embeddings / pooled activations) for each model. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.",
    "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode formation energy and E_hull from internal representations, confirming both are encoded and selecting best layers (replicating prior pointwise-energy findings as a control).",
    "3. Relational-beyond-pointwise probe: Test whether E_hull is decodable BEYOND the compound's own formation energy. Regress out the compound's formation-energy-predictable component and re-decode E_hull from the residual representation. Primary metric: Relational-Beyond-Pointwise Index = probe R^2 for E_hull from model representation minus R^2 achievable from formation energy alone. A positive index indicates the model encodes competitive-margin information beyond pointwise energetics. Also search (via a supervised direction and, where feasible, sparse autoencoders) for a dedicated 'competitive margin / stability' direction distinct from the formation-energy direction, quantifying their cosine separation.",
    "4. Competitor-context counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For a curated set of target compositions, construct two contexts from Materials Project phase diagrams that leave the target structure identical but change its competitive environment: (a) a context where the target is ON the convex hull (stable), and (b) a context where an additional lower-lying competing phase (or a phase mixture) exists so the SAME target is ABOVE the hull (unstable). For representation FMs/MLIPs, provide the competing phases as accompanying context (e.g., co-embedded reference set / conditioning) or measure the internal stability readout relative to the constructed local hull; for the generative model, condition on the two contexts and measure the generated stability distribution for the target composition. Metric: Competitor-Context Sensitivity Ratio = |change in internal stability signal (or decision) between context (a) and (b)| / |change under a control context edit that alters unrelated phases far from the target composition, leaving its hull facet unchanged|. Mechanistic (relational) representation => ratio >> 1; pointwise representation => ratio ~ 1.",
    "5. Facet-locality control: A genuine hull mechanism should be sensitive only to competitors that touch the target's hull facet, not to distant phases. Test whether internal stability shifts scale with whether an added/removed competitor actually changes the local facet (computed exactly from the phase diagram), versus distant compositional changes that do not. Metric: correlation between internal-signal shift and true facet-change indicator, with distant-phase edits as negative controls.",
    "6. Causal competition-direction steering: Using the recovered competitive-margin direction, add matched-magnitude perturbations to a target's representation and test whether the model's stability classification / generation propensity moves in the hull-consistent direction; matched-norm random and formation-energy-direction perturbations serve as controls. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of competition direction| / |effect of random direction|, and whether steering can flip an on-hull compound to predicted-unstable without changing its intrinsic formation-energy readout.",
    "7. Hull-boundary failure prediction: Matbench Discovery shows models fail most near E_hull = 0. Test whether an internal 'competition-representation strength' score (probe confidence for the residual E_hull signal, or competitor-context sensitivity) predicts, per compound, where the model misclassifies stable vs metastable. Metric: AUROC for flagging near-boundary errors from the internal signal, compared to output-confidence and formation-energy-only baselines.",
    "8. Cross-model and layer analysis: Report where competitive-margin structure emerges across depth and whether the recovered competition subspaces align across the three model types (CKA), assessing whether relational hull structure is a convergent internal property or absent/model-specific. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) the interpretable seven-facet composition-only hull model's relational parameters and (lower bound) a pure formation-energy regressor with no competitor access."
  ],
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": [
    "Formation energy and E_hull are strongly correlated on the standard database, making the relational-beyond-pointwise separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with residualization, the competitor-context counterfactual (which holds the target fixed), and facet-locality controls.",
    "Providing 'competitor context' is architecture-dependent: representation FMs and MLIPs do not natively take a phase-diagram context, so context must be injected via co-embedded reference sets or by evaluating the internal readout relative to a constructed local hull; we report per-model how context is supplied and treat the hull-conditioned generative model as the cleanest testbed since it explicitly ingests distance-to-hull.",
    "Constructing counterfactual contexts requires curated phase diagrams and, ideally, hypothetical competing phases; we use Materials Project phase diagrams and real competing phases where possible, and clearly flag any synthetic competitors.",
    "Linear probes recovering a competition direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering and random/formation-energy-direction controls, though distributed encodings may blur single-direction interventions.",
    "Some models pool to a single structure embedding, limiting fine-grained analysis; we prioritize models exposing usable per-structure representations and report pooling caveats.",
    "A negative result (models encode pointwise formation energy but not the relational competition) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting hull-conditioned generative discovery pipelines, though it may be perceived as confirmatory of skepticism; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether crystal FMs represent the convex-hull competition that defines stability.",
    "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes/SAEs and public Materials Project data, keeping compute at academic scale."
  ]
}}
