direction distinct from the formation-energy direction, quantifying their cosine separation.", '4. Competitor-context counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For a curated set of target compositions, construct two contexts from Materials Project phase diagrams that leave the target structure identical but change its competitive environment: (a) a context where the target is ON the convex hull (stable), and (b) a context where an additional lower-lying competing phase (or a phase mixture) exists so the SAME target is ABOVE the hull (unstable). For representation FMs/MLIPs, provide the competing phases as accompanying context (e.g., co-embedded reference set / conditioning) or measure the internal stability readout relative to the constructed local hull; for the generative model, condition on the two contexts and measure the generated stability distribution for the target composition. Metric: Competitor-Context Sensitivity Ratio = |change in internal stability signal (or decision) between context (a) and (b)| / |change under a control context edit that alters unrelated phases far from the target composition, leaving its hull facet unchanged|. Mechanistic (relational) representation => ratio >> 1; pointwise representation => ratio ~ 1.', "5. Facet-locality control: A genuine hull mechanism should be sensitive only to competitors that touch the target's hull facet, not to distant phases. Test whether internal stability shifts scale with whether an added/removed competitor actually changes the local facet (computed exactly from the phase diagram), versus distant compositional changes that do not. Metric: correlation between internal-signal shift and true facet-change indicator, with distant-phase edits as negative controls.", "6. Causal competition-direction steering: Using the recovered competitive-margin direction, add matched-magnitude perturbations to a target's representation and test whether the model's stability classification / generation propensity moves in the