Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "phase_amplitude_coupling_probe", "Title": "Coupling, Not Just Power: Do EEG Foundation Models Represent Cross-Frequency Phase-Amplitude Coupling, or Only Marginal Band Power?", "Short Hypothesis": "A defining, relational mechanism of brain activity is cross-frequency phase-amplitude coupling (PAC): the phase of a slow oscillation (e.g., theta/alpha) modulates the amplitude of a fast oscillation (e.g., gamma), a coupling that carries cognitive and clinical meaning and cannot be captured by marginal band power. We hypothesize that if EEG foundation models represent this neurophysiological mechanism rather than surface spectral statistics, then (a) PAC strength will be decodable from internal representations beyond what per-band power predicts, (b) a phase-scrambling counterfactual that destroys coupling while preserving each band's power spectrum will specifically collapse the internal PAC signal, whereas an amplitude-matched control that changes power but preserves coupling will not, and (c) steering a recovered PAC direction causally shifts downstream predictions. The phase-scramble-vs-power-match counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that marginal-spectral interpretability cannot provide.", "Related Work": "EEG foundation models (CSBrain 2025, BrainOmni 2025, CodeBrain 2025, MSBraM 2026, LaBraM, CBraMod) achieve strong cross-task decoding, and reviews (Kwon & Shin 2025) call for physiology-aware, interpretable representations. Existing EEG-model interpretability is (i) saliency/attribution over frequency bands (Frequency-band GNN dementia biomarkers 2025; activation-maximization for spectral features 2021), or (ii) descriptive alignment of learned features with spectral rhythms (CodeBrain's tokenizer). Neuroscience establishes PAC and cross-frequency coupling (CFC) as a core relational coding mechanism (Cracking the Code of Oscillatory Activity 2011; oscillatory representational content studies), and Riemannian pipelines have hand-engineered CFC covariance features (SPD-CFC 2023) precisely because standard band-power features miss it. Crucially, no work tests whether pretrained EEG FMs internally represent PAC (a relational, cross-frequency mechanism) beyond marginal band power, whether that representation is causally load-bearing, or uses a phase-scrambling counterfactual that isolates coupling from power. Prior interpretability answers 'which band matters'; PAC asks 'do bands interact as the brain's mechanism dictates'. Our relational-beyond-marginal probe, phase-scramble-vs-power-match counterfactual, and causal PAC steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, transferred into a safety-critical clinical/BCI setting.", "Abstract": "EEG foundation models now deliver strong, transferable decoding across cognitive, emotional, and clinical tasks, yet what neurophysiology they actually internalize remains unclear. Existing interpretability tells us which frequency bands a model weights, but band power is a marginal, per-band statistic; the brain's information is carried substantially by relational mechanisms, chief among them cross-frequency phase-amplitude coupling (PAC), in which the phase of a slow rhythm modulates the amplitude of a fast one. PAC is diagnostically and cognitively meaningful (working memory, seizure dynamics, Parkinsonian beta-gamma coupling) and is provably invisible to marginal band power, making it an ideal mechanism-defining probe. We ask whether pretrained EEG foundation models (e.g., LaBraM, CBraMod, CodeBrain) internally represent PAC. First, a relational-beyond-marginal probe: we test whether PAC strength (modulation index) is decodable from internal representations beyond what per-band power predicts. Second, and most decisively, a phase-scrambling counterfactual: we surrogate-scramble the slow-oscillation phase to abolish coupling while exactly preserving each band's power spectrum, and compare against an amplitude-matched control that alters power but preserves coupling. A mechanistic representation must collapse its internal PAC signal under phase-scrambling but not under the power-matched control; a spectral-statistics model responds oppositely. Third, causal steering: perturbing a recovered PAC direction should shift downstream clinical/cognitive predictions coupling-consistently. Metrics quantify a PAC-beyond-power index, a scramble-vs-power-match causal asymmetry, and a mechanism-based failure predictor. Either outcome sharpens what deployed brain foundation models represent, with direct implications for their clinical and BCI reliability.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load public EEG foundation models with accessible weights and per-timestep/per-token representations (e.g., LaBraM, CBraMod, CodeBrain). Assemble EEG datasets with established PAC phenomena and labels: working-memory / cognitive-load tasks (theta-gamma PAC), sleep staging (slow-oscillation-spindle coupling), and clinical cohorts where PAC is a known biomarker (e.g., epilepsy/seizure recordings, Parkinsonian beta-gamma). For every epoch, compute ground-truth PAC via the modulation index (Tort) and comodulograms across phase-frequency/amplitude-frequency pairs, plus per-band power (delta-gamma). All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode per-band power and the PAC modulation index from internal representations across layers, confirming what is encoded and selecting best layers. Establish that band power is strongly decodable (expected) as a reference.", "3. Relational-beyond-marginal test (primary probe): Test whether PAC is decodable BEYOND per-band power. Regress out the per-band-power-predictable component of the representation and re-decode the modulation index from the residual. Primary metric: PAC-Beyond-Power Index = probe R^2 for modulation index from model representation minus R^2 achievable from the full per-band power vector alone. A positive index indicates encoded relational cross-frequency structure beyond marginal spectra. Report per phase-amplitude band pair (e.g., theta-gamma, alpha-gamma).", "4. Phase-scrambling counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For each epoch, construct two matched surrogates. (a) Phase-scramble surrogate: apply amplitude-adjusted Fourier-transform / phase-randomization surrogates that destroy cross-frequency phase relationships (abolishing PAC) while EXACTLY preserving each frequency's power spectrum. (b) Power-matched control: rescale band amplitudes to change per-band power by a comparable amount while preserving the phase relationships (coupling intact). Feed both to the model and measure the change in its internal PAC signal (probed modulation index / recovered PAC feature) and, where applicable, downstream prediction. Metric: Scramble-vs-Power-Match Causal Asymmetry = |PAC-signal drop under phase-scramble| / |PAC-signal change under power-matched control|. Mechanistic representation => asymmetry >> 1 (scrambling collapses the internal PAC signal, power-matching does not); spectral-statistics representation => asymmetry ~ 1 (or reversed: responds to power, not coupling).", "5. Comodulogram fidelity: Test whether the model's internal PAC representation reproduces the correct phase-frequency and amplitude-frequency SPECIFICITY of the true comodulogram (e.g., theta phase modulating gamma amplitude specifically), rather than a generic 'something is coupled' signal. Metric: correlation between the model-implied coupling map (from probing PAC at each band pair) and the ground-truth comodulogram, versus a shuffled-band-pair control.", "6. Causal PAC steering: Using the recovered PAC direction (probe direction or an SAE feature aligned with coupling), add matched-magnitude perturbations to move representations toward/away from high PAC and measure whether downstream predictions (cognitive load level, sleep stage, seizure/pathology likelihood) shift coupling-consistently. Controls: matched-norm random directions and a band-power direction should not produce the coupling-consistent shift. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of PAC direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "7. Mechanism-based failure prediction: Test whether an internal 'PAC-representation strength' score predicts, per epoch/subject, where the model's clinical/cognitive prediction is wrong, especially in cohorts where PAC is the discriminative biomarker. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error cases from the internal PAC signal, compared to output-confidence and band-power-only baselines. This connects the representational signature to clinical reliability.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where PAC structure emerges across depth and whether recovered PAC subspaces align across the different foundation models (CKA), assessing whether cross-frequency coupling is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a hand-engineered CFC/Riemannian-SPD PAC feature classifier and (lower bound) a band-power-only classifier, placing FMs on the marginal-to-relational spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["PAC and band power are correlated in real EEG (high-power epochs can inflate spurious PAC), making the relational-beyond-power separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with power-residualization, the phase-scramble-vs-power-match counterfactual (which holds power fixed), amplitude-adjusted surrogates, and established bias-corrected PAC estimators.", "Phase-scrambling surrogates that perfectly preserve power while destroying coupling are nontrivial; we use standard amplitude-adjusted Fourier-transform surrogates and validate that per-band power is preserved and modulation index is abolished before feeding to the model, reporting surrogate-quality diagnostics.", "Some EEG FMs tokenize/normalize signals (patching, spectral tokenization, per-channel normalization) that may discard or transform phase information; we document each model's preprocessing, probe pre-tokenization activations where feasible, and prioritize models retaining temporal/phase structure.", "PAC ground truth is itself estimator-dependent and noisy for short epochs; we use longer epochs where possible, multiple PAC metrics (Tort modulation index, mean vector length), and report sensitivity to estimator choice.", "Linear probes recovering a PAC direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/band-power-direction controls, though distributed encodings may blur single-direction interventions.", "A negative result (models encode marginal band power but not cross-frequency coupling) is scientifically decisive and directly clinically relevant, since it would warn against trusting EEG FMs for PAC-driven diagnoses (seizure, Parkinsonian, working-memory biomarkers); we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether brain foundation models represent cross-frequency phase-amplitude coupling.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes/SAEs and public EEG datasets, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
