Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "allosteric_propagation_probe", "Title": "Feeling the Distant Site: Do Protein Language Models Internally Represent Long-Range Allosteric Coupling, or Only Local Context?", "Short Hypothesis": "Allostery is a relational, long-range mechanism: a mutation at a site far from the active site perturbs function through a physically-propagated coupling path, and recent large-scale data show these effects decay with distance from the active site in a conserved way. We hypothesize that if protein language models (PLMs) internally represent this mechanism rather than only local sequence context, then (a) a mutation's internal representational perturbation will propagate to distant functionally-coupled residues (not just its local neighborhood), (b) the magnitude of this propagated internal signal will reproduce the conserved distance-decay law of allosteric mutational effects, and (c) the internal coupling will causally track true allosteric partners, not merely spatial or sequence proximity. A propagation-vs-locality counterfactual makes this a mechanism-defining test that local-annotation interpretability cannot provide.", "Related Work": "Existing PLM interpretability matches features to LOCAL structure/function: BERTology (attention to contacts, binding sites), InterPLM and 'Interpreting and Steering PLMs' (SAE features for binding sites, motifs, transmembrane regions), ProtoMech (cross-layer transcoder circuits for local motifs), and XAI-attribution work (active sites, PROSITE patterns). All validate against marginal, spatially-local annotations. Separately, the allostery literature establishes long-range coupling as a real mechanism: 'Allostery is a widespread cause of loss-of-function variant pathogenicity' (2025) shows a CONSERVED distance-dependent decay of allosteric mutational effects outside active sites from large-scale deep mutational scanning; coevolution methods (KeyAlloSite), MD-based GNNs (AlloPool, NRI), and energy-based predictors (ZHMolEReP) model allosteric pathways. Critically, 'When Does Structure Help?' (2026) reports ESM-2 gives below-chance allostery predictions while AF2 barely exceeds chance, hinting sequence-only PLMs may be blind to long-range mechanism. No interpretability work tests whether a PLM's INTERNAL representation propagates a mutation's effect to distant allosterically-coupled residues, reproduces the conserved distance-decay law, or causally distinguishes true allosteric partners from mere spatial/sequence neighbors. Our propagation-distance-decay-and-causal-coupling test is the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, moving beyond 'the model sees the local motif' to 'the model represents that a distant site talks to the active site.'", "Abstract": "Protein language models (PLMs) are known to encode local structural and functional features such as contacts, binding sites, and motifs. But one of the most important protein mechanisms is inherently long-range and relational: allostery, in which a mutation at a site far from the active site alters function through a physically-propagated coupling path. Recent large-scale deep-mutational-scanning studies establish that allostery is a widespread cause of variant pathogenicity and that allosteric mutational effects decay with distance from the active site in a conserved, quantifiable way. This makes allostery an ideal, physics-grounded probe for whether PLMs represent long-range mechanism or only local context. We ask whether ESM-2 internally represents allosteric coupling. First, a propagation probe: applying an in-silico mutation at a residue, we measure how far its perturbation to the model's internal representation travels along the chain and in 3D, testing whether it reaches distant functionally-coupled residues rather than only its local neighborhood. Second, and most decisively, a distance-decay test: we test whether the magnitude of the internally-propagated signal reproduces the conserved distance-dependent decay of true allosteric mutational effects measured experimentally, versus a local-context model whose perturbation stays local. Third, a coupling-specificity counterfactual: we test whether the internal coupling is stronger for true allosteric partners than for distance-matched, spatially-close-but-non-coupled control residues, and whether steering along a recovered allosteric-coupling direction causally shifts predictions at the distant partner. Metrics quantify a propagation-range index, distance-decay agreement, and coupling-specificity ratio. Either outcome sharpens whether PLMs, increasingly used for variant-effect prediction, represent the long-range mechanism behind a major class of pathogenic variants.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Use ESM-2 (150M, 650M; 3B as compute allows), inference-only plus small probes. Assemble allostery ground truth from public sources: the large-scale allosteric deep-mutational-scanning / distance-decay dataset behind 'Allostery is a widespread cause of loss-of-function variant pathogenicity' (2025), ASBench and AlloReverse allosteric-site benchmarks, and AlloSigDB allosteric-residue annotations, each paired with high-resolution PDB structures giving active-site location, 3D coordinates, and (where available) experimentally-measured per-residue allosteric mutational effect and distance to the active site.", "2. Propagation probe: For a curated set of proteins with known allosteric sites, apply single in-silico point mutations at a residue and measure the induced change (delta) in ESM-2's per-residue representations at ALL other positions, per layer. Quantify how the perturbation magnitude falls off with sequence separation and with 3D distance from the mutated site. Primary metric: Propagation-Range Index = fraction of the total representational perturbation deposited beyond a local window (e.g., >8 residues in sequence AND >10 Angstrom in 3D). A local-context model deposits almost everything locally; a mechanism-representing model propagates to distant coupled sites.", "3. Distance-decay reproduction (primary, decisive test): For mutations at allosteric sites, measure the internally-propagated perturbation arriving at the active-site residues as a function of the mutation's distance from the active site. Test whether this internal signal reproduces the CONSERVED distance-dependent decay of true allosteric mutational effects reported experimentally (fit the same decay form; compare decay constants). Metric: correlation and decay-constant agreement between internal propagated signal and experimental allosteric effect vs. distance. Controls: (a) mutations at active-site/local residues should show a different (steep, short-range) profile; (b) shuffled distance labels should destroy the fit. A number-fitting/local model will not reproduce the conserved long-range decay.", "4. Coupling-specificity counterfactual: For each true allosteric-partner pair (allosteric residue A coupled to active site or effector site F), compare the internal coupling A->F against distance-matched control residues that are equally far in sequence/3D but are NOT allosterically coupled (per experimental data). Metric: Coupling-Specificity Ratio = internal coupling(true allosteric partner) / internal coupling(distance-matched non-coupled control). Mechanistic representation => ratio >> 1; a representation driven only by spatial/sequence distance => ratio ~ 1.", "5. Prediction-level allosteric-effect decoding beyond locality: Train a probe to predict experimental per-mutation allosteric effect from the internal propagated signal at the active site, and test whether it exceeds a strong baseline using only local features (mutation identity, local secondary structure, distance-to-active-site scalar). Metric: Allostery-Beyond-Locality Index = probe R^2(propagated internal signal) minus R^2(local + distance baseline). Positive index indicates encoded long-range coupling beyond trivial distance.", "6. Causal steering of the coupling: Recover an 'allosteric-coupling' direction (via a supervised probe or SAE feature aligned with the A->F propagation). Steer this direction at the allosteric residue with matched magnitude and measure whether ESM-2's masked-prediction / variant-effect readout at the distant active-site partner shifts in the coupling-consistent direction, while a distance-matched non-coupled control residue does not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect at true coupled partner| / |effect at matched control|, with random-direction controls.", "7. Layer/scale and cross-model analysis: Report where allosteric propagation and distance-decay structure emerge across layers and how they change from ESM-2 150M to 650M to 3B, and test whether AF2 single-sequence Evoformer representations show stronger propagation than ESM-2 (motivated by the 2026 information-bonus finding that AF2 uniquely captures above-chance allostery). CKA aligns the recovered coupling subspaces across models.", "8. Brackets: Compare against (upper bound) a coevolution/MD-based allosteric-pathway predictor (KeyAlloSite / AlloPool) on the same proteins, and (lower bound) a purely local sequence-window baseline, placing PLMs on the local-to-long-range-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Allosteric coupling correlates with 3D and sequence distance and with coevolution, so the coupling-specificity separation is statistically demanding; we mitigate with distance-matched non-coupled controls, the conserved-distance-decay test (which a distance-only model cannot reproduce in the correct functional form), and the allostery-beyond-locality baseline that regresses out distance.", "The 2026 information-bonus result suggests ESM-2 may genuinely be weak at allostery; a null internal signal could reflect true model blindness rather than probing failure. We treat this as a scientifically decisive outcome (sequence-only PLMs do not represent long-range allosteric mechanism) and include AF2 representations as a positive-control comparison.", "Ground-truth allosteric coupling and experimental per-mutation allosteric effects are limited and noisy; we restrict to well-characterized proteins with deep-mutational-scanning allostery data and report sensitivity to annotation confidence.", "In-silico mutation perturbations in a nonlinear transformer may push representations off-manifold; we bound perturbation magnitude, use masked-position and single-substitution edits, and report sensitivity, relying on the input-space mutation propagation as primary evidence and steering as corroboration.", "Distributed/superposed encodings may mean no single clean coupling direction exists; we use both probe directions and SAE features and report aggregate-direction results.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models (ESM-2, optionally AF2 single-sequence) plus small probes/SAEs and public allostery datasets and structures, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'allosteric_propagation_probe', 'Title': 'Feeling the Distant Site: Do Protein Language Models Internally Represent Long-Range Allosteric Coupling, or Only Local Context?', 'Short Hypothesis': "Allostery is a relational, long-range mechanism: a mutation at a site far from the active site perturbs function through a physically-propagated coupling path, and recent large-scale data show these effects decay with distance from the active site in a conserved way. We hypothesize that if protein language models (PLMs) internally represent this mechanism rather than only local sequence context, then (a) a mutation's internal representational perturbation will propagate to distant functionally-coupled residues (not just its local neighborhood), (b) the magnitude of this propagated internal signal will reproduce the conserved distance-decay law of allosteric mutational effects, and (c) the internal coupling will causally track true allosteric partners, not merely spatial or sequence proximity. A propagation-vs-locality counterfactual makes this a mechanism-defining test that local-annotation interpretability cannot provide.", 'Related Work': "Existing PLM interpretability matches features to LOCAL structure/function: BERTology (attention to contacts, binding sites), InterPLM and 'Interpreting and Steering PLMs' (SAE features for binding sites, motifs, transmembrane regions), ProtoMech (cross-layer transcoder circuits for local motifs), and XAI-attribution work (active sites, PROSITE patterns). All validate against marginal, spatially-local annotations. Separately, the allostery literature establishes long-range coupling as a real mechanism: 'Allostery is a widespread cause of loss-of-function variant pathogenicity' (2025) shows a CONSERVED distance-dependent decay of allosteric mutational effects outside active sites from large-scale deep mutational scanning; coevolution methods (KeyAlloSite), MD-based GNNs (AlloPool, NRI), and energy-based predictors (ZHMolEReP) model allosteric pathways. Critically, 'When Does Structure Help?' (2026) reports ESM-2 gives below-chance allostery predictions while AF2 barely exceeds chance, hinting sequence-only PLMs may be blind to long-range mechanism. No interpretability work tests whether a PLM's INTERNAL representation propagates a mutation's effect to distant allosterically-coupled residues, reproduces the conserved distance-decay law, or causally distinguishes true allosteric partners from mere spatial/sequence neighbors. Our propagation-distance-decay-and-causal-coupling test is the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, moving beyond 'the model sees the local motif' to 'the model represents that a distant site talks to the active site.'", 'Abstract': "Protein language models (PLMs) are known to encode local structural and functional features such as contacts, binding sites, and motifs. But one of the most important protein mechanisms is inherently long-range and relational: allostery, in which a mutation at a site far from the active site alters function through a physically-propagated coupling path. Recent large-scale deep-mutational-scanning studies establish that allostery is a widespread cause of variant pathogenicity and that allosteric mutational effects decay with distance from the active site in a conserved, quantifiable way. This makes allostery an ideal, physics-grounded probe for whether PLMs represent long-range mechanism or only local context. We ask whether ESM-2 internally represents allosteric coupling. First, a propagation probe: applying an in-silico mutation at a residue, we measure how far its perturbation to the model's internal representation travels along the chain and in 3D, testing whether it reaches distant functionally-coupled residues rather than only its local neighborhood. Second, and most decisively, a distance-decay test: we test whether the magnitude of the internally-propagated signal reproduces the conserved distance-dependent decay of true allosteric mutational effects measured experimentally, versus a local-context model whose perturbation stays local. Third, a coupling-specificity counterfactual: we test whether the internal coupling is stronger for true allosteric partners than for distance-matched, spatially-close-but-non-coupled control residues, and whether steering along a recovered allosteric-coupling direction causally shifts predictions at the distant partner. Metrics quantify a propagation-range index, distance-decay agreement, and coupling-specificity ratio. Either outcome sharpens whether PLMs, increasingly used for variant-effect prediction, represent the long-range mechanism behind a major class of pathogenic variants.", 'Experiments': ["1. Models and data: Use ESM-2 (150M, 650M; 3B as compute allows), inference-only plus small probes. Assemble allostery ground truth from public sources: the large-scale allosteric deep-mutational-scanning / distance-decay dataset behind 'Allostery is a widespread cause of loss-of-function variant pathogenicity' (2025), ASBench and AlloReverse allosteric-site benchmarks, and AlloSigDB allosteric-residue annotations, each paired with high-resolution PDB structures giving active-site location, 3D coordinates, and (where available) experimentally-measured per-residue allosteric mutational effect and distance to the active site.", "2. Propagation probe: For a curated set of proteins with known allosteric sites, apply single in-silico point mutations at a residue and measure the induced change (delta) in ESM-2's per-residue representations at ALL other positions, per layer. Quantify how the perturbation magnitude falls off with sequence separation and with 3D distance from the mutated site. Primary metric: Propagation-Range Index = fraction of the total representational perturbation deposited beyond a local window (e.g., >8 residues in sequence AND >10 Angstrom in 3D). A local-context model deposits almost everything locally; a mechanism-representing model propagates to distant coupled sites.", "3. Distance-decay reproduction (primary, decisive test): For mutations at allosteric sites, measure the internally-propagated perturbation arriving at the active-site residues as a function of the mutation's distance from the active site. Test whether this internal signal reproduces the CONSERVED distance-dependent decay of true allosteric mutational effects reported experimentally (fit the same decay form; compare decay constants). Metric: correlation and decay-constant agreement between internal propagated signal and experimental allosteric effect vs. distance. Controls: (a) mutations at active-site/local residues should show a different (steep, short-range) profile; (b) shuffled distance labels should destroy the fit. A number-fitting/local model will not reproduce the conserved long-range decay.", '4. Coupling-specificity counterfactual: For each true allosteric-partner pair (allosteric residue A coupled to active site or effector site F), compare the internal coupling A->F against distance-matched control residues that are equally far in sequence/3D but are NOT allosterically coupled (per experimental data). Metric: Coupling-Specificity Ratio = internal coupling(true allosteric partner) / internal coupling(distance-matched non-coupled control). Mechanistic representation => ratio >> 1; a representation driven only by spatial/sequence distance => ratio ~ 1.', '5. Prediction-level allosteric-effect decoding beyond locality: Train a probe to predict experimental per-mutation allosteric effect from the internal propagated signal at the active site, and test whether it exceeds a strong baseline using only local features (mutation identity, local secondary structure, distance-to-active-site scalar). Metric: Allostery-Beyond-Locality Index = probe R^2(propagated internal signal) minus R^2(local + distance baseline). Positive index indicates encoded long-range coupling beyond trivial distance.', "6. Causal steering of the coupling: Recover an 'allosteric-coupling' direction (via a supervised probe or SAE feature aligned with the A->F propagation). Steer this direction at the allosteric residue with matched magnitude and measure whether ESM-2's masked-prediction / variant-effect readout at the distant active-site partner shifts in the coupling-consistent direction, while a distance-matched non-coupled control residue does not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect at true coupled partner| / |effect at matched control|, with random-direction controls.", '7. Layer/scale and cross-model analysis: Report where allosteric propagation and distance-decay structure emerge across layers and how they change from ESM-2 150M to 650M to 3B, and test whether AF2 single-sequence Evoformer representations show stronger propagation than ESM-2 (motivated by the 2026 information-bonus finding that AF2 uniquely captures above-chance allostery). CKA aligns the recovered coupling subspaces across models.', '8. Brackets: Compare against (upper bound) a coevolution/MD-based allosteric-pathway predictor (KeyAlloSite / AlloPool) on the same proteins, and (lower bound) a purely local sequence-window baseline, placing PLMs on the local-to-long-range-mechanism spectrum.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Allosteric coupling correlates with 3D and sequence distance and with coevolution, so the coupling-specificity separation is statistically demanding; we mitigate with distance-matched non-coupled controls, the conserved-distance-decay test (which a distance-only model cannot reproduce in the correct functional form), and the allostery-beyond-locality baseline that regresses out distance.', 'The 2026 information-bonus result suggests ESM-2 may genuinely be weak at allostery; a null internal signal could reflect true model blindness rather than probing failure. We treat this as a scientifically decisive outcome (sequence-only PLMs do not represent long-range allosteric mechanism) and include AF2 representations as a positive-control comparison.', 'Ground-truth allosteric coupling and experimental per-mutation allosteric effects are limited and noisy; we restrict to well-characterized proteins with deep-mutational-scanning allostery data and report sensitivity to annotation confidence.', 'In-silico mutation perturbations in a nonlinear transformer may push representations off-manifold; we bound perturbation magnitude, use masked-position and single-substitution edits, and report sensitivity, relying on the input-space mutation propagation as primary evidence and steering as corroboration.', 'Distributed/superposed encodings may mean no single clean coupling direction exists; we use both probe directions and SAE features and report aggregate-direction results.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models (ESM-2, optionally AF2 single-sequence) plus small probes/SAEs and public allostery datasets and structures, keeping compute at academic scale.']}

