the spin gap beyond what geometry predicts. Most decisively, a spin-flip counterfactual holds geometry fixed and flips only the spin label: a mechanistic representation must shift its energy readout and internal state by the correct spin gap, while a geometry-only model cannot. We validate causally by steering the recovered spin direction. Metrics quantify a spin-blindness index, a spin-gap-beyond-geometry index, and a spin-flip causal asymmetry. Either outcome sharpens whether atomistic foundation models can be trusted on the open-shell, spin-dependent chemistry that dominates catalysis and radical reactions.", 'Experiments': ['1. Models and data: Assemble (i) geometry-only universal MLIPs that ingest only nuclei/charge (MACE-MP-0, CHGNet, SevenNet, Orb) and (ii) spin-aware atomistic/quantum-chemistry models that ingest spin multiplicity or are trained across spin states (e.g., SpookyNet, spin-conditioned MACE/AIMNet2 variants, and any charge-/spin-aware foundation MLIP with public weights). Curate spin-state pairs with reliable reference energies: singlet/triplet methylene and substituted carbenes, singlet/triplet O2, and octahedral transition-metal complexes with high-/low-spin states (Fe(II), Co(III), Mn) from published CASPT2/DMRG/DFT spin-gap benchmarks (e.g., TMC/SSE datasets). All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.', '2. Spin-blindness demonstration (geometry-only models): For MLIPs that take only geometry+charge, confirm analytically and empirically that distinct spin isomers of identical geometry receive identical energies and identical internal representations. Metric: Spin-Blindness Index = fraction of spin pairs for which |energy(spin A) - energy(spin B)| = 0 and representation distance = 0. This is a decisive structural negative result quantifying that a major class of deployed MLIPs cannot represent spin-state energetics at all — directly relevant to their (mis)use on open-shell systems.', '3. Baseline decodability (spin-aware models): For spin-aware models, train linear probes on internal representations to decode spin multiplicity and total energy, confirming both are encoded and selecting best layers.', "4. Spin-gap-beyond-geometry probe: Test whether the model's internal representation encodes the spin-state energy ordering BEYOND what nuclear geometry predicts. Regress out the geometry-predictable component (using geometry-only features / a geometry-only MLIP's energy) and re-decode the reference spin gap from the residual. Primary metric: Spin-Gap-Beyond-Geometry Index = probe R^2 for the spin gap from the spin-aware model's representation minus R^2 from geometry-only features. Also test whether the model reproduces the correct SIGN of the gap (which spin state is ground state) across carbenes, O2, and metal complexes.", "5. Spin-flip counterfactual (primary, decisive test): At FIXED nuclear geometry, flip only the spin multiplicity input and measure the change in the model's energy readout and internal representation. Compare against the reference spin gap. Metric: Spin-Flip Causal Asymmetry = |internal/energy shift under spin-flip| / |shift under a matched control edit (e.g., a small geometry perturbation of comparable energy scale)|, and the correlation between the model's spin-flip energy response and the reference CASPT2/DFT spin gap across the dataset. A mechanistic representation reproduces the correct spin-gap magnitude and sign; a spin-insensitive representation shows ~0 response (geometry-only) or an uncorrelated response.", "6. Exchange dose-response: For systems where the spin gap can be tuned (e.g., ligand-field strength series controlling high-/low-spin splitting, or substituent series tuning carbene singlet-triplet gaps), test whether the model's internal spin-state signal follows the physically-correct trend (e.g., stronger-field ligands stabilizing low-spin). Metric: Spearman correlation between the internal spin-state readout and the reference gap across the series; a mechanistic model reproduces the Hund's-rule / ligand-field monotonic trend.", "7. Causal spin-state steering: Recover a 'spin-state/exchange' direction (via a supervised probe or SAE feature separating spin isomers). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's energy prediction shifts in the spin-gap-consistent direction, while matched-norm random directions do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of spin direction| / |effect of random direction|.", '8. Cross-model, layer, and universality analysis: Report where spin-state structure emerges across depth, whether spin-aware models genuinely internalize exchange (vs merely gating on the spin label), and whether recovered spin-state subspaces align across models (CKA). Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a spin-state classifier / ML-XC functional that captures exchange by construction, and (lower bound) the spin-blind geometry-only MLIPs, placing models on the geometry-only-to-exchange-mechanism spectrum.', '9. Reactivity link: For a set of radical/open-shell reactions where spin state controls the barrier (e.g., two-state reactivity in metal catalysis), test whether the internal spin-state representation predicts the spin-crossover-relevant energetics, connecting the mechanism to downstream reactivity trust.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Many public MLIPs are geometry-only and thus spin-blind by construction; the spin-blindness result is definitional for those models, so the richer mechanism tests apply only to spin-aware models, of which fewer public foundation-scale weights exist. We prioritize the strongest available spin-aware model and treat the geometry-only blindness as a decisive, separately-reported finding.', 'Spin-aware models may simply gate energy on the spin label without internally representing exchange; we mitigate by requiring correct sign AND magnitude AND monotonic ligand-field/substituent trends (dose-response), which a trivial label-lookup would not reproduce across unseen systems, and by cross-system transfer tests.', 'Reference spin gaps (CASPT2/DMRG) are themselves method-dependent and can disagree, especially for transition metals; we use consensus benchmark subsets, report sensitivity to the reference method, and emphasize sign and trend agreement over exact magnitude.', 'Spin and geometry can be correlated in equilibrium structures (different spin states relax to different geometries); the spin-flip counterfactual controls this by holding geometry fixed, but off-equilibrium single-point energies may fall outside training distribution — we bound this and report OOD diagnostics.', 'Linear probes recovering a spin direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random-direction controls, though distributed encodings may blur single-direction interventions.', 'A negative result (spin-aware models gate on the label but do not reproduce exchange trends, and geometry-only models are blind) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting atomistic foundation models on catalysis, radicals, and magnetism, though it may be perceived as confirmatory of skepticism; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of spin-state exchange energetics in atomistic foundation models.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes/SAEs and public spin-state benchmark datasets, keeping compute at academic scale.']}

