Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "sabatier_volcano_probe", "Title": "Too Weak or Too Strong? Testing Whether Catalysis Foundation Models Internally Represent the Sabatier Volcano, Not Just Monotone Binding Energy", "Short Hypothesis": "Catalytic activity follows the Sabatier principle: it is a NON-MONOTONE, relational function of intermediate binding energy \u2014 optimal at an intermediate value, with activity limited by poor reactant activation when binding is too weak and by poor product desorption when binding is too strong. Models trained to predict pointwise adsorption energies (e.g., on OC20) are never required to learn this two-sided, rate-determining-step structure. We hypothesize that if a catalysis foundation model internally represents the mechanism, its representation will (a) encode the volcano's two-sided distance-to-peak, (b) distinguish weak-binding-limited from strong-binding-limited catalysts even when their binding-energy deviation magnitudes are equal, and (c) causally track the switch in rate-determining step across the peak; a pointwise/monotone representation collapses the two limiting regimes. The opposite-side-of-the-peak counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that monotone binding-energy prediction cannot pass.", "Related Work": "Post-hoc XAI on shallow catalysis ML (SHAP/LIME/symbolic regression on adsorption-energy predictors; 'Explainable Data-driven Modeling of Adsorption Energy' 2024; HEA scaling-relation studies 2023; 'Beyond Scaling Relations' 2019; AutoML feature-deletion 2024) recovers descriptors and MONOTONE linear scaling relations, and volcano-plot ML (Meyer et al. 2018; Sharma et al. 2024 'Mapping and Reshaping the Activity Volcano') USES models to construct volcanoes downstream. Interpretability of atomistic foundation models ('Comparing latent features of uMLIPs' 2025; 'Beyond Converging Representations' 2025; CGCNN interpretability 2018) recovers pointwise site energies and characterizes latent geometry. Crucially, all of these either (i) attribute features to a monotone adsorption-energy target, or (ii) apply the model's energy predictions to build a volcano externally. None tests whether the model's INTERNAL representation encodes the Sabatier principle itself \u2014 the non-monotone, two-sided, rate-determining-step structure that defines catalytic activity \u2014 nor uses an opposite-side-of-the-peak counterfactual to separate weak- from strong-binding limitation. Because these models are trained on pointwise adsorption energies, whether they internalize the relational volcano mechanism is untested; that is our novel, mechanism-defining contribution.", "Abstract": "The central organizing principle of heterogeneous catalysis is the Sabatier principle: catalytic activity is a non-monotone function of how strongly a surface binds key intermediates, peaking at an intermediate binding strength. Below the peak, activity is limited by weak reactant activation; above it, by poor product desorption. This yields the iconic volcano plot \u2014 a relational, two-sided mechanism in which the rate-determining step switches across the summit. Catalysis foundation models are trained to predict pointwise adsorption energies (e.g., on the Open Catalyst dataset), a target that never requires learning this two-sided structure, so whether they internalize the Sabatier mechanism is unknown. We probe pretrained catalysis foundation models (e.g., GemNet-OC, EquiformerV2, and universal MLIPs on OC20/OC22 systems) at the representation level. First, a two-sided volcano probe: we test whether the model's internal representation encodes signed distance-to-peak and, critically, on which SIDE of the peak a catalyst lies, beyond what its scalar binding energy alone predicts. Second, and most decisively, an opposite-side counterfactual: we pair a too-weak-binding catalyst with a too-strong-binding one whose binding-energy deviation from the peak has equal magnitude but opposite sign, so their activities are similar but their limiting mechanisms opposite; a Sabatier-aware representation must separate them into weak- versus strong-limited regimes, while a monotone representation collapses them. Third, causal steering across the peak tests whether the internal rate-determining-step signal flips as predicted. Metrics quantify a volcano-side-beyond-energy index, an opposite-side separation ratio, and a rate-determining-step flip asymmetry. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed catalyst-discovery models represent the mechanism they are used to optimize.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load pretrained catalysis foundation models with public weights spanning uses \u2014 an adsorption-energy predictor trained on the Open Catalyst dataset (GemNet-OC and/or EquiformerV2 on OC20-IS2RE/S2EF) and a universal MLIP (MACE-MP-0 / equivalent) applied to catalytic surfaces. Assemble evaluation systems with known volcano structure: well-characterized reactions with established descriptors and experimental/DFT volcano data (e.g., hydrogen evolution (H* binding), oxygen reduction (O*/OH* binding), CO oxidation, and ammonia synthesis (N* binding)) across transition-metal surfaces and alloys. For each catalyst-reaction pair record the key intermediate binding energy, the known volcano peak (optimal binding), the signed distance-to-peak, the limiting-step side (weak vs strong), and a proxy activity (theoretical turnover / limiting potential from microkinetic or thermodynamic-limiting-potential models). Extract per-structure internal representations (pooled activations) across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode the scalar key-intermediate binding energy and the proxy activity from internal representations, confirming both are encoded and selecting best layers (replicating pointwise-energy findings as a control).", "3. Two-sided volcano-side probe (primary probe): Test whether the representation encodes which SIDE of the volcano a catalyst is on, beyond its scalar binding energy. (a) Classify weak-limited vs strong-limited from the representation; (b) decode SIGNED distance-to-peak. Then regress out the binding-energy-predictable component and re-run both. Primary metric: Volcano-Side-Beyond-Energy Index = side-classification accuracy (and signed-distance R^2) from the model representation minus what the scalar binding energy alone achieves. A monotone representation cannot exceed the binding-energy baseline for the SIGN of the deviation once magnitude is controlled; a Sabatier-aware representation can.", "4. Opposite-side counterfactual (primary, decisive test): Construct matched pairs of catalysts \u2014 one binding TOO WEAKLY, one binding TOO STRONGLY \u2014 whose binding-energy deviations from the peak are equal in magnitude but opposite in sign (so proxy activities are similar, limiting mechanisms opposite). Measure the representational distance within these opposite-side pairs versus within same-side control pairs matched on |deviation|. Metric: Opposite-Side Separation Ratio = representational distance(opposite-side pair) / representational distance(same-side, same-|deviation| pair). Mechanistic (Sabatier) representation => ratio >> 1 (opposite limiting regimes are internally distinguished); monotone/pointwise representation => ratio ~ 1 (only magnitude matters, sign collapses). Report across reactions and as a function of |deviation|.", "5. Rate-determining-step signal: Test whether the model encodes the identity of the limiting step (activation-limited vs desorption-limited) as a recoverable direction, and whether this signal correlates with the true limiting step across the volcano rather than with binding-energy magnitude alone. Metric: limiting-step decoding accuracy conditioned on matched |deviation|.", "6. Causal steering across the peak: Recover a signed 'distance-to-peak / rate-determining-step' direction (via a supervised probe or SAE feature separating the two sides). Steer a catalyst's representation with matched magnitude toward the strong-binding or weak-binding side and test whether the model's predicted activity readout moves in the volcano-consistent (non-monotone) direction \u2014 i.e., moving a peak catalyst either way should DECREASE predicted activity, and moving a too-strong catalyst toward the peak should INCREASE it. Controls: matched-norm random directions and a pure binding-energy-magnitude direction should not reproduce the two-sided non-monotone response. Metric: Rate-Determining-Step Flip Asymmetry = fraction of steers producing the volcano-predicted activity change vs. random-direction controls; mechanism-selectivity = |volcano-consistent effect| / |random-direction effect|.", "7. Failure-prediction link: Test whether an internal 'volcano-representation strength' score (side-classification confidence / opposite-side separability) predicts, per catalyst, where the model's activity ranking is wrong \u2014 especially near the peak where small errors flip the predicted best catalyst. Metric: AUROC for flagging near-peak mis-ranking from the internal signal, vs. output-confidence and binding-energy-only baselines.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where two-sided volcano structure emerges across depth and whether recovered volcano-side subspaces align across models (CKA), assessing whether Sabatier structure is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) an explicit volcano/microkinetic activity model that encodes the two-sided mechanism by construction, and (lower bound) a monotone binding-energy regressor with no side information, placing foundation models on the pointwise-to-relational-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Binding energy and volcano side are correlated across a catalyst set (side is the sign of the deviation), so the beyond-energy separation is statistically demanding; we mitigate with the opposite-side counterfactual that matches |deviation| exactly and controls that regress out binding-energy magnitude.", "The 'true' volcano peak and limiting-step side depend on the chosen descriptor and microkinetic assumptions; we restrict to well-established reactions (HER, ORR, CO oxidation, NH3 synthesis) with consensus descriptors and report sensitivity to peak-location and activity-proxy choices.", "Adsorption-energy foundation models may not natively output an 'activity' quantity; we derive proxy activity externally (thermodynamic limiting potential / microkinetic turnover) for ground truth and probe whether the internal representation nonetheless organizes by the two-sided structure. This is a strength (mechanism need not be in the training target) but limits the causal-steering readout to models with an activity-relatable head; we report per-model.", "Steering in nonlinear equivariant networks can push representations off-manifold; we bound perturbation magnitude, report sensitivity, and use matched-norm and binding-energy-direction controls, relying primarily on the input-space opposite-side counterfactual as causal-adjacent evidence.", "Linear probes recovering a volcano-side direction do not guarantee causal use; distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions \u2014 we use both probe directions and SAE features and report aggregate-direction results.", "A negative result (models encode monotone binding energy but collapse the two limiting regimes) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting catalysis foundation models used to build volcano plots and rank candidates, though it may be perceived as confirmatory of skepticism; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether catalysis foundation models represent the Sabatier principle.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes/SAEs and public catalysis datasets (OC20/OC22) and DFT/experimental volcano references, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
