Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "reproduction_number_threshold_probe", "Title": "Above or Below One? Testing Whether Epidemic Time-Series Foundation Models Represent the Reproduction-Number Threshold Mechanism, Not Just Curve Momentum", "Short Hypothesis": "The defining mechanism of epidemic dynamics is the effective reproduction number R_t and its exact threshold at R_t=1: R_t>1 implies growth, R_t<1 implies decline, and the same recent case curve can be heading up or down depending only on R_t (e.g., a peak plateau vs. an inflection). Time-series foundation models (TSFMs) now used for epidemic forecasting and policy counterfactuals are trained to extrapolate curve shape, which never requires learning this threshold. We hypothesize that if a TSFM represents the epidemic mechanism rather than curve momentum, then (a) R_t and its side relative to 1 are decodable from internal representations beyond what the recent curve shape/slope predicts, (b) an R-holding counterfactual (matched recent curve, opposite R_t side) will be internally distinguished and forecast in opposite directions, and (c) steering a recovered 'R-threshold' direction causally flips growth-vs-decline forecasts. The matched-curve/opposite-R counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that curve-fitting cannot pass.", "Related Work": "Recent work shows TSFMs (Chronos, TimesFM, TimeGPT, TabPFN-TS, Lag-Llama) forecast epidemics well and even produce policy counterfactuals ('Foundation time series models for forecasting and policy evaluation', 2025), and epidemiology-aware pretrained models exist (CAPE, 2025, with 'compartmental prototypes'). Physics-informed and hybrid mechanistic-neural models (EINNs 2023; PINN-SIR 2025; hybrid neural-ODE 2026) BUILD R/SIR structure in by construction. TSFM interpretability shows encoded, steerable concepts: time2time (2025) causally implants 'crash/regime' semantics and finds severity encoded as a continuous latent; 'Exploring Representations...' (2024) and 'On the Internal Semantics of TSFMs' (2025) recover linear trend/periodicity/level-shift concepts. Crucially, none tests whether a TSFM internally represents the reproduction-number threshold mechanism (R_t vs. 1) that governs whether an epidemic grows or declines, nor uses a matched-curve/opposite-R counterfactual to separate this mechanism from mere growth momentum. Because R_t and recent curve slope are correlated in ordinary data, correlation-based forecasting benchmarks cannot arbitrate this; our threshold probe, R-holding counterfactual, and causal R-steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, transferred into a safety-critical public-health setting.", "Abstract": "Time-series foundation models (TSFMs) are increasingly used to forecast infectious-disease epidemics and even to evaluate public-health policies through counterfactual scenarios. Yet epidemic dynamics are defined by an exact, threshold-based mechanism: the effective reproduction number R_t, whose value relative to 1 determines whether cases grow or decline. Because TSFMs are trained to extrapolate observed curve shape, they can succeed by learning growth momentum without ever internalizing the R_t=1 threshold. This distinction is decision-critical: the most consequential forecasts are precisely at turning points, where recent curve shape is ambiguous and only R_t settles the direction. We propose the first mechanism-defining test of whether epidemic-forecasting TSFMs represent the reproduction-number mechanism. Using synthetic SIR/SEIR trajectories (where R_t is exactly known and controllable) and real surveillance data with estimated R_t, we probe pretrained TSFMs (Chronos, TimesFM, Lag-Llama, and the epidemiology-aware CAPE). First, a threshold-beyond-momentum probe: can R_t and its side relative to 1 be decoded from internal representations beyond what recent slope/curvature predicts? Second, and most decisively, an R-holding counterfactual: we construct trajectory pairs with near-identical recent curves but opposite R_t sides (one approaching a peak from below, one just past it), so momentum is matched but mechanism is opposite; a mechanistic representation must separate them and forecast opposite directions. Third, causal steering along a recovered R-threshold direction should flip growth-vs-decline forecasts. Metrics quantify a threshold-beyond-momentum index, a matched-curve turning-point asymmetry, and a steering flip rate. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed epidemic TSFMs forecast for the right mechanistic reason.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load pretrained TSFMs with public weights spanning general and epidemic-aware families: Chronos, TimesFM, Lag-Llama (general TSFMs used for epidemics), and CAPE (epidemiology-aware, compartmental-prototype pretrained). Generate synthetic SIR/SEIR/SAIRD trajectories with exactly-known, time-varying R_t (varying transmission and recovery rates), spanning growth (R_t>1), decline (R_t<1), and turning-point regimes. Assemble real surveillance series (influenza-like illness, RSV, COVID-19, dengue) with published R_t estimates (e.g., EpiEstim). Extract per-timestep internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode R_t (continuous) and the binary R_t>1 vs. R_t<1 side from internal representations across layers, confirming what is encoded and selecting best layers. Establish curve-momentum baselines: recent slope, curvature, and a short-window AR fit computed directly from the input series.", "3. Threshold-beyond-momentum probe (primary probe): Test whether R_t (and its side relative to 1) is decodable BEYOND recent curve momentum. Regress out the momentum-predictable component (recent slope/curvature/AR features) and re-decode R_t and its side from the residual representation. Primary metric: Threshold-Beyond-Momentum Index = probe R^2 (and side-classification accuracy) for R_t from the model representation minus what momentum features alone achieve. A curve-fitting model cannot exceed the momentum baseline for the R_t side at turning points; a mechanistic model can.", "4. R-holding counterfactual (primary, decisive test): Construct matched trajectory pairs with near-identical recent observed windows but opposite R_t sides: e.g., a curve approaching its peak from below (R_t just above 1, still rising) vs. a curve just past its peak (R_t just below 1, about to fall) whose recent segments are made near-indistinguishable by construction in the synthetic generator (matched level, slope, curvature over the input window). Measure (a) internal representational distance within opposite-R pairs vs. same-R control pairs matched on recent curve shape, and (b) the direction of the model's forecast for each. Metrics: Matched-Curve Separation Ratio = representational distance(opposite-R pair) / representational distance(same-R control pair); and Turning-Point Direction Accuracy = fraction of opposite-R pairs where the model forecasts growth for the R>1 member and decline for the R<1 member. Mechanistic representation => separation ratio >> 1 and high direction accuracy; momentum representation => ratio ~ 1 and chance-level direction accuracy (it follows the shared recent shape).", "5. Threshold-locality control: A genuine R-threshold mechanism should show the sharpest representational/forecast change as R_t crosses 1, not at arbitrary R_t values. Sweep R_t across a range and test whether the internal 'growth-vs-decline' signal and forecast direction exhibit a step/inflection near R_t=1 rather than a smooth monotone function of R_t. Metric: location and sharpness of the transition in the internal signal vs. the true threshold at 1; a mechanistic model localizes the change at R_t=1.", "6. Causal R-threshold steering: Recover an 'R-threshold / growth-vs-decline' direction (via a supervised probe or a delta-representation between matched R>1 and R<1 states, following delta-steering methods). Add matched-magnitude perturbations along this direction to a trajectory sitting near the threshold and test whether the model's forecast flips from growth to decline (and vice versa). Controls: matched-norm random directions and a pure recent-slope direction should not reliably flip the forecast in the threshold-consistent way. Metrics: Steering Flip Rate = fraction of steers producing the R-consistent forecast flip; mechanism-selectivity = |effect of R direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "7. Policy-counterfactual faithfulness: Prior work uses TSFMs for policy counterfactuals (e.g., intervention reduces R_t). Test whether the model's counterfactual response to an R-reducing intervention is consistent with the epidemic mechanism: reducing R_t below 1 should turn growth into decline. Compare the model's counterfactual trajectory against the true SIR counterfactual (synthetic ground truth). Metric: correlation between model counterfactual turning behavior and the true post-intervention R_t side; tests whether counterfactual claims rest on encoded R rather than curve extrapolation.", "8. Failure-prediction link: Test whether an internal 'R-representation strength' score (threshold-side classification confidence / matched-curve separability) predicts, per forecast, where the model gets the turning-point direction wrong. Metric: AUROC for flagging turning-point direction errors from the internal signal, vs. output-uncertainty and momentum-only baselines. This connects the representational signature to public-health forecast reliability.", "9. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where R-threshold structure emerges across depth and whether recovered R-threshold subspaces align across general TSFMs and the epidemiology-aware CAPE (CKA), assessing whether the epidemic mechanism is a convergent internal property or only present in epidemiology-aware pretraining. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a mechanistic SIR/PINN model that encodes R by construction and (lower bound) a pure momentum extrapolator, placing TSFMs on the curve-fitting-to-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["R_t and recent curve slope are correlated in ordinary trajectories, making the threshold-beyond-momentum separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with momentum-residualization, the R-holding counterfactual (which matches the recent curve exactly in the synthetic generator), and the threshold-locality control that a momentum model cannot reproduce.", "Real-data R_t is an estimate (method- and delay-dependent), adding label noise; we use synthetic data with exactly-known R_t for the primary decisive tests and treat real surveillance as a robustness/transfer check, reporting sensitivity to the R_t estimator.", "Constructing near-identical recent windows with opposite R_t requires careful synthetic generation (matching level, slope, curvature over the input window); residual differences could leak the side. We quantify and report the residual matching error and show results as a function of match tightness.", "TSFMs normalize/tokenize input series (scaling, patching), which may discard absolute-level information relevant to R; we document each model's preprocessing, probe pre-normalization activations where feasible, and prioritize models exposing per-timestep representations.", "Linear probes recovering an R direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/slope-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions.", "A negative result (models forecast from curve momentum and fail the matched-curve/opposite-R counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant, warning against trusting TSFM epidemic forecasts and policy counterfactuals precisely at turning points where they matter most; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether epidemic TSFMs represent the reproduction-number threshold.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and standard epidemic simulators / public surveillance data, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'reproduction_number_threshold_probe', 'Title': 'Above or Below One? Testing Whether Epidemic Time-Series Foundation Models Represent the Reproduction-Number Threshold Mechanism, Not Just Curve Momentum', 'Short Hypothesis': "The defining mechanism of epidemic dynamics is the effective reproduction number R_t and its exact threshold at R_t=1: R_t>1 implies growth, R_t<1 implies decline, and the same recent case curve can be heading up or down depending only on R_t (e.g., a peak plateau vs. an inflection). Time-series foundation models (TSFMs) now used for epidemic forecasting and policy counterfactuals are trained to extrapolate curve shape, which never requires learning this threshold. We hypothesize that if a TSFM represents the epidemic mechanism rather than curve momentum, then (a) R_t and its side relative to 1 are decodable from internal representations beyond what the recent curve shape/slope predicts, (b) an R-holding counterfactual (matched recent curve, opposite R_t side) will be internally distinguished and forecast in opposite directions, and (c) steering a recovered 'R-threshold' direction causally flips growth-vs-decline forecasts. The matched-curve/opposite-R counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that curve-fitting cannot pass.", 'Related Work': "Recent work shows TSFMs (Chronos, TimesFM, TimeGPT, TabPFN-TS, Lag-Llama) forecast epidemics well and even produce policy counterfactuals ('Foundation time series models for forecasting and policy evaluation', 2025), and epidemiology-aware pretrained models exist (CAPE, 2025, with 'compartmental prototypes'). Physics-informed and hybrid mechanistic-neural models (EINNs 2023; PINN-SIR 2025; hybrid neural-ODE 2026) BUILD R/SIR structure in by construction. TSFM interpretability shows encoded, steerable concepts: time2time (2025) causally implants 'crash/regime' semantics and finds severity encoded as a continuous latent; 'Exploring Representations...' (2024) and 'On the Internal Semantics of TSFMs' (2025) recover linear trend/periodicity/level-shift concepts. Crucially, none tests whether a TSFM internally represents the reproduction-number threshold mechanism (R_t vs. 1) that governs whether an epidemic grows or declines, nor uses a matched-curve/opposite-R counterfactual to separate this mechanism from mere growth momentum. Because R_t and recent curve slope are correlated in ordinary data, correlation-based forecasting benchmarks cannot arbitrate this; our threshold probe, R-holding counterfactual, and causal R-steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, transferred into a safety-critical public-health setting.", 'Abstract': 'Time-series foundation models (TSFMs) are increasingly used to forecast infectious-disease epidemics and even to evaluate public-health policies through counterfactual scenarios. Yet epidemic dynamics are defined by an exact, threshold-based mechanism: the effective reproduction number R_t, whose value relative to 1 determines whether cases grow or decline. Because TSFMs are trained to extrapolate observed curve shape, they can succeed by learning growth momentum without ever internalizing the R_t=1 threshold. This distinction is decision-critical: the most consequential forecasts are precisely at turning points, where recent curve shape is ambiguous and only R_t settles the direction. We propose the first mechanism-defining test of whether epidemic-forecasting TSFMs represent the reproduction-number mechanism. Using synthetic SIR/SEIR trajectories (where R_t is exactly known and controllable) and real surveillance data with estimated R_t, we probe pretrained TSFMs (Chronos, TimesFM, Lag-Llama, and the epidemiology-aware CAPE). First, a threshold-beyond-momentum probe: can R_t and its side relative to 1 be decoded from internal representations beyond what recent slope/curvature predicts? Second, and most decisively, an R-holding counterfactual: we construct trajectory pairs with near-identical recent curves but opposite R_t sides (one approaching a peak from below, one just past it), so momentum is matched but mechanism is opposite; a mechanistic representation must separate them and forecast opposite directions. Third, causal steering along a recovered R-threshold direction should flip growth-vs-decline forecasts. Metrics quantify a threshold-beyond-momentum index, a matched-curve turning-point asymmetry, and a steering flip rate. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed epidemic TSFMs forecast for the right mechanistic reason.', 'Experiments': ['1. Models and data: Load pretrained TSFMs with public weights spanning general and epidemic-aware families: Chronos, TimesFM, Lag-Llama (general TSFMs used for epidemics), and CAPE (epidemiology-aware, compartmental-prototype pretrained). Generate synthetic SIR/SEIR/SAIRD trajectories with exactly-known, time-varying R_t (varying transmission and recovery rates), spanning growth (R_t>1), decline (R_t<1), and turning-point regimes. Assemble real surveillance series (influenza-like illness, RSV, COVID-19, dengue) with published R_t estimates (e.g., EpiEstim). Extract per-timestep internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.', '2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode R_t (continuous) and the binary R_t>1 vs. R_t<1 side from internal representations across layers, confirming what is encoded and selecting best layers. Establish curve-momentum baselines: recent slope, curvature, and a short-window AR fit computed directly from the input series.', '3. Threshold-beyond-momentum probe (primary probe): Test whether R_t (and its side relative to 1) is decodable BEYOND recent curve momentum. Regress out the momentum-predictable component (recent slope/curvature/AR features) and re-decode R_t and its side from the residual representation. Primary metric: Threshold-Beyond-Momentum Index = probe R^2 (and side-classification accuracy) for R_t from the model representation minus what momentum features alone achieve. A curve-fitting model cannot exceed the momentum baseline for the R_t side at turning points; a mechanistic model can.', "4. R-holding counterfactual (primary, decisive test): Construct matched trajectory pairs with near-identical recent observed windows but opposite R_t sides: e.g., a curve approaching its peak from below (R_t just above 1, still rising) vs. a curve just past its peak (R_t just below 1, about to fall) whose recent segments are made near-indistinguishable by construction in the synthetic generator (matched level, slope, curvature over the input window). Measure (a) internal representational distance within opposite-R pairs vs. same-R control pairs matched on recent curve shape, and (b) the direction of the model's forecast for each. Metrics: Matched-Curve Separation Ratio = representational distance(opposite-R pair) / representational distance(same-R control pair); and Turning-Point Direction Accuracy = fraction of opposite-R pairs where the model forecasts growth for the R>1 member and decline for the R<1 member. Mechanistic representation => separation ratio >> 1 and high direction accuracy; momentum representation => ratio ~ 1 and chance-level direction accuracy (it follows the shared recent shape).", "5. Threshold-locality control: A genuine R-threshold mechanism should show the sharpest representational/forecast change as R_t crosses 1, not at arbitrary R_t values. Sweep R_t across a range and test whether the internal 'growth-vs-decline' signal and forecast direction exhibit a step/inflection near R_t=1 rather than a smooth monotone function of R_t. Metric: location and sharpness of the transition in the internal signal vs. the true threshold at 1; a mechanistic model localizes the change at R_t=1.", "6. Causal R-threshold steering: Recover an 'R-threshold / growth-vs-decline' direction (via a supervised probe or a delta-representation between matched R>1 and R<1 states, following delta-steering methods). Add matched-magnitude perturbations along this direction to a trajectory sitting near the threshold and test whether the model's forecast flips from growth to decline (and vice versa). Controls: matched-norm random directions and a pure recent-slope direction should not reliably flip the forecast in the threshold-consistent way. Metrics: Steering Flip Rate = fraction of steers producing the R-consistent forecast flip; mechanism-selectivity = |effect of R direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "7. Policy-counterfactual faithfulness: Prior work uses TSFMs for policy counterfactuals (e.g., intervention reduces R_t). Test whether the model's counterfactual response to an R-reducing intervention is consistent with the epidemic mechanism: reducing R_t below 1 should turn growth into decline. Compare the model's counterfactual trajectory against the true SIR counterfactual (synthetic ground truth). Metric: correlation between model counterfactual turning behavior and the true post-intervention R_t side; tests whether counterfactual claims rest on encoded R rather than curve extrapolation.", "8. Failure-prediction link: Test whether an internal 'R-representation strength' score (threshold-side classification confidence / matched-curve separability) predicts, per forecast, where the model gets the turning-point direction wrong. Metric: AUROC for flagging turning-point direction errors from the internal signal, vs. output-uncertainty and momentum-only baselines. This connects the representational signature to public-health forecast reliability.", '9. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where R-threshold structure emerges across depth and whether recovered R-threshold subspaces align across general TSFMs and the epidemiology-aware CAPE (CKA), assessing whether the epidemic mechanism is a convergent internal property or only present in epidemiology-aware pretraining. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a mechanistic SIR/PINN model that encodes R by construction and (lower bound) a pure momentum extrapolator, placing TSFMs on the curve-fitting-to-mechanism spectrum.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['R_t and recent curve slope are correlated in ordinary trajectories, making the threshold-beyond-momentum separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with momentum-residualization, the R-holding counterfactual (which matches the recent curve exactly in the synthetic generator), and the threshold-locality control that a momentum model cannot reproduce.', 'Real-data R_t is an estimate (method- and delay-dependent), adding label noise; we use synthetic data with exactly-known R_t for the primary decisive tests and treat real surveillance as a robustness/transfer check, reporting sensitivity to the R_t estimator.', 'Constructing near-identical recent windows with opposite R_t requires careful synthetic generation (matching level, slope, curvature over the input window); residual differences could leak the side. We quantify and report the residual matching error and show results as a function of match tightness.', "TSFMs normalize/tokenize input series (scaling, patching), which may discard absolute-level information relevant to R; we document each model's preprocessing, probe pre-normalization activations where feasible, and prioritize models exposing per-timestep representations.", 'Linear probes recovering an R direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/slope-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions.', 'A negative result (models forecast from curve momentum and fail the matched-curve/opposite-R counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant, warning against trusting TSFM epidemic forecasts and policy counterfactuals precisely at turning points where they matter most; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether epidemic TSFMs represent the reproduction-number threshold.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and standard epidemic simulators / public surveillance data, keeping compute at academic scale.']}

