Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "universality_class_collapse_probe", "Title": "Same Exponents, Different Stuff: Do Physics Foundation Models Represent Universality Classes, or Just Microscopic Detail?", "Short Hypothesis": "Universality is the defining, exactly-known mechanism of critical phenomena: near a continuous phase transition, microscopically-different systems collapse onto the SAME critical exponents, determined only by symmetry and spatial dimensionality, not microscopic details. We hypothesize that if a physics/scientific foundation model represents this mechanism rather than surface configuration statistics, its internal representation near criticality will (a) organize systems by universality class (collapsing microscopically-distinct systems of the same class together while separating same-looking systems of different classes), (b) encode a recoverable 'reduced temperature / distance-to-criticality' direction that reproduces finite-size scaling collapse, and (c) causally track class membership. A same-class-different-microscopics vs different-class-similar-microscopics counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that correlation with raw configurations cannot pass.", "Related Work": "A large literature draws an ANALOGY between deep networks and the renormalization group (Scale-invariant feature extraction and RG flow 2018; Towards quantifying information flows 2022; Interpreting Deep Learning via RG 2022; RG for DNNs: Universality of learning 2025), and studies criticality signatures IN networks (Universal Scaling Laws of Absorbing Phase Transitions in DNNs 2023; Critical Phase Transition in LLMs 2024; Internal Representations in SNNs and criticality 2024). These treat the NETWORK itself as a critical/RG system, or map data-derived RG flows. None tests whether a model TRAINED ON PHYSICAL SYSTEM DATA internally represents the physics concept of universality classes \u2014 that critical exponents depend only on symmetry and dimensionality, not microscopic details \u2014 nor uses a same-class/different-class counterfactual to separate this mechanism from raw-configuration correlation. Statistical-physics work (Potts/percolation hyperuniversality 2025; lower-bound-on-nu conjecture 2025; quantum critical exponent extraction 2026) rigorously defines universality classes and exponents as ground truth. Our contribution is the first mechanism-defining test of whether a scientific foundation model encodes universality-class structure, its causal load-bearingness, and its finite-size-scaling faithfulness \u2014 distinct from the network-as-RG analogy line.", "Abstract": "The single deepest organizing principle of critical phenomena is universality: near a continuous phase transition, systems that differ completely at the microscopic level \u2014 different lattices, interactions, and degrees of freedom \u2014 exhibit identical critical exponents, determined only by symmetry and spatial dimensionality. Two systems that look nothing alike can belong to the same universality class, while two that look almost identical can belong to different ones. This makes universality an ideal, exactly-known mechanism for testing whether foundation models that ingest physical system data learn the underlying mechanism or merely surface configuration statistics. We probe scientific foundation models applied to spin/lattice systems (either public multi-physics/statistical-mechanics surrogates, or a modest transformer/CNN foundation model pretrained across many statistical-mechanics systems), evaluated on Monte-Carlo configurations from the 2D Ising, 3-state Potts, XY, and site/bond percolation models whose universality classes and critical exponents are exactly known. First, a class-organization probe: near criticality, are microscopically-distinct systems of the same class collapsed together in the representation, and same-looking systems of different classes separated, beyond raw-configuration similarity? Second, a finite-size-scaling test: does a recovered 'reduced-temperature' direction reproduce the data-collapse scaling function with the correct critical exponents? Third, and most decisively, a universality-class counterfactual: pairs matched to be same-class-but-different-microscopics versus different-class-but-similar-microscopics; a mechanistic representation groups by class, a configuration-statistics one groups by appearance. We validate causally by steering the class direction. Metrics quantify a class-beyond-configuration index, scaling-collapse fidelity, and a same-class/different-class separation ratio. Either outcome sharpens what these models represent.", "Experiments": ["1. Systems and data: Generate Monte-Carlo configuration ensembles (Metropolis/Wolff/Swendsen-Wang) for statistical-mechanics models with EXACTLY-KNOWN universality classes and critical exponents: 2D Ising (class: 2D Ising), 2D q=3 Potts (3-state Potts class), 2D XY (BKT), 2D site and bond percolation (percolation class), and dilute/next-nearest-neighbor Ising variants (same 2D-Ising class but different microscopics). Sweep temperature/occupation across each transition (below, at, above criticality) at several lattice sizes L for finite-size scaling. Record ground-truth class label, reduced temperature t=(T-Tc)/Tc, and known exponents (nu, beta, gamma).", "2. Model: Use either (a) a public scientific/statistical-mechanics foundation model with usable weights and per-configuration representations, or (b) a modest CNN/transformer foundation model pretrained self-supervised (masked reconstruction / temperature regression) across MULTIPLE of these systems simultaneously \u2014 crucially never told universality-class labels. Extract per-configuration internal representations across layers. All primary analysis is inference-only plus small probes.", "3. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode temperature/reduced-temperature and a phase label (ordered/disordered) per system, confirming the representation encodes basic thermodynamic state and selecting best layers. Establish raw-configuration similarity baselines (pixel/spin overlap, magnetization, energy, two-point correlation length).", "4. Class-organization / class-beyond-configuration probe (primary probe): Near criticality, test whether universality-CLASS is decodable/clusterable from the representation BEYOND raw-configuration similarity. Classify class from the representation and cluster configurations; then regress out the raw-configuration-predictable component and re-test. Primary metric: Class-Beyond-Configuration Index = class-decoding accuracy (and clustering purity) from the model representation minus what raw-configuration/order-parameter features alone achieve. A configuration-statistics model cannot exceed the appearance baseline; a universality-aware model can.", "5. Universality-class counterfactual (primary, decisive test): Construct matched pairs. (a) Same-class/different-microscopics: e.g. nearest-neighbor 2D Ising vs dilute or next-nearest-neighbor 2D Ising at their respective critical points \u2014 same class, visibly different configurations. (b) Different-class/similar-microscopics: e.g. Ising (q=2 Potts) vs q=3 Potts at criticality, or near-critical vs off-critical configurations that look similar \u2014 different class (or different scaling), similar appearance. Measure representational distance within (a) vs within (b). Metric: Same-Class/Different-Class Separation Ratio = representational distance(same-class pair) / representational distance(different-class-but-similar-appearance pair). Mechanistic (universality) representation => ratio << 1 (same class collapses together despite different microscopics); configuration-statistics representation => ratio >= 1 (groups by appearance, not class).", "6. Finite-size-scaling faithfulness: Recover a 'reduced-temperature / distance-to-criticality' direction from the representation across L and t. Test whether the model-implied observables obey the finite-size-scaling collapse M*L^{beta/nu} = f(t*L^{1/nu}) with the CORRECT known exponents \u2014 i.e., does the internal signal produce a clean data collapse using the true class exponents, and fail to collapse when the wrong-class exponents are imposed? Metric: scaling-collapse quality (residual of the collapse) with correct vs wrong-class exponents; a mechanistic representation collapses only under its true class's exponents.", "7. Causal class steering: Recover a class-membership direction (supervised probe or delta-representation between two classes at criticality) and steer a configuration's representation with matched magnitude toward another class; test whether the model's class/exponent readout (or masked-reconstruction statistics) shifts class-consistently, while matched-norm random and raw-configuration-direction perturbations do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of class direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "8. Layer/emergence and universality: Report where class structure emerges across depth (hypothesizing an intermediate 'coarse-graining' zone), whether it strengthens with lattice size (correlation length), and whether recovered class subspaces align across models (CKA). Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a supervised class classifier and (lower bound) a raw-configuration/order-parameter classifier, placing the foundation model on the appearance-to-universality spectrum.", "9. Cross-dimension / held-out-system generalization: Test whether class structure transfers to a system held out of pretraining but in a known class (e.g., a 2D Ising variant, or 3D Ising if feasible), assessing whether the model encodes universality as a general mechanism or memorizes per-system statistics."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Universality-class membership correlates with visible configuration features near criticality (e.g., cluster geometry), making the class-beyond-configuration separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with raw-configuration residualization, the matched same-class/different-microscopics counterfactual, and the finite-size-scaling test which a configuration-statistics model cannot pass with correct exponents.", "Public scientific foundation models trained on statistical-mechanics configurations may be scarce; we fall back on pretraining a modest multi-system CNN/transformer surrogate ourselves, which is academic-scale (small lattices, standard Monte-Carlo data) but is not a 'large' foundation model \u2014 we report this scope clearly and treat cross-system generalization as the foundation-model-relevant test.", "Distinguishing BKT (XY) scaling from second-order transitions requires care (essential singularity, no simple power-law nu); we treat XY as a separate, carefully-analyzed case and focus decisive counterfactuals on clean second-order classes (Ising, Potts, percolation).", "Finite-size effects and limited lattice sizes blur exponents; we use standard finite-size scaling with multiple L and report exponent-estimation uncertainty, relying on relative collapse quality (correct vs wrong-class exponents) rather than absolute precision.", "Linear probes recovering a class direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/configuration-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions.", "A negative result (models encode configuration statistics and group by appearance, not universality class) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting such models for cross-system extrapolation and critical-point prediction, though it may be perceived as confirmatory of skepticism; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether scientific foundation models represent universality classes.", "All experiments are inference-only on the model plus small probes and standard Monte-Carlo simulations (or one modest self-supervised pretraining run), keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'universality_class_collapse_probe', 'Title': 'Same Exponents, Different Stuff: Do Physics Foundation Models Represent Universality Classes, or Just Microscopic Detail?', 'Short Hypothesis': "Universality is the defining, exactly-known mechanism of critical phenomena: near a continuous phase transition, microscopically-different systems collapse onto the SAME critical exponents, determined only by symmetry and spatial dimensionality, not microscopic details. We hypothesize that if a physics/scientific foundation model represents this mechanism rather than surface configuration statistics, its internal representation near criticality will (a) organize systems by universality class (collapsing microscopically-distinct systems of the same class together while separating same-looking systems of different classes), (b) encode a recoverable 'reduced temperature / distance-to-criticality' direction that reproduces finite-size scaling collapse, and (c) causally track class membership. A same-class-different-microscopics vs different-class-similar-microscopics counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that correlation with raw configurations cannot pass.", 'Related Work': 'A large literature draws an ANALOGY between deep networks and the renormalization group (Scale-invariant feature extraction and RG flow 2018; Towards quantifying information flows 2022; Interpreting Deep Learning via RG 2022; RG for DNNs: Universality of learning 2025), and studies criticality signatures IN networks (Universal Scaling Laws of Absorbing Phase Transitions in DNNs 2023; Critical Phase Transition in LLMs 2024; Internal Representations in SNNs and criticality 2024). These treat the NETWORK itself as a critical/RG system, or map data-derived RG flows. None tests whether a model TRAINED ON PHYSICAL SYSTEM DATA internally represents the physics concept of universality classes — that critical exponents depend only on symmetry and dimensionality, not microscopic details — nor uses a same-class/different-class counterfactual to separate this mechanism from raw-configuration correlation. Statistical-physics work (Potts/percolation hyperuniversality 2025; lower-bound-on-nu conjecture 2025; quantum critical exponent extraction 2026) rigorously defines universality classes and exponents as ground truth. Our contribution is the first mechanism-defining test of whether a scientific foundation model encodes universality-class structure, its causal load-bearingness, and its finite-size-scaling faithfulness — distinct from the network-as-RG analogy line.', 'Abstract': "The single deepest organizing principle of critical phenomena is universality: near a continuous phase transition, systems that differ completely at the microscopic level — different lattices, interactions, and degrees of freedom — exhibit identical critical exponents, determined only by symmetry and spatial dimensionality. Two systems that look nothing alike can belong to the same universality class, while two that look almost identical can belong to different ones. This makes universality an ideal, exactly-known mechanism for testing whether foundation models that ingest physical system data learn the underlying mechanism or merely surface configuration statistics. We probe scientific foundation models applied to spin/lattice systems (either public multi-physics/statistical-mechanics surrogates, or a modest transformer/CNN foundation model pretrained across many statistical-mechanics systems), evaluated on Monte-Carlo configurations from the 2D Ising, 3-state Potts, XY, and site/bond percolation models whose universality classes and critical exponents are exactly known. First, a class-organization probe: near criticality, are microscopically-distinct systems of the same class collapsed together in the representation, and same-looking systems of different classes separated, beyond raw-configuration similarity? Second, a finite-size-scaling test: does a recovered 'reduced-temperature' direction reproduce the data-collapse scaling function with the correct critical exponents? Third, and most decisively, a universality-class counterfactual: pairs matched to be same-class-but-different-microscopics versus different-class-but-similar-microscopics; a mechanistic representation groups by class, a configuration-statistics one groups by appearance. We validate causally by steering the class direction. Metrics quantify a class-beyond-configuration index, scaling-collapse fidelity, and a same-class/different-class separation ratio. Either outcome sharpens what these models represent.", 'Experiments': ['1. Systems and data: Generate Monte-Carlo configuration ensembles (Metropolis/Wolff/Swendsen-Wang) for statistical-mechanics models with EXACTLY-KNOWN universality classes and critical exponents: 2D Ising (class: 2D Ising), 2D q=3 Potts (3-state Potts class), 2D XY (BKT), 2D site and bond percolation (percolation class), and dilute/next-nearest-neighbor Ising variants (same 2D-Ising class but different microscopics). Sweep temperature/occupation across each transition (below, at, above criticality) at several lattice sizes L for finite-size scaling. Record ground-truth class label, reduced temperature t=(T-Tc)/Tc, and known exponents (nu, beta, gamma).', '2. Model: Use either (a) a public scientific/statistical-mechanics foundation model with usable weights and per-configuration representations, or (b) a modest CNN/transformer foundation model pretrained self-supervised (masked reconstruction / temperature regression) across MULTIPLE of these systems simultaneously — crucially never told universality-class labels. Extract per-configuration internal representations across layers. All primary analysis is inference-only plus small probes.', '3. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode temperature/reduced-temperature and a phase label (ordered/disordered) per system, confirming the representation encodes basic thermodynamic state and selecting best layers. Establish raw-configuration similarity baselines (pixel/spin overlap, magnetization, energy, two-point correlation length).', '4. Class-organization / class-beyond-configuration probe (primary probe): Near criticality, test whether universality-CLASS is decodable/clusterable from the representation BEYOND raw-configuration similarity. Classify class from the representation and cluster configurations; then regress out the raw-configuration-predictable component and re-test. Primary metric: Class-Beyond-Configuration Index = class-decoding accuracy (and clustering purity) from the model representation minus what raw-configuration/order-parameter features alone achieve. A configuration-statistics model cannot exceed the appearance baseline; a universality-aware model can.', '5. Universality-class counterfactual (primary, decisive test): Construct matched pairs. (a) Same-class/different-microscopics: e.g. nearest-neighbor 2D Ising vs dilute or next-nearest-neighbor 2D Ising at their respective critical points — same class, visibly different configurations. (b) Different-class/similar-microscopics: e.g. Ising (q=2 Potts) vs q=3 Potts at criticality, or near-critical vs off-critical configurations that look similar — different class (or different scaling), similar appearance. Measure representational distance within (a) vs within (b). Metric: Same-Class/Different-Class Separation Ratio = representational distance(same-class pair) / representational distance(different-class-but-similar-appearance pair). Mechanistic (universality) representation => ratio << 1 (same class collapses together despite different microscopics); configuration-statistics representation => ratio >= 1 (groups by appearance, not class).', "6. Finite-size-scaling faithfulness: Recover a 'reduced-temperature / distance-to-criticality' direction from the representation across L and t. Test whether the model-implied observables obey the finite-size-scaling collapse M*L^{beta/nu} = f(t*L^{1/nu}) with the CORRECT known exponents — i.e., does the internal signal produce a clean data collapse using the true class exponents, and fail to collapse when the wrong-class exponents are imposed? Metric: scaling-collapse quality (residual of the collapse) with correct vs wrong-class exponents; a mechanistic representation collapses only under its true class's exponents.", "7. Causal class steering: Recover a class-membership direction (supervised probe or delta-representation between two classes at criticality) and steer a configuration's representation with matched magnitude toward another class; test whether the model's class/exponent readout (or masked-reconstruction statistics) shifts class-consistently, while matched-norm random and raw-configuration-direction perturbations do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of class direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "8. Layer/emergence and universality: Report where class structure emerges across depth (hypothesizing an intermediate 'coarse-graining' zone), whether it strengthens with lattice size (correlation length), and whether recovered class subspaces align across models (CKA). Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a supervised class classifier and (lower bound) a raw-configuration/order-parameter classifier, placing the foundation model on the appearance-to-universality spectrum.", '9. Cross-dimension / held-out-system generalization: Test whether class structure transfers to a system held out of pretraining but in a known class (e.g., a 2D Ising variant, or 3D Ising if feasible), assessing whether the model encodes universality as a general mechanism or memorizes per-system statistics.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Universality-class membership correlates with visible configuration features near criticality (e.g., cluster geometry), making the class-beyond-configuration separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with raw-configuration residualization, the matched same-class/different-microscopics counterfactual, and the finite-size-scaling test which a configuration-statistics model cannot pass with correct exponents.', "Public scientific foundation models trained on statistical-mechanics configurations may be scarce; we fall back on pretraining a modest multi-system CNN/transformer surrogate ourselves, which is academic-scale (small lattices, standard Monte-Carlo data) but is not a 'large' foundation model — we report this scope clearly and treat cross-system generalization as the foundation-model-relevant test.", 'Distinguishing BKT (XY) scaling from second-order transitions requires care (essential singularity, no simple power-law nu); we treat XY as a separate, carefully-analyzed case and focus decisive counterfactuals on clean second-order classes (Ising, Potts, percolation).', 'Finite-size effects and limited lattice sizes blur exponents; we use standard finite-size scaling with multiple L and report exponent-estimation uncertainty, relying on relative collapse quality (correct vs wrong-class exponents) rather than absolute precision.', 'Linear probes recovering a class direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/configuration-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions.', 'A negative result (models encode configuration statistics and group by appearance, not universality class) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting such models for cross-system extrapolation and critical-point prediction, though it may be perceived as confirmatory of skepticism; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether scientific foundation models represent universality classes.', 'All experiments are inference-only on the model plus small probes and standard Monte-Carlo simulations (or one modest self-supervised pretraining run), keeping compute at academic scale.']}

