Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "ps_traveltime_distance_probe", "Title": "Timing, Not Loudness: Do Seismic Foundation Models Represent the P\u2013S Travel-Time Distance Mechanism, or Just Waveform Amplitude Statistics?", "Short Hypothesis": "Earthquake distance is fixed by an exactly-known relational mechanism: the arrival-time difference between the fast P-wave and the slower S-wave grows linearly with hypocentral distance (delta_t \u2248 distance \u00d7 (1/v_S \u2212 1/v_P)), and magnitude follows a saturating log-amplitude-corrected-for-distance law\u2014not raw amplitude. Seismic foundation models are trained on waveforms where distance and amplitude are correlated (nearer, larger). We hypothesize that if these models represent the seismological mechanism rather than amplitude/morphology statistics, then (a) distance is decodable from internal representations beyond what waveform amplitude predicts, (b) a P\u2013S-gap counterfactual that stretches only the inter-arrival time (at fixed amplitude and morphology) will shift the internal distance estimate as physics dictates, while a whole-trace time-shift control (which leaves the gap unchanged) will not, and (c) steering a recovered 'P\u2013S gap' direction causally shifts distance predictions. The gap-vs-shift counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that amplitude-based correlation cannot pass.", "Related Work": "Interpretability of seismic models is entirely post-hoc XAI attribution confirming LOCAL, descriptive alignment: Grad-CAM/SHAP show PhaseNet attends to P/S arrivals and that vertical vs horizontal components drive P vs S picks (2025); Integrated Gradients traces feature-importance drift in earthquake forecasting (2026) and pre-rupture deformation (2025); RNN hidden-state visualization for volcano-seismic events (2022). Seismic foundation models with public weights now exist (SeisLM 2024, SeisCLIP 2023, Seismic Foundation Model 2023, SeisMoLLM 2025) and decode distance/magnitude as downstream tasks. Crucially, all interpretability work is correlational attribution ('the model looks at the P and S arrivals'); none tests whether the model's INTERNAL representation encodes the RELATIONAL travel-time law\u2014that the P\u2013S time gap, not amplitude, determines distance\u2014nor uses a gap-vs-shift counterfactual to separate this mechanism from amplitude/morphology correlation. Because distance and amplitude are correlated in catalogs, ordinary attribution and benchmark accuracy cannot arbitrate this. Our P\u2013S-gap counterfactual, distance-beyond-amplitude probe, and causal gap-steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, moving beyond 'the model sees the arrivals' to 'the model represents that the arrival SEPARATION sets the distance.'", "Abstract": "Seismic foundation models now estimate earthquake distance and magnitude from raw waveforms, and interpretability studies confirm they attend to P- and S-wave arrivals. But attending to the arrivals is not the same as representing the mechanism that makes them informative: hypocentral distance is set by the travel-time difference between the fast P-wave and slower S-wave, a relation that grows linearly with distance and is independent of how loud the signal is, while magnitude requires correcting amplitude for that distance. Because nearer earthquakes are also larger and louder, distance and amplitude are correlated in training data, so benchmark accuracy and attribution maps cannot tell whether a model uses the P\u2013S timing mechanism or an amplitude shortcut. We propose the first mechanism-defining test. Using pretrained seismic foundation models (SeisLM, SeisCLIP, Seismic Foundation Model) evaluated on public labeled waveform datasets (STEAD, DiTing) with known source distances, we ask three questions. First, a distance-beyond-amplitude probe: is distance decodable from internal representations beyond what waveform amplitude/energy predicts? Second, and most decisively, a P\u2013S-gap counterfactual: we resample the segment between the P and S arrivals to stretch or compress only the inter-arrival time at fixed amplitude and waveform morphology, which by physics must change the inferred distance, and compare against a whole-trace time-shift control that leaves the gap unchanged; a mechanistic representation must move its distance estimate for the gap edit but not the shift, while an amplitude-statistics model does the opposite. Third, causal gap-direction steering. Metrics quantify a distance-beyond-amplitude index, a gap-vs-shift causal asymmetry, and a magnitude-distance-correction fidelity test. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed seismic models locate earthquakes for the right physical reason.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load public seismic foundation models with accessible weights and per-trace representations (SeisLM, SeisCLIP, Seismic Foundation Model; SeisMoLLM if feasible). Assemble labeled three-component waveform datasets with ground-truth source parameters: STEAD and DiTing, each providing P and S arrival picks, hypocentral distance, magnitude, and back-azimuth. Compute, per trace, the ground-truth P\u2013S arrival-time gap delta_t, the true distance, peak amplitude/energy, and magnitude. Extract internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode distance, delta_t (the P\u2013S gap), magnitude, and amplitude/energy from internal representations across layers, confirming what is encoded and selecting best layers. Establish amplitude-based baselines: predict distance directly from peak amplitude/energy and simple waveform-shape features computed from the raw trace.", "3. Distance-beyond-amplitude probe (primary probe): Test whether distance is decodable BEYOND waveform amplitude/energy. Regress out the amplitude/energy-predictable component of the representation and re-decode distance from the residual. Primary metric: Distance-Beyond-Amplitude Index = probe R^2 for distance from the model representation minus R^2 achievable from amplitude/energy features alone. Also test whether the recovered distance signal aligns with the P\u2013S gap delta_t rather than amplitude (correlate the probe's distance direction with a delta_t probe vs an amplitude probe). A mechanistic representation carries distance information tied to the timing gap; an amplitude-shortcut model does not exceed the amplitude baseline.", "4. P\u2013S-gap counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For each trace with clean P and S picks, construct two matched edits. (a) Gap edit: resample (time-stretch or compress) ONLY the waveform segment between the P and S arrivals to change delta_t by a controlled factor, while preserving amplitude scale and the morphology of the P and S wave packets themselves (splice the unaltered pre-P and post-S segments back, with tapered joins); this changes the physically-implied distance. (b) Shift control: translate the entire trace in time by a comparable number of samples, leaving delta_t (and thus distance) unchanged. Feed both to the model and measure the change in its internal distance estimate (probed distance / distance-head output). Metric: Gap-vs-Shift Causal Asymmetry = |change in distance estimate under gap edit| / |change under whole-trace shift control|. Mechanistic representation => asymmetry >> 1 and the gap-edit distance change tracks the physically-predicted linear delta_t\u2013distance relation (correct sign and slope); amplitude/morphology-statistics representation => asymmetry ~ 1 (insensitive to the timing gap).", "5. Physics dose-response: Sweep the gap-stretch factor across a range and test whether the model's induced distance change follows the linear delta_t \u2192 distance law (delta_t = distance \u00d7 (1/v_S \u2212 1/v_P)) with the correct sign and approximately correct slope, versus a flat or amplitude-driven response. Metric: Spearman correlation and slope agreement between induced distance change and the physically-predicted distance change; shuffled-gap-factor labels serve as a control.", "6. Magnitude distance-correction fidelity: Magnitude physics requires correcting amplitude for distance (larger amplitude at greater distance implies a bigger event). Construct a counterfactual that increases the P\u2013S gap (implying greater distance) at fixed amplitude, and test whether the model's magnitude estimate INCREASES accordingly (as a distance-corrected magnitude should), rather than staying fixed (amplitude-only readout). Metric: correlation between induced magnitude change and the distance-correction prediction; a control that changes amplitude at fixed gap should move magnitude in the amplitude direction, giving a double dissociation.", "7. Causal gap-direction steering: Recover a 'P\u2013S gap / distance' direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between short-gap and long-gap traces). Add matched-magnitude perturbations along this direction and test whether the model's distance readout shifts in the gap-consistent direction, while matched-norm random and amplitude-direction perturbations do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of gap direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "8. Failure-prediction link: Test whether an internal 'gap-representation strength' score (gap-vs-shift sensitivity or delta_t-probe confidence) predicts, per trace, where the model's distance/magnitude estimate is wrong\u2014especially for low-amplitude distant events and high-amplitude near events where the amplitude shortcut misleads. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error traces from the internal signal, vs output-confidence and amplitude-only baselines.", "9. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where P\u2013S-gap/distance structure emerges across depth and whether recovered gap subspaces align across the different foundation models (CKA), assessing whether the travel-time mechanism is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a classical delta_t-to-distance estimator that uses the timing law by construction, and (lower bound) an amplitude-only distance regressor, placing foundation models on the amplitude-shortcut-to-timing-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Distance and amplitude are strongly correlated in real catalogs, making the distance-beyond-amplitude separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with amplitude-residualization, the gap-vs-shift counterfactual (which holds amplitude and morphology fixed while varying only the timing gap), and the physics dose-response test that an amplitude model cannot reproduce with the correct slope.", "The gap-edit counterfactual is a synthetic waveform manipulation; time-stretching the inter-arrival segment may introduce spectral artifacts or push traces off the model's data distribution, confounding the gap-edit response with generic OOD failure. We mitigate with small stretch factors, tapered splice joins, spectral-artifact diagnostics, and by comparing against the whole-trace shift control of matched sample-count perturbation; we report sensitivity to stretch magnitude.", "Some foundation models ingest time-frequency spectra (e.g., SeisCLIP) or normalize/tokenize waveforms in ways that may discard absolute timing or amplitude; we document each model's preprocessing, probe pre-normalization activations where feasible, and prioritize models that retain raw temporal structure (SeisLM) for the cleanest timing test.", "Arrival picks (P and S) in STEAD/DiTing are pipeline estimates with error, adding noise to the gap edit and to delta_t labels; we restrict counterfactuals to high-SNR traces with confident picks and report sensitivity to pick-quality thresholds.", "Linear probes recovering a gap/distance direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/amplitude-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions.", "A negative result (models estimate distance from amplitude/morphology statistics and fail the gap-vs-shift counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting seismic foundation models for earthquake location and early warning\u2014especially for atypical events where the amplitude\u2013distance correlation breaks\u2014though it may be perceived as confirmatory of skepticism; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether seismic FMs represent the P\u2013S travel-time distance law.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public labeled waveform datasets (STEAD, DiTing), keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'ps_traveltime_distance_probe', 'Title': 'Timing, Not Loudness: Do Seismic Foundation Models Represent the P–S Travel-Time Distance Mechanism, or Just Waveform Amplitude Statistics?', 'Short Hypothesis': "Earthquake distance is fixed by an exactly-known relational mechanism: the arrival-time difference between the fast P-wave and the slower S-wave grows linearly with hypocentral distance (delta_t ≈ distance × (1/v_S − 1/v_P)), and magnitude follows a saturating log-amplitude-corrected-for-distance law—not raw amplitude. Seismic foundation models are trained on waveforms where distance and amplitude are correlated (nearer, larger). We hypothesize that if these models represent the seismological mechanism rather than amplitude/morphology statistics, then (a) distance is decodable from internal representations beyond what waveform amplitude predicts, (b) a P–S-gap counterfactual that stretches only the inter-arrival time (at fixed amplitude and morphology) will shift the internal distance estimate as physics dictates, while a whole-trace time-shift control (which leaves the gap unchanged) will not, and (c) steering a recovered 'P–S gap' direction causally shifts distance predictions. The gap-vs-shift counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that amplitude-based correlation cannot pass.", 'Related Work': "Interpretability of seismic models is entirely post-hoc XAI attribution confirming LOCAL, descriptive alignment: Grad-CAM/SHAP show PhaseNet attends to P/S arrivals and that vertical vs horizontal components drive P vs S picks (2025); Integrated Gradients traces feature-importance drift in earthquake forecasting (2026) and pre-rupture deformation (2025); RNN hidden-state visualization for volcano-seismic events (2022). Seismic foundation models with public weights now exist (SeisLM 2024, SeisCLIP 2023, Seismic Foundation Model 2023, SeisMoLLM 2025) and decode distance/magnitude as downstream tasks. Crucially, all interpretability work is correlational attribution ('the model looks at the P and S arrivals'); none tests whether the model's INTERNAL representation encodes the RELATIONAL travel-time law—that the P–S time gap, not amplitude, determines distance—nor uses a gap-vs-shift counterfactual to separate this mechanism from amplitude/morphology correlation. Because distance and amplitude are correlated in catalogs, ordinary attribution and benchmark accuracy cannot arbitrate this. Our P–S-gap counterfactual, distance-beyond-amplitude probe, and causal gap-steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, moving beyond 'the model sees the arrivals' to 'the model represents that the arrival SEPARATION sets the distance.'", 'Abstract': 'Seismic foundation models now estimate earthquake distance and magnitude from raw waveforms, and interpretability studies confirm they attend to P- and S-wave arrivals. But attending to the arrivals is not the same as representing the mechanism that makes them informative: hypocentral distance is set by the travel-time difference between the fast P-wave and slower S-wave, a relation that grows linearly with distance and is independent of how loud the signal is, while magnitude requires correcting amplitude for that distance. Because nearer earthquakes are also larger and louder, distance and amplitude are correlated in training data, so benchmark accuracy and attribution maps cannot tell whether a model uses the P–S timing mechanism or an amplitude shortcut. We propose the first mechanism-defining test. Using pretrained seismic foundation models (SeisLM, SeisCLIP, Seismic Foundation Model) evaluated on public labeled waveform datasets (STEAD, DiTing) with known source distances, we ask three questions. First, a distance-beyond-amplitude probe: is distance decodable from internal representations beyond what waveform amplitude/energy predicts? Second, and most decisively, a P–S-gap counterfactual: we resample the segment between the P and S arrivals to stretch or compress only the inter-arrival time at fixed amplitude and waveform morphology, which by physics must change the inferred distance, and compare against a whole-trace time-shift control that leaves the gap unchanged; a mechanistic representation must move its distance estimate for the gap edit but not the shift, while an amplitude-statistics model does the opposite. Third, causal gap-direction steering. Metrics quantify a distance-beyond-amplitude index, a gap-vs-shift causal asymmetry, and a magnitude-distance-correction fidelity test. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed seismic models locate earthquakes for the right physical reason.', 'Experiments': ['1. Models and data: Load public seismic foundation models with accessible weights and per-trace representations (SeisLM, SeisCLIP, Seismic Foundation Model; SeisMoLLM if feasible). Assemble labeled three-component waveform datasets with ground-truth source parameters: STEAD and DiTing, each providing P and S arrival picks, hypocentral distance, magnitude, and back-azimuth. Compute, per trace, the ground-truth P–S arrival-time gap delta_t, the true distance, peak amplitude/energy, and magnitude. Extract internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.', '2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode distance, delta_t (the P–S gap), magnitude, and amplitude/energy from internal representations across layers, confirming what is encoded and selecting best layers. Establish amplitude-based baselines: predict distance directly from peak amplitude/energy and simple waveform-shape features computed from the raw trace.', "3. Distance-beyond-amplitude probe (primary probe): Test whether distance is decodable BEYOND waveform amplitude/energy. Regress out the amplitude/energy-predictable component of the representation and re-decode distance from the residual. Primary metric: Distance-Beyond-Amplitude Index = probe R^2 for distance from the model representation minus R^2 achievable from amplitude/energy features alone. Also test whether the recovered distance signal aligns with the P–S gap delta_t rather than amplitude (correlate the probe's distance direction with a delta_t probe vs an amplitude probe). A mechanistic representation carries distance information tied to the timing gap; an amplitude-shortcut model does not exceed the amplitude baseline.", '4. P–S-gap counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For each trace with clean P and S picks, construct two matched edits. (a) Gap edit: resample (time-stretch or compress) ONLY the waveform segment between the P and S arrivals to change delta_t by a controlled factor, while preserving amplitude scale and the morphology of the P and S wave packets themselves (splice the unaltered pre-P and post-S segments back, with tapered joins); this changes the physically-implied distance. (b) Shift control: translate the entire trace in time by a comparable number of samples, leaving delta_t (and thus distance) unchanged. Feed both to the model and measure the change in its internal distance estimate (probed distance / distance-head output). Metric: Gap-vs-Shift Causal Asymmetry = |change in distance estimate under gap edit| / |change under whole-trace shift control|. Mechanistic representation => asymmetry >> 1 and the gap-edit distance change tracks the physically-predicted linear delta_t–distance relation (correct sign and slope); amplitude/morphology-statistics representation => asymmetry ~ 1 (insensitive to the timing gap).', "5. Physics dose-response: Sweep the gap-stretch factor across a range and test whether the model's induced distance change follows the linear delta_t → distance law (delta_t = distance × (1/v_S − 1/v_P)) with the correct sign and approximately correct slope, versus a flat or amplitude-driven response. Metric: Spearman correlation and slope agreement between induced distance change and the physically-predicted distance change; shuffled-gap-factor labels serve as a control.", "6. Magnitude distance-correction fidelity: Magnitude physics requires correcting amplitude for distance (larger amplitude at greater distance implies a bigger event). Construct a counterfactual that increases the P–S gap (implying greater distance) at fixed amplitude, and test whether the model's magnitude estimate INCREASES accordingly (as a distance-corrected magnitude should), rather than staying fixed (amplitude-only readout). Metric: correlation between induced magnitude change and the distance-correction prediction; a control that changes amplitude at fixed gap should move magnitude in the amplitude direction, giving a double dissociation.", "7. Causal gap-direction steering: Recover a 'P–S gap / distance' direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between short-gap and long-gap traces). Add matched-magnitude perturbations along this direction and test whether the model's distance readout shifts in the gap-consistent direction, while matched-norm random and amplitude-direction perturbations do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of gap direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "8. Failure-prediction link: Test whether an internal 'gap-representation strength' score (gap-vs-shift sensitivity or delta_t-probe confidence) predicts, per trace, where the model's distance/magnitude estimate is wrong—especially for low-amplitude distant events and high-amplitude near events where the amplitude shortcut misleads. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error traces from the internal signal, vs output-confidence and amplitude-only baselines.", '9. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where P–S-gap/distance structure emerges across depth and whether recovered gap subspaces align across the different foundation models (CKA), assessing whether the travel-time mechanism is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a classical delta_t-to-distance estimator that uses the timing law by construction, and (lower bound) an amplitude-only distance regressor, placing foundation models on the amplitude-shortcut-to-timing-mechanism spectrum.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Distance and amplitude are strongly correlated in real catalogs, making the distance-beyond-amplitude separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with amplitude-residualization, the gap-vs-shift counterfactual (which holds amplitude and morphology fixed while varying only the timing gap), and the physics dose-response test that an amplitude model cannot reproduce with the correct slope.', "The gap-edit counterfactual is a synthetic waveform manipulation; time-stretching the inter-arrival segment may introduce spectral artifacts or push traces off the model's data distribution, confounding the gap-edit response with generic OOD failure. We mitigate with small stretch factors, tapered splice joins, spectral-artifact diagnostics, and by comparing against the whole-trace shift control of matched sample-count perturbation; we report sensitivity to stretch magnitude.", "Some foundation models ingest time-frequency spectra (e.g., SeisCLIP) or normalize/tokenize waveforms in ways that may discard absolute timing or amplitude; we document each model's preprocessing, probe pre-normalization activations where feasible, and prioritize models that retain raw temporal structure (SeisLM) for the cleanest timing test.", 'Arrival picks (P and S) in STEAD/DiTing are pipeline estimates with error, adding noise to the gap edit and to delta_t labels; we restrict counterfactuals to high-SNR traces with confident picks and report sensitivity to pick-quality thresholds.', 'Linear probes recovering a gap/distance direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/amplitude-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions.', 'A negative result (models estimate distance from amplitude/morphology statistics and fail the gap-vs-shift counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting seismic foundation models for earthquake location and early warning—especially for atypical events where the amplitude–distance correlation breaks—though it may be perceived as confirmatory of skepticism; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether seismic FMs represent the P–S travel-time distance law.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public labeled waveform datasets (STEAD, DiTing), keeping compute at academic scale.']}

