ation-state-beyond-structure probe: is a molecule's dominant charge state at a given pH decodable from internal representations beyond what its neutral structure predicts? Second, and most decisively, a pH-crossing counterfactual: we vary pH across an ionizable group's pKa while holding the input structure fixed and measure whether the internal charge-state and pH-dependent-property signal shifts as Henderson-Hasselbalch dictates (sigmoidal titration curve centered at pKa), versus an equal-magnitude pH change that does not cross any pKa, which should not. Third, causal steering along a recovered ionization direction and site-localization. Metrics quantify a protonation-beyond-structure index, a cross-vs-no-cross causal asymmetry, and a titration-curve fidelity score. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed chemistry models represent the ionization mechanism that controls drug behavior.", 'Experiments': ['1. Models and data: Load pretrained chemistry FMs spanning representation types - a masked chemistry FM (SMI-TED / FM4M), a SMILES transformer (ChemBERTa-2), and a 3D/graph model (Uni-Mol) - plus, where feasible, a pH-aware protonation model (Uni-pKa / Uni-Mol pKa backbone) as an upper-bracket reference. Assemble molecules with well-characterized single and multiple ionizable groups and reliable experimental data: SAMPL6-8 pKa sets, Novartis/ChEMBL pKa data, and pH-dependent property datasets (logD-vs-pH profiles, pH-dependent solubility). For each molecule record its ionizable-group pKa(s), the dominant protonation microstate at a range of pH values, and per-pH property labels. Provide pH to models either via an explicit pH input token/conditioning (for pH-aware models) or by supplying the pH-appropriate protonation microstate SMILES (protonated/deprotonated form) to structure-only models. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.', "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes on internal representations (per layer) to decode (a) neutral-structure features (ionizable-group identity/count), (b) each ionizable group's pKa, and (c) the dominant charge state at a given pH. Confirm what is encoded, select best layers, and establish neutral-structure baselines (does the model 'see' the ionizable groups at all).", '3. Protonation-state-beyond-structure probe (primary probe): Test whether the dominant charge state at a given pH is decodable BEYOND what the neutral structure predicts. Regress out the neutral-structure-predictable component of the representation and re-decode charge state (and net formal charge) from the residual, across a grid of pH values. Primary metric: Protonation-Beyond-Structure Index = charge-state decoding accuracy from the model representation minus what neutral-structure features alone achieve. A mechanism-representing model carries pH-conditioned charge information; a neutral-structure model does not exceed the structure baseline.', "4. pH-crossing counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For each molecule with a known ionizable pKa, construct two matched pH edits that hold the INPUT structure representation fixed (or hold the microstate fixed and vary only the pH conditioning, for pH-aware models). (a) Cross edit: change pH so it crosses the group's pKa (e.g., from pKa-2 to pKa+2), which by Henderson-Hasselbalch flips the dominant charge state. (b) No-cross control: change pH by the same magnitude in a region far from any pKa (charge state unchanged). Measure the change in the model's internal charge-state / pH-dependent-property signal (probed charge, or logD/solubility head output) between the two edits. Metric: Cross-vs-No-Cross Causal Asymmetry = |signal change under pH-cross edit| / |signal change under matched no-cross control|. Mechanistic representation => asymmetry >> 1 (crossing the pKa flips the internal charge/property signal; equal non-crossing pH change does little); neutral-structure/pH-insensitive representation => asymmetry ~ 1.", "5. Titration-curve fidelity (physics dose-response): Sweep pH finely across an ionizable group's pKa and test whether the model's internal charge-state signal traces the sigmoidal Henderson-Hasselbalch titration curve - centered at the correct pKa with the correct steepness - rather than a flat or arbitrary response. Metric: goodness-of-fit of the internal signal to a Henderson-Hasselbalch sigmoid and error in the recovered inflection point vs the true pKa; shuffled-pKa labels serve as a control. For multiprotic molecules, test whether multiple titration steps appear at the correct successive pKa values.", '6. Site-localization: Test whether the pH-crossing effect localizes to the ionizable atom/group rather than being diffuse, using per-token/per-atom representation changes (for models with per-atom activations) or occlusion of the ionizable group. Metric: fraction of the pH-induced representational change concentrated at the ionizable site vs matched non-ionizable sites. A mechanistic representation localizes the ionization signal to the titrating group.', "7. Causal ionization-site steering: Recover an 'ionization / charge-state' direction (via a supervised probe direction, an SAE feature separating protonated from deprotonated forms, or a delta-representation between the two microstates). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's pH-dependent property readout (logD/solubility) shifts in the Henderson-Hasselbalch-consistent direction, while matched-norm random directions do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of ionization direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "8. Property-consequence test (pH-dependent logD): Since ionization governs logD (charged species partition less into lipid), test whether the model's internal charge-state signal predicts the correct pH-dependent logD profile (logD drops as the molecule becomes charged past its pKa), beyond neutral logP. Metric: correlation between internal-charge-driven logD prediction and experimental logD-vs-pH curves; double-dissociation control where a non-ionizable analog shows a flat logD-vs-pH profile.", "9. Failure-prediction and safety link: Test whether an internal 'ionization-representation strength' score predicts where the model's pH-dependent property estimate is wrong, especially for molecules whose charge state flips between physiologically relevant pH values (e.g., gastric pH ~1.5 vs blood pH ~7.4), where getting charge state wrong misestimates bioavailability. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error molecules from the internal signal vs neutral-structure and output-confidence baselines.", '10. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where protonation-state structure emerges across depth and whether recovered ionization subspaces align across the FMs (CKA), assessing whether the Henderson-Hasselbalch mechanism is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a pH-aware protonation-ensemble model (Uni-pKa) that encodes the mechanism by construction, and (lower bound) a neutral-structure-only regressor with no pH access, placing FMs on the static-structure-to-ionization-mechanism spectrum.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['pKa and neutral structure are entangled (the ionizable group is part of the structure), making the protonation-beyond-structure separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with neutral-structure residualization, the pH-crossing counterfactual (which holds structure fixed and varies only pH), and the titration-curve fidelity test that a neutral-structure model cannot pass with the correct inflection at pKa.', "Most chemistry FMs take no pH input, so 'varying pH' must be operationalized either by supplying the pH-appropriate protonation microstate SMILES (which changes the input, partially confounding the structure-fixed ideal) or via pH conditioning only in pH-aware models. We run both operationalizations, clearly report the caveat, and treat the microstate-swap as changing only the ionization of the target group while matching the rest of the structure; a truly mechanism-aware model should nonetheless respond only when the swap corresponds to a real pKa-crossing.", "Providing protonated microstates as input tests whether the model correctly propagates a charge-state change to properties, which is a slightly different (but still mechanism-relevant) claim than 'the model infers pH-dependence from neutral structure'; we distinguish these two claims explicitly and report both.", 'Experimental pKa and pH-dependent property data are noisy and assay-dependent (SAMPL, ChEMBL, Novartis); we use curated benchmark subsets, focus counterfactuals on molecules with a single dominant ionizable group and confident pKa, and report sensitivity to data confidence thresholds; multiprotic molecules are analyzed separately.', 'Linear probes recovering an ionization direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random-direction controls and site-localization, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and SAE features.', 'A negative result (models encode neutral structure but not the pH-dependent ionization mechanism, failing the cross-vs-no-cross counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant - it would warn against trusting chemistry FMs for pH-dependent solubility, permeability, and bioavailability predictions, especially for ionizable drugs that change charge between gut and blood pH; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether chemistry FMs represent the Henderson-Hasselbalch protonation mechanism.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes/SAEs and public pKa/property datasets (SAMPL6-8, ChEMBL, Novartis), keeping compute at academic scale.']}

