Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "cascade_flux_irreversibility_probe", "Title": "Which Way Does Energy Flow? Testing Whether Turbulence Foundation Models Represent the Directional, Time-Irreversible Energy Cascade, Not Just Instantaneous Field Statistics", "Short Hypothesis": "The defining mechanism of turbulence is the Richardson-Kolmogorov energy cascade: kinetic energy is transferred directionally across scales (net forward, large-to-small) at a rate Pi(k) whose mean is set by the dissipation rate, and this transfer breaks time-reversal symmetry (a time-reversed turbulent flow would carry energy up-scale, which is physically forbidden). A single velocity snapshot is nearly statistically identical under time reversal, so instantaneous field statistics cannot encode cascade direction. We hypothesize that if a turbulence/physics foundation model represents the cascade mechanism rather than instantaneous spatial statistics, then (a) the signed inter-scale energy flux Pi(k) is decodable from internal representations of a short trajectory beyond what single-snapshot statistics predict, (b) feeding the model a time-reversed trajectory (identical instantaneous statistics, inverted cascade direction) flips its internal flux signal and degrades its rollout as physics dictates, while a spatially-scrambled control matched on the spectrum does not, and (c) steering a recovered cascade-flux direction causally shifts small-scale energy generation. The forward-vs-time-reversed counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that field-statistics correlation cannot pass.", "Related Work": "Interpretability of physics/weather foundation models shows single physical QUANTITIES are encoded and steerable: 'Physics Steering' (2025) extracts steerable vorticity/diffusion/speed directions that transfer across systems; 'Uncovering Emergent Physics Representations Learned In-Context by LLMs' (2025) finds energy features whose ablation degrades forecasting; GraphCast SAE work recovers cyclones/atmospheric rivers. All recover a scalar quantity at a point/instant. Separately, a rich turbulence literature (Kolmogorov -5/3 spectrum; the coherent structure of the energy cascade, 2023; information-flux causality of inter-scale transfer, 2024; hidden scale invariance, 2023-2025) establishes the cascade as a directional, time-irreversible, scale-relational mechanism, and the emerging thermodynamics-of-turbulence view frames the cascade explicitly as an arrow of time. Crucially, no interpretability work tests whether a foundation model's INTERNAL representation encodes the directional, time-irreversible inter-scale energy flux Pi(k) that DEFINES turbulence, whether that flux representation is causally load-bearing, or uses a time-reversal counterfactual to separate cascade mechanism from instantaneous field statistics. Prior physics-interpretability answers 'is a quantity encoded'; the cascade asks 'is the directional flow of energy across scales, and its broken time-symmetry, represented'. Our directional-flux probe, forward-vs-time-reversed counterfactual, and causal flux steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, distinct from conservation-law or single-quantity tests.", "Abstract": "Turbulence is defined not by any single field value but by a directional, scale-relational mechanism: the Richardson-Kolmogorov energy cascade, in which kinetic energy is transferred on average from large to small scales at a flux Pi(k), a process that breaks time-reversal symmetry and constitutes a physical arrow of time. Foundation models for physics have been shown to encode individual quantities such as energy and vorticity, but whether they represent the cascade itself remains untested. This is decisive because the cascade cannot be read off a single snapshot: a velocity field and its time-reverse are statistically almost identical, yet their cascade directions are opposite. We probe pretrained multi-physics PDE surrogates (e.g., Poseidon, Multiple-Physics-Pretraining, DPOT) on isotropic-turbulence trajectories (JHTDB DNS), where the signed inter-scale flux Pi(k) is computable exactly. First, a flux-beyond-snapshot probe: is Pi(k) decodable from internal representations of a short trajectory beyond what single-frame spectral statistics allow? Second, and most decisively, a time-reversal counterfactual: we feed the model a forward trajectory versus its time-reverse (identical instantaneous statistics, inverted energy flux) and compare against a phase-scrambled control matched on the energy spectrum; a mechanistic representation must flip its internal flux signal and mis-forecast the reversed (unphysical, up-scale-cascading) trajectory, while responding little to the spectrum-matched scramble. Third, causal steering of a recovered cascade-flux direction should shift small-scale energy generation as physics predicts. Metrics quantify a flux-beyond-snapshot index, a forward-vs-reversed asymmetry, and a steering-selectivity ratio. Either outcome sharpens whether turbulence foundation models represent the mechanism that defines turbulence.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load pretrained physics foundation models with public weights that handle fluid dynamics (Poseidon, Multiple-Physics-Pretraining, DPOT). Assemble forced/decaying isotropic-turbulence trajectories from the Johns Hopkins Turbulence Database (JHTDB) and standard 2D/3D DNS (channel/isotropic) benchmarks. For each timestep and scale band k, compute the ground-truth signed spectral inter-scale energy flux Pi(k) (filtered subgrid energy transfer / spectral energy budget), the energy spectrum E(k), and the mean dissipation rate. Extract per-timestep internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes on internal representations to decode the energy spectrum E(k), total energy, and the signed flux Pi(k) per scale band, confirming what is encoded and selecting best layers. Establish single-snapshot baselines: predict Pi(k) from a single frame's spectral/structure-function statistics (which, being near time-symmetric, should carry weak directional information).", "3. Flux-beyond-snapshot probe (primary probe): Test whether the signed flux Pi(k) is decodable from the model's representation of a SHORT TRAJECTORY (multiple frames) beyond what a single-snapshot spectral-statistics baseline achieves. Primary metric: Flux-Beyond-Snapshot Index = probe R^2 for signed Pi(k) from the model representation minus R^2 from single-frame statistics. Also test whether the recovered flux direction captures the correct SIGN (net forward transfer) and the -5/3 inertial-range scaling, versus a magnitude-only readout.", "4. Time-reversal counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For each trajectory, construct (a) the forward trajectory and (b) its exact time-reverse (reverse frame order and flip velocity sign), which has identical instantaneous statistics but an inverted, physically-forbidden up-scale cascade. Feed both to the model and measure (i) the change in its internal signed-flux signal and (ii) its rollout/forecast error. Compare against (c) a phase-scrambled control that randomizes spatial phases while preserving E(k) (changes appearance, preserves spectrum, destroys coherent cascade structure). Metrics: Forward-vs-Reversed Flux-Sign Flip = fraction of trajectories where the internal flux signal reverses sign under time reversal; Forward-vs-Reversed Rollout Asymmetry = rollout error(reversed) / rollout error(forward); and comparison to the spectrum-matched scramble to show the effect is cascade-specific, not generic OOD. Mechanistic representation => large flip and asymmetry for time-reversal but small response to spectrum-matched scramble; instantaneous-statistics representation => symmetric response.", "5. Physics dose-response: Across trajectories spanning a range of dissipation rates / Reynolds numbers, test whether the internal flux signal tracks the physically-correct mean flux (equal to dissipation rate in the inertial range) with the right sign and magnitude scaling. Metric: Spearman correlation and slope agreement between internal flux readout and true Pi; shuffled labels as control.", "6. Causal cascade-flux steering: Recover a signed cascade-flux direction (supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between high-forward-flux and low/backward-flux states). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's rollout produces the physically-consistent change in small-scale energy generation (increasing forward flux should build up small-scale energy / sharpen the spectrum tail). Controls: matched-norm random directions and an energy-magnitude direction should not reproduce the directional small-scale-buildup effect. Metric: steering-selectivity = |cascade-consistent effect| / |random-direction effect|.", "7. Failure-prediction link: Test whether an internal 'cascade-representation strength' score (flux-sign confidence / time-reversal sensitivity) predicts, per trajectory, where the model's rollout diverges from DNS, especially in high-Reynolds intermittent regimes where the cascade is strongest. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error rollouts from the internal signal, vs output-uncertainty and spectrum-only baselines.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where directional-flux structure emerges across depth (hypothesizing an intermediate 'coarse-graining' zone) and whether recovered cascade-flux subspaces align across the different foundation models (CKA), assessing whether the cascade is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) an LES/subgrid-flux estimator that computes Pi(k) by construction, and (lower bound) a single-snapshot spectral classifier with no temporal information, placing foundation models on the snapshot-statistics-to-cascade-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["The signed flux Pi(k) correlates with instantaneous spatial structure (strain/enstrophy configurations), making the flux-beyond-snapshot separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with single-snapshot residualization, the time-reversal counterfactual (which holds instantaneous statistics fixed and inverts only direction), and the spectrum-matched phase-scramble control that a cascade-specific effect would not respond to.", "Feeding a time-reversed (unphysical) trajectory pushes the model off its training distribution, so the reversed-rollout error could reflect generic OOD failure rather than cascade sensitivity; we control this with the spectrum-matched scramble (also OOD but non-directional) and by testing the internal flux-sign flip on inputs, which is an intra-representation measure less confounded by output-space OOD.", "Some physics FMs operate on fixed short input windows or specific resolutions that may limit access to multi-frame temporal information needed to see direction; we document each model's input protocol, use the maximal available window, and prioritize models that ingest trajectories.", "Ground-truth Pi(k) depends on the filtering/decomposition choice (spectral vs physical-space filter); we use standard filtered subgrid transfer and report sensitivity to filter width and scale-band definition.", "Linear probes recovering a flux direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/energy-magnitude-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions.", "A negative result (models encode instantaneous field statistics but not the directional, time-irreversible cascade, responding symmetrically to time reversal) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting turbulence surrogates for long rollouts and extrapolation, though it may be perceived as confirmatory of skepticism; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether turbulence foundation models represent the energy cascade and its broken time-symmetry.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public DNS/turbulence datasets (JHTDB), keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
