ignal tracks the physically-correct mean flux (equal to dissipation rate in the inertial range) with the right sign and magnitude scaling. Metric: Spearman correlation and slope agreement between internal flux readout and true Pi; shuffled labels as control.', "6. Causal cascade-flux steering: Recover a signed cascade-flux direction (supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between high-forward-flux and low/backward-flux states). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's rollout produces the physically-consistent change in small-scale energy generation (increasing forward flux should build up small-scale energy / sharpen the spectrum tail). Controls: matched-norm random directions and an energy-magnitude direction should not reproduce the directional small-scale-buildup effect. Metric: steering-selectivity = |cascade-consistent effect| / |random-direction effect|.", "7. Failure-prediction link: Test whether an internal 'cascade-representation strength' score (flux-sign confidence / time-reversal sensitivity) predicts, per trajectory, where the model's rollout diverges from DNS, especially in high-Reynolds intermittent regimes where the cascade is strongest. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error rollouts from the internal signal, vs output-uncertainty and spectrum-only baselines.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where directional-flux structure emerges across depth (hypothesizing an intermediate 'coarse-graining' zone) and whether recovered cascade-flux subspaces align across the different foundation models (CKA), assessing whether the cascade is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) an LES/subgrid-flux estimator that computes Pi(k) by construction, and (lower bound) a single-snapshot spectral classifier with no temporal information, placing foundation models on the snapshot-statistics-to-cascade-mechanism spectrum."], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['The signed flux Pi(k) correlates with instantaneous spatial structure (strain/enstrophy configurations), making the flux-beyond-snapshot separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with single-snapshot residualization, the time-reversal counterfactual (which holds instantaneous statistics fixed and inverts only direction), and the spectrum-matched phase-scramble control that a cascade-specific effect would not respond to.', 'Feeding a time-reversed (unphysical) trajectory pushes the model off its training distribution, so the reversed-rollout error could reflect generic OOD failure rather than cascade sensitivity; we control this with the spectrum-matched scramble (also OOD but non-directional) and by testing the internal flux-sign flip on inputs, which is an intra-representation measure less confounded by output-space OOD.', "Some physics FMs operate on fixed short input windows or specific resolutions that may limit access to multi-frame temporal information needed to see direction; we document each model's input protocol, use the maximal available window, and prioritize models that ingest trajectories.", 'Ground-truth Pi(k) depends on the filtering/decomposition choice (spectral vs physical-space filter); we use standard filtered subgrid transfer and report sensitivity to filter width and scale-band definition.', 'Linear probes recovering a flux direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/energy-magnitude-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions.', 'A negative result (models encode instantaneous field statistics but not the directional, time-irreversible cascade, responding symmetrically to time reversal) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting turbulence surrogates for long rollouts and extrapolation, though it may be perceived as confirmatory of skepticism; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether turbulence foundation models represent the energy cascade and its broken time-symmetry.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public DNS/turbulence datasets (JHTDB), keeping compute at academic scale.']}

