Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "contralateral_symmetry_probe", "Title": "Compared to the Other Side? Testing Whether Radiology Foundation Models Represent the Bilateral-Symmetry Comparison Mechanism, Not Just Local Texture", "Short Hypothesis": "A large, clinically dominant class of radiological findings is defined RELATIONALLY by bilateral asymmetry: the body is approximately bilaterally symmetric, so radiologists call a region abnormal when it DIFFERS from its contralateral counterpart (one whiter lung, one denser breast, one effusion-filled hemithorax), and the identical local texture appearing symmetrically on both sides is often a normal variant or systemic. We hypothesize that if radiology foundation models represent this contralateral-comparison mechanism rather than absolute local texture, then (a) a region's abnormality signal is decodable from internal representations beyond what its local patch texture alone predicts, (b) a symmetry counterfactual - implanting the same abnormal-looking texture bilaterally (symmetric, should suppress asymmetry-defined pathology) versus unilaterally (asymmetric, pathology) - shifts the internal finding signal accordingly, whereas a texture-only model responds identically, and (c) the effect causally depends on the contralateral region. The bilateral-vs-unilateral implant is an exact, mechanism-defining counterfactual that texture-based correlation and concept-alignment interpretability cannot provide.", "Related Work": "Radiology-imaging interpretability is dominated by (i) shortcut/spurious-feature detection and mitigation (Detecting Shortcuts in Chest X-Rays 2023; 'Shortcuts Causing Bias in Radiology AI' 2023; RoentMod counterfactual editing 2025; Reveal2Revise medical-AI-safety 2025; Weight-Space Correlation feature-utilization 2025), (ii) concept dissection and alignment (Mammo-CLIP Dissect 2025; Mammo-SAE 2025; expert-labeled CNN units 2018), and (iii) representation-quality/robustness critiques (ViT semantic-meaningfulness 2025; pathology representational-similarity 2025) and anatomy-aware SSL (AFiRe 2025, RayDINO 2024, EXACT 2026). All of these test whether features are spurious, whether units align with named concepts, or whether representations are robust - i.e. LOCAL, per-region annotation-matching or shortcut auditing. None tests whether the model internally performs the RELATIONAL contralateral (bilateral-symmetry) COMPARISON that formally defines a major class of radiological findings, whether that comparison is causally load-bearing, or uses a bilateral-vs-unilateral implant counterfactual to separate the symmetry mechanism from absolute texture. Because abnormal texture and abnormal asymmetry co-occur in real scans, correlation and concept-alignment cannot arbitrate this. Our relational-beyond-local probe, symmetric-vs-asymmetric implant counterfactual, and causal contralateral-ablation are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, moving beyond 'the model sees the opacity' to 'the model represents that this side differs from the other.'", "Abstract": "Radiologists diagnose a large fraction of findings not by absolute local appearance but by comparison: the body is approximately bilaterally symmetric, so a whiter lung, a denser breast, or a fluid-filled hemithorax is flagged because it differs from the opposite side, while the same texture appearing on both sides is often a normal variant. This relational bilateral-symmetry mechanism is exactly known and clinically central, making it an ideal probe for whether radiology foundation models learn mechanism or merely local texture. We test whether models such as RayDINO, a multi-anatomy X-ray encoder, and Mammo-CLIP internally represent the contralateral-comparison mechanism, using public chest X-ray (MIMIC-CXR, CheXpert) and mammography data with lateralized finding labels. First, a relational-beyond-local probe: is a region's abnormality decodable from internal representations beyond what its local patch texture alone predicts, and does the recovered signal track left-right difference? Second, and most decisively, a symmetry counterfactual: using anatomically realistic image editing we implant the same abnormal-looking texture either bilaterally (symmetric, which for asymmetry-defined findings should suppress the pathology signal) or unilaterally (asymmetric, pathology), and measure whether the internal finding signal responds to the asymmetry rather than the raw texture; a texture-only model responds identically to both. Third, causal contralateral ablation: masking or replacing the opposite side should shift the finding signal for a mechanism-representing model but not a local one. Metrics quantify a relational-beyond-local index, a bilateral-vs-unilateral causal asymmetry, and a contralateral-dependence ratio. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed radiology models diagnose for the right relational reason.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load public radiology foundation models with accessible weights and spatial (per-patch) representations: a chest X-ray SSL encoder (RayDINO), a multi-anatomy X-ray model where available, and a mammography vision-language model (Mammo-CLIP). Use MIMIC-CXR and CheXpert (chest radiographs, with laterality-annotatable findings: pleural effusion, pneumothorax, lobar/unilateral consolidation, asymmetric lung opacity) and a mammography set (CBIS-DDSM / VinDr-Mammo, with breast-asymmetry and unilateral-density labels). Derive, per image, the finding location, its laterality, and a bilateral-symmetry-defined vs absolute-texture-defined finding tag. Extract per-patch internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode (a) local patch abnormality (does this patch contain abnormal texture) and (b) whether a region is pathological given its clinical (relational) label. Establish a strict LOCAL texture baseline that sees only the target patch (no contralateral context) and select best layers.", "3. Relational-beyond-local probe (primary probe): For asymmetry-defined findings, test whether the region's clinical abnormality is decodable BEYOND its local patch texture. Regress out the local-patch-predictable component of the representation and re-decode pathology from the residual; also test whether the recovered signal tracks the LEFT-RIGHT difference (correlate the region representation with the difference between it and its mirrored contralateral region representation). Primary metric: Relational-Beyond-Local Index = probe accuracy from the model representation minus what local-patch texture alone achieves. A mechanism-representing model carries contralateral-difference information; a texture model does not exceed the local baseline. Control: for absolute-texture-defined findings (e.g., a calcification whose diagnosis does not require the other side) the relational index should be near zero, giving a double dissociation.", "4. Symmetric-vs-asymmetric implant counterfactual (primary, decisive test): Using anatomically realistic image editing (RoentMod-style counterfactual insertion for chest X-rays; contralateral copy-paste / mirroring for controlled texture implants), construct matched pairs. (a) Unilateral implant: insert an abnormal-looking texture (e.g., increased opacity / density) on ONE side (asymmetric, pathological). (b) Bilateral implant: insert the SAME texture symmetrically on BOTH sides (symmetric, which for asymmetry-defined findings often reads as normal variant / systemic). Match the two edits on total implanted texture. Measure the change in the model's internal finding signal (probed pathology / classification-head output for the asymmetry-defined finding) between the two edits. Metric: Bilateral-vs-Unilateral Causal Asymmetry = |finding-signal change under unilateral implant| / |finding-signal change under bilateral implant|. Mechanistic (symmetry-comparison) representation => asymmetry >> 1 (unilateral triggers the finding, bilateral suppresses it); texture-only representation => asymmetry ~ 1 (both raise the signal equally, since it only counts texture). Report per finding type.", "5. Contralateral-ablation causal test: Mask, black-out, or replace the CONTRALATERAL region (the healthy opposite side of a true unilateral finding) with a matched normal patch and measure the change in the model's finding signal for the (untouched) pathological side. A mechanism-representing model should CHANGE its judgment when the reference side is removed/altered (it can no longer compute the difference), while a local-texture model should be unaffected. Controls: ablating an equally-sized, equally-distant IPSILATERAL non-diagnostic region should not produce the same shift. Metric: Contralateral-Dependence Ratio = |signal change under contralateral ablation| / |signal change under matched ipsilateral-control ablation|.", "6. Causal symmetry-direction steering: Recover a 'left-right asymmetry / contralateral-difference' direction (via a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between symmetric and asymmetric versions of the same scan). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's finding readout shifts in the asymmetry-consistent direction, while matched-norm random directions and a raw-texture-magnitude direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of asymmetry direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "7. Failure-prediction and safety link: Test whether an internal 'symmetry-comparison strength' score (relational-beyond-local confidence / contralateral-dependence) predicts, per case, where the model's finding judgment is wrong - especially on hard cases: bilaterally symmetric normal variants misread as unilateral pathology, and genuine unilateral findings on scans with contralateral distractors. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error cases from the internal signal, versus output-confidence and local-texture baselines.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where contralateral-comparison structure emerges across depth and whether recovered asymmetry subspaces align across the different foundation models (CKA), assessing whether the bilateral-symmetry mechanism is a convergent internal property or absent/model-specific. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a model with an explicit contralateral-difference input branch (Siamese left-right comparison) and (lower bound) a single-patch texture classifier with no contralateral access, placing foundation models on the local-texture-to-symmetry-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Abnormal texture and abnormal asymmetry co-occur in real scans, making the relational-beyond-local separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with local-patch residualization, the bilateral-vs-unilateral implant counterfactual (which holds implanted texture fixed and varies only symmetry), the absolute-texture-defined-finding double-dissociation control, and matched ipsilateral-ablation controls.", "The implant and ablation counterfactuals are synthetic edits; anatomically unrealistic implants (especially bilateral copies) could push images off the model's data distribution, confounding the effect with generic OOD failure. We mitigate with validated realistic editing (RoentMod-style for chest X-ray, careful contralateral mirroring/copy for controlled texture), report realism/artifact diagnostics, and compare unilateral vs bilateral edits of matched realism so any OOD effect is shared.", "Bilateral symmetry is only approximate (cardiac silhouette, gastric bubble, handedness), so a truly symmetric implant is not perfectly normal; we restrict primary tests to anatomically near-symmetric regions (lung fields, breast parenchyma) and report sensitivity, and we use radiologist-adjudicated laterality labels where available.", "Some findings ARE defined by absolute texture (calcifications, specific nodules) rather than asymmetry; we explicitly separate asymmetry-defined from texture-defined findings and use the latter as a negative control rather than expecting a relational signal.", "Foundation models that pool to a single global embedding limit per-region/contralateral analysis; we prioritize models exposing per-patch spatial representations (RayDINO, ViT patch tokens, patch-level Mammo-CLIP) and report pooling caveats.", "Linear probes recovering an asymmetry direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering, contralateral ablation, and random/texture-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions.", "A negative result (models diagnose from absolute local texture and respond identically to symmetric vs asymmetric implants, failing the contralateral-ablation test) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant - it would warn that deployed radiology models miss the relational comparison radiologists rely on, risking false positives on symmetric normal variants and false negatives when contralateral context matters; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether radiology foundation models represent the bilateral-symmetry comparison mechanism.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public radiology datasets (MIMIC-CXR, CheXpert, CBIS-DDSM/VinDr-Mammo) with validated counterfactual editing tools, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
