Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "hill_cooperativity_shape_probe", "Title": "Steepness Is Not Potency: Testing Whether Drug-Response Foundation Models Represent the Cooperative Hill Dose-Response Mechanism, Not Just Where the Curve Sits", "Short Hypothesis": "The dose-response of a drug is governed by an exactly-known relational mechanism, the Hill equation: response is a sigmoidal function of dose whose LOCATION is set by potency (IC50/EC50) but whose STEEPNESS is set by the Hill coefficient nH, which encodes cooperativity/multi-site binding (nH=1 is non-cooperative Michaelis-Menten-like; nH>1 is positively cooperative, ultrasensitive; nH<1 is negatively cooperative). Potency and cooperativity are physically independent axes: two drugs with identical IC50 can have completely different curve shapes and therefore different therapeutic windows and safety margins. We hypothesize that if drug-response foundation models represent this mechanism rather than a scalar potency proxy, then (a) the Hill coefficient (curve shape/cooperativity) is decodable from internal representations along an axis distinct from potency, (b) a shape-vs-potency counterfactual - changing nH at fixed IC50 versus changing IC50 at fixed nH - moves the two internal signals separately, and (c) steering a recovered 'cooperativity' direction causally reshapes predicted dose-response steepness without shifting its location. The shape-holding counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that dose-response prediction accuracy and potency correlation cannot provide.", "Related Work": "The Hill equation is the century-old, exactly-known model of dose-response and cooperativity (A hundred years of the Hill equation 2013; Hill coefficients, dose-response curves and allosteric mechanisms 2009; multiphasic dose-response fitting 2015 shows ~28% of curves need beyond-Hill shape), and it explicitly separates potency (IC50) from cooperativity (nH). Drug-response and pharmacology ML - DrugCell, cell-viability foundation models, and molecular-property FMs - predict dose-response or IC50 from drug/cell features, and interpretability of chemistry/toxicity models (Interpreting NN Toxicity Prediction by Extracting Learned Chemical Features 2024; SAE studies on chemistry FMs; MoleX) recovers substructure/functional-group features validated against POINTWISE, potency-like annotations. Physics/scientific-model interpretability (From Neurons to Neutrons 2024; Physics Steering 2025) recovers and steers single scalar quantities, and 'What Has a Foundation Model Found?' (2025) shows models often learn task-specific heuristics rather than the underlying world model. Crucially, no interpretability work tests whether a drug-response FM internally represents the RELATIONAL Hill mechanism - specifically that curve STEEPNESS/cooperativity (nH) is a distinct axis from potency (IC50) - whether that shape representation is causally load-bearing, or uses a shape-holding counterfactual to separate cooperativity from potency. Because nH and IC50 are entangled and both correlate with overall efficacy in screening data, correlation-based benchmarks and pointwise-feature interpretability cannot arbitrate this. Our cooperativity-beyond-potency probe, shape-vs-potency counterfactual, and causal steepness steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, and they land in a safety-critical setting because curve steepness (Hill coefficient) directly governs therapeutic window and toxicity margins.", "Abstract": "A drug's dose-response curve is described by an exactly-known relational mechanism, the Hill equation: response rises sigmoidally with dose, its location fixed by potency (IC50) and its steepness fixed by the Hill coefficient, which encodes cooperativity and multi-site binding. These are physically independent axes - two drugs with identical potency can have radically different curve shapes, and the shape (steepness) determines the therapeutic window and safety margin. This makes the Hill equation an ideal, mechanism-defining probe for whether drug-response foundation models learn the underlying pharmacological mechanism or merely a scalar potency proxy. We probe pretrained drug-response and molecular-property foundation models on large cell-viability screens (GDSC, PRISM, NCI-60) whose curves are fitted to Hill parameters. First, a cooperativity-beyond-potency probe: is the Hill coefficient (curve steepness) decodable from internal representations along an axis distinct from potency? Second, and most decisively, a shape-vs-potency counterfactual: using controlled synthetic and matched real curves we change the Hill coefficient at fixed IC50 (reshape without relocating) versus change IC50 at fixed Hill (relocate without reshaping), and test whether the two internal signals move independently - a mechanistic representation separates them, a potency-proxy representation collapses shape onto location. Third, causal steepness steering: perturbing a recovered cooperativity direction should reshape the predicted curve's steepness without shifting its midpoint. Metrics quantify a cooperativity-beyond-potency index, a shape-potency disentanglement ratio, and a Hill-fidelity dose-response test. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed drug-response models represent the curve-shape mechanism that governs drug safety.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load pretrained drug-response / pharmacology foundation models with public weights and usable representations - a cell-viability / dose-response predictor (e.g., DrugCell or a transformer trained on GDSC/PRISM) and a molecular-property foundation model applied to drug SMILES (ChemBERTa-2 / SMI-TED). Assemble large fitted dose-response datasets: GDSC1/2, PRISM Repurposing, and NCI-60, each providing per drug-cell-line curves with fitted Hill parameters (IC50/EC50 and Hill coefficient nH) and the raw dose-response points. For every curve record IC50, nH, max effect (Emax), and a shape-vs-location tag. Extract internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode IC50 (potency), nH (steepness/cooperativity), and Emax from internal representations across layers, confirming what is encoded and selecting best layers. Establish scalar baselines: predict nH directly from potency and from simple raw-curve features to quantify how much shape information exceeds trivial correlates.", "3. Cooperativity-beyond-potency probe (primary probe): Test whether the Hill coefficient is decodable BEYOND potency. Regress out the potency-predictable component of the representation and re-decode nH from the residual. Primary metric: Cooperativity-Beyond-Potency Index = probe R^2 for nH from the model representation minus what potency (and Emax) alone achieve. Also measure the angle between the recovered potency direction and the recovered cooperativity direction; a mechanistic representation encodes them as distinct (near-orthogonal) axes, a potency-proxy representation collapses them.", "4. Shape-vs-potency counterfactual (primary, decisive test): Construct matched curve pairs. (a) Reshape edit: change the Hill coefficient nH (make the curve steeper/shallower) while holding IC50 fixed (same potency, different cooperativity). (b) Relocate edit: change IC50 (shift the curve left/right) while holding nH fixed (different potency, same shape). Use both synthetic curves generated from the Hill equation (exact control) and matched real drug-cell pairs selected to differ in one parameter at a time. Feed the curves/inputs to the model and measure the change in the internal potency signal and the internal cooperativity signal for each edit. Metric: Shape-Potency Disentanglement Ratio = for the reshape edit, |change in cooperativity signal| / |change in potency signal|; and for the relocate edit, |change in potency signal| / |change in cooperativity signal|. A mechanistic representation gives both ratios >> 1 (each edit moves its own axis, a double dissociation); a potency-proxy representation gives ratios near 1 or collapses shape onto location.", "5. Hill-fidelity dose-response test: Sweep dose finely and test whether the model's predicted response curve actually follows a Hill sigmoid with the correct steepness (fit the model's predicted curve to a Hill equation and compare recovered nH to ground truth), rather than a fixed-steepness or linear response. Metric: correlation and slope agreement between the model-implied nH and the true nH across drugs; also test whether the model reproduces ULTRASENSITIVITY (nH>1 curves being sharper than nH=1) rather than defaulting to a single canonical steepness. Shuffled-nH labels serve as a control.", "6. Causal steepness steering: Recover a 'cooperativity/steepness' direction (a supervised probe direction, an SAE feature separating steep from shallow curves, or a delta-representation between high-nH and low-nH cases at matched IC50). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's predicted dose-response curve becomes steeper/shallower WITHOUT shifting its midpoint (IC50), while a matched potency direction shifts the midpoint without changing steepness. Controls: matched-norm random directions should do neither. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |steepness change from cooperativity steering| / |steepness change from random steering|, and cross-talk = |IC50 shift from cooperativity steering| (should be near zero for a disentangled representation).", "7. Safety-relevance / failure-prediction link: Curve steepness governs therapeutic window - a steep (high-nH) curve has a narrow margin between ineffective and toxic doses. Test whether an internal 'cooperativity-representation strength' score predicts, per drug, where the model mis-estimates steepness (and therefore would mis-estimate the safety margin), especially for the ~28% of curves that deviate from a canonical single-Hill shape (multiphasic/biphasic). Metric: AUROC for flagging high-steepness-error curves from the internal signal, versus output-confidence and potency-only baselines.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where cooperativity structure emerges across depth and whether recovered cooperativity subspaces align across the drug-response FM and the molecular-property FM (CKA), assessing whether the Hill shape mechanism is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) an explicit two-parameter Hill regressor that separates IC50 and nH by construction, and (lower bound) a single-scalar potency predictor with no shape parameter, placing foundation models on the potency-proxy-to-Hill-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["nH and IC50 are entangled in screening data (both correlate with overall efficacy and with drug/target class), making the cooperativity-beyond-potency separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with potency-residualization, the shape-vs-potency double-dissociation counterfactual (which holds one parameter fixed while varying the other), and synthetic Hill curves giving exact independent control.", "Fitted Hill coefficients from screening data are noisy, especially for curves with few doses or shallow responses, adding label noise to nH; we restrict primary analyses to well-sampled, high-confidence fits, use synthetic curves for the decisive counterfactual, and report sensitivity to fit-quality thresholds; multiphasic curves are analyzed separately.", "Some drug-response FMs take only drug and cell-line identity (not a curve) as input and output a single scalar (e.g., IC50), so 'representing curve shape' may be architecturally out of scope for them; we prioritize models that predict full dose-response curves or ingest dose, clearly report per-model scope, and treat scalar-only models as an informative lower bracket that by construction cannot represent nH.", "Operationalizing the shape/potency edits depends on the model's input format (curve-in vs identity-in); for identity-in models we test whether the internal representation nonetheless encodes nH as a distinct axis and use synthetic-curve models where a curve can be manipulated directly, reporting the caveat.", "Linear probes recovering a cooperativity direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering, cross-talk controls, and random-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and SAE features.", "A negative result (models encode a scalar potency proxy and collapse curve shape onto location, failing the shape-vs-potency counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant - it would warn that deployed drug-response models miss the cooperativity/steepness information that determines therapeutic window and toxicity margin, especially for ultrasensitive or multiphasic drugs; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether drug-response foundation models represent the Hill cooperative dose-response mechanism.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes/SAEs and public dose-response datasets (GDSC, PRISM, NCI-60), keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
