ternal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.', '2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode IC50 (potency), nH (steepness/cooperativity), and Emax from internal representations across layers, confirming what is encoded and selecting best layers. Establish scalar baselines: predict nH directly from potency and from simple raw-curve features to quantify how much shape information exceeds trivial correlates.', '3. Cooperativity-beyond-potency probe (primary probe): Test whether the Hill coefficient is decodable BEYOND potency. Regress out the potency-predictable component of the representation and re-decode nH from the residual. Primary metric: Cooperativity-Beyond-Potency Index = probe R^2 for nH from the model representation minus what potency (and Emax) alone achieve. Also measure the angle between the recovered potency direction and the recovered cooperativity direction; a mechanistic representation encodes them as distinct (near-orthogonal) axes, a potency-proxy representation collapses them.', '4. Shape-vs-potency counterfactual (primary, decisive test): Construct matched curve pairs. (a) Reshape edit: change the Hill coefficient nH (make the curve steeper/shallower) while holding IC50 fixed (same potency, different cooperativity). (b) Relocate edit: change IC50 (shift the curve left/right) while holding nH fixed (different potency, same shape). Use both synthetic curves generated from the Hill equation (exact control) and matched real drug-cell pairs selected to differ in one parameter at a time. Feed the curves/inputs to the model and measure the change in the internal potency signal and the internal cooperativity signal for each edit. Metric: Shape-Potency Disentanglement Ratio = for the reshape edit, |change in cooperativity signal| / |change in potency signal|; and for the relocate edit, |change in potency signal| / |change in cooperativity signal|. A mechanistic representation gives both ratios >> 1