tric: AUROC for flagging high-steepness-error curves from the internal signal, versus output-confidence and potency-only baselines.", '8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where cooperativity structure emerges across depth and whether recovered cooperativity subspaces align across the drug-response FM and the molecular-property FM (CKA), assessing whether the Hill shape mechanism is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) an explicit two-parameter Hill regressor that separates IC50 and nH by construction, and (lower bound) a single-scalar potency predictor with no shape parameter, placing foundation models on the potency-proxy-to-Hill-mechanism spectrum.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['nH and IC50 are entangled in screening data (both correlate with overall efficacy and with drug/target class), making the cooperativity-beyond-potency separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with potency-residualization, the shape-vs-potency double-dissociation counterfactual (which holds one parameter fixed while varying the other), and synthetic Hill curves giving exact independent control.', 'Fitted Hill coefficients from screening data are noisy, especially for curves with few doses or shallow responses, adding label noise to nH; we restrict primary analyses to well-sampled, high-confidence fits, use synthetic curves for the decisive counterfactual, and report sensitivity to fit-quality thresholds; multiphasic curves are analyzed separately.', "Some drug-response FMs take only drug and cell-line identity (not a curve) as input and output a single scalar (e.g., IC50), so 'representing curve shape' may be architecturally out of scope for them; we prioritize models that predict full dose-response curves or ingest dose, clearly report per-model scope, and treat scalar-only models as an informative lower bracket that by construction cannot represent nH.", "Operationalizing the shape/potency edits depends on the model's input format (curve-in vs identity-in); for identity-in models we test whether the internal representation nonetheless encodes nH as a distinct axis and use synthetic-curve models where a curve can be manipulated directly, reporting the caveat.", 'Linear probes recovering a cooperativity direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering, cross-talk controls, and random-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and SAE features.', 'A negative result (models encode a scalar potency proxy and collapse curve shape onto location, failing the shape-vs-potency counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant - it would warn that deployed drug-response models miss the cooperativity/steepness information that determines therapeutic window and toxicity margin, especially for ultrasensitive or multiphasic drugs; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether drug-response foundation models represent the Hill cooperative dose-response mechanism.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes/SAEs and public dose-response datasets (GDSC, PRISM, NCI-60), keeping compute at academic scale.']}

