Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "amoc_density_buoyancy_probe", "Title": "Density, Not Just Temperature: Testing Whether Ocean Foundation Models Represent the Buoyancy-Driven AMOC Mechanism via a Temperature-Salinity Compensation Counterfactual", "Short Hypothesis": "The Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) is driven by an exactly-known relational mechanism: overturning strength scales (via thermal wind) with the meridional DENSITY gradient set at high latitudes by surface buoyancy loss, where density depends nonlinearly on BOTH temperature and salinity through the equation of state \u2014 warming and freshening both REDUCE density and both weaken overturning. This means the same sea-surface temperature (SST) field can imply strengthening or weakening AMOC depending on salinity. We hypothesize that if ocean/climate foundation models represent the buoyancy mechanism rather than SST statistics, then (a) the AMOC-relevant density gradient is decodable from internal representations beyond what temperature alone predicts, (b) a temperature-salinity compensation counterfactual that changes T and S together so surface DENSITY is unchanged shifts the internal overturning signal little, whereas a matched edit that changes density (equal T change, no compensating S change) shifts it strongly, and (c) steering a recovered density-gradient direction causally shifts predicted overturning. The density-compensated-vs-uncompensated counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that SST-based correlation cannot pass.", "Related Work": "Interpretability of Earth-system foundation models has surged: SAE studies on GraphCast (2025) recover interpretable features (tropical cyclones, atmospheric rivers, sea-ice) and steer them; GraphDOP work (2025) shows unified observation-driven world-model representations and applies data-assimilation diagnostics (FSOI); an ocean-atmosphere emulator-error study (2026) correlates GraphCast forecast errors with SST to infer implicit ocean information. All of these recover single features or global input-output error correlations \u2014 none tests whether a model's internal representation encodes the RELATIONAL, density-driven buoyancy mechanism that governs the AMOC, nor uses a temperature-salinity compensation counterfactual to separate density from temperature. Physical oceanography establishes the mechanism precisely: AMOC strength scales with the meridional density gradient via near-geostrophic/thermal-wind balance and is controlled by high-latitude surface buoyancy loss and the nonlinear equation of state (AMOC reviews 2015/2019; 'What Causes the AMOC to Weaken in CMIP5?' 2019 uses thermal wind to attribute weakening to density changes; 'Controls on AMOC strength' 2024 links buoyancy forcing and stratification to overturning). Because SST and density are correlated in ordinary ocean data, correlation-based benchmarks and SST-error diagnostics cannot arbitrate whether a model uses temperature statistics or the density mechanism. Our density-beyond-temperature probe, TS-compensation counterfactual, and causal density-gradient steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, transferred into the safety-critical setting of AMOC tipping-point prediction.", "Abstract": "The Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) is a climate tipping element, and machine-learning ocean/Earth-system foundation models are increasingly used to emulate and forecast ocean state. AMOC dynamics are governed by an exactly-known relational mechanism: overturning strength scales with the meridional density gradient, which is set at high latitudes by surface buoyancy loss, and density depends nonlinearly on both temperature and salinity through the equation of state. Crucially, warming and freshening both reduce density and both weaken overturning, so the same sea-surface-temperature field can accompany strengthening or weakening AMOC depending on salinity. This makes the AMOC an ideal, mechanism-defining testbed for whether ocean foundation models learn the buoyancy mechanism or merely temperature statistics. We probe pretrained ocean/climate foundation models (and observation-driven Earth-system emulators exposing internal states) on reanalysis data (ORAS5/GLORYS, plus CMIP output) where temperature, salinity, density, and the AMOC streamfunction are known. First, a density-beyond-temperature probe: is the AMOC-relevant meridional density gradient decodable from internal representations beyond what temperature alone predicts, along an axis distinct from SST? Second, and most decisively, a temperature-salinity compensation counterfactual: we perturb surface T and S together so that density is unchanged (density-compensated) versus perturbing T without compensating S (density-changing) with matched temperature magnitude; a mechanistic representation must move its overturning signal for the density-changing edit but not the compensated one, while a temperature-statistics model responds to both equally. Third, causal density-gradient steering. Metrics quantify a density-beyond-temperature index, a compensated-vs-uncompensated causal asymmetry, and a buoyancy dose-response test. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed ocean models represent the mechanism behind a major climate tipping point.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load pretrained ocean/Earth-system foundation models with public weights that ingest ocean temperature and salinity and expose internal representations (e.g., ocean emulators / Earth-system foundation models such as ocean-state transformers, and observation-driven emulators like GraphDOP-style models where accessible; as a fallback, a modest multi-variable ocean surrogate we pretrain on reanalysis). Assemble reanalysis and model datasets providing 3D temperature, salinity, potential density, and the diagnosed AMOC streamfunction: ORAS5 and GLORYS ocean reanalyses, plus a set of CMIP6 members spanning AMOC strengths. For each field compute ground-truth density via the equation of state, the North Atlantic meridional density gradient, high-latitude surface buoyancy loss, and the AMOC index (e.g., streamfunction maximum at 26N/40N). Extract internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode SST/temperature, salinity, potential density, the meridional density gradient, and the AMOC index from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded and select best layers. Establish temperature-only baselines: predict density gradient and AMOC index from temperature fields alone.", "3. Density-beyond-temperature probe (primary probe): Test whether the AMOC-relevant meridional density gradient (and AMOC index) is decodable BEYOND temperature. Regress out the temperature-predictable component of the representation and re-decode the density gradient from the residual. Primary metric: Density-Beyond-Temperature Index = probe R^2 for density gradient (and AMOC index) from the model representation minus what temperature alone achieves. Also measure the angle between the recovered temperature direction and the recovered density direction; a mechanistic representation encodes density as an axis distinct from temperature (i.e., incorporates salinity), a temperature-statistics representation collapses them.", "4. Temperature-salinity compensation counterfactual (primary, decisive test): In the North Atlantic deep-water-formation region, construct two matched surface edits holding all else fixed. (a) Density-compensated edit: change surface temperature by delta_T AND change surface salinity by the delta_S that leaves potential density unchanged (using the equation-of-state expansion coefficients), so density and thus the buoyancy-mechanism signal should not change. (b) Density-changing edit: change surface temperature by the same delta_T with NO compensating salinity change, so density changes. Feed both to the model and measure the change in its internal overturning signal (probed density gradient / AMOC index, or the model's forecast AMOC-relevant fields). Metric: Compensated-vs-Uncompensated Causal Asymmetry = |overturning-signal change under density-changing edit| / |change under density-compensated edit|. Mechanistic representation => asymmetry >> 1 (responds to density, not raw temperature); temperature-statistics representation => asymmetry ~ 1 (responds to temperature regardless of compensation). Report as a function of delta_T magnitude and region.", "5. Buoyancy dose-response (physics test): Sweep the density-changing surface buoyancy perturbation (via temperature and/or salinity edits that alter high-latitude density) across a range and test whether the model's internal AMOC signal responds monotonically and with the physically-correct sign \u2014 increasing high-latitude density (cooling/salinification) should strengthen overturning; decreasing it (warming/freshening) should weaken overturning \u2014 following the thermal-wind scaling with the meridional density gradient. Metric: Spearman correlation and sign agreement between induced AMOC-signal change and the physically-predicted change; shuffled-perturbation labels serve as a control. Test both freshwater-hosing-like (salinity) and warming-like (temperature) perturbations to confirm the model integrates both into density, not just temperature.", "6. Causal density-gradient steering: Recover a 'meridional density gradient / overturning' direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between high-density-gradient and low-density-gradient states). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's AMOC-relevant readout shifts in the buoyancy-consistent direction, while matched-norm random directions and a pure SST direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of density-gradient direction| / |effect of random direction|, and cross-talk with the SST direction.", "7. Failure-prediction and tipping-point link: Test whether an internal 'density-representation strength' score (density-beyond-temperature confidence / compensation-counterfactual sensitivity) predicts, per case, where the model mis-estimates AMOC strength \u2014 especially in freshening-dominated scenarios (Arctic/subpolar freshwater input) where the temperature-only shortcut misleads about overturning. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-AMOC-error cases from the internal signal, versus output-uncertainty and temperature-only baselines. This connects the representational signature to reliability near the AMOC tipping point.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where density/overturning structure emerges across depth and whether recovered density subspaces align across the different ocean foundation models (CKA), assessing whether the buoyancy mechanism is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a thermal-wind/density-gradient estimator that computes overturning from density by construction, and (lower bound) an SST-only AMOC regressor, placing foundation models on the temperature-statistics-to-density-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Temperature and density are strongly correlated in ocean data (temperature dominates density variance in many regions), making the density-beyond-temperature separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with temperature-residualization, the TS-compensation counterfactual (which holds density fixed while varying temperature), the buoyancy dose-response test that a temperature-only model cannot reproduce with correct sign in salinity-dominated regimes, and by focusing decisive tests on the subpolar North Atlantic where salinity strongly modulates density.", "The compensation counterfactual is a synthetic surface edit; unrealistic T-S combinations may push inputs off the model's data distribution, confounding the density-changing response with generic out-of-distribution failure. We mitigate with small physically-plausible perturbations, verify density is (un)changed via the equation of state before feeding to the model, report OOD diagnostics, and compare compensated vs uncompensated edits of matched temperature magnitude so any OOD effect is shared.", "Many ocean/Earth-system foundation models are proprietary or lack accessible internal states; we prioritize models with public weights and usable representations and, as a fallback, pretrain a modest multi-variable ocean surrogate on reanalysis (academic-scale) to serve as the primary testbed, reporting this scope clearly.", "The AMOC streamfunction is a deep, integrated quantity while foundation models may emphasize surface fields; the internal 'overturning signal' may be weak or only surface-proxied. We probe both the density gradient (a more directly represented quantity) and any AMOC-index readout, and treat the density-gradient result as the primary mechanism evidence.", "Reanalysis salinity (especially subpolar/estuarine) has known biases, adding label noise; we use consensus regions, multiple reanalysis products, and CMIP output for controlled T-S variation, reporting sensitivity to product choice.", "Linear probes recovering a density direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/SST-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions \u2014 we use both probe directions and delta-representations.", "A negative result (models encode temperature statistics but not the density-driven buoyancy mechanism, failing the compensation counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant \u2014 it would warn against trusting ocean foundation models for AMOC weakening/tipping-point prediction, precisely where freshening breaks the temperature-density correlation; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether ocean foundation models represent the buoyancy-driven AMOC mechanism.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models (or one modest academic-scale surrogate) plus small probes and public reanalysis/CMIP data, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'amoc_density_buoyancy_probe', 'Title': 'Density, Not Just Temperature: Testing Whether Ocean Foundation Models Represent the Buoyancy-Driven AMOC Mechanism via a Temperature-Salinity Compensation Counterfactual', 'Short Hypothesis': 'The Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) is driven by an exactly-known relational mechanism: overturning strength scales (via thermal wind) with the meridional DENSITY gradient set at high latitudes by surface buoyancy loss, where density depends nonlinearly on BOTH temperature and salinity through the equation of state — warming and freshening both REDUCE density and both weaken overturning. This means the same sea-surface temperature (SST) field can imply strengthening or weakening AMOC depending on salinity. We hypothesize that if ocean/climate foundation models represent the buoyancy mechanism rather than SST statistics, then (a) the AMOC-relevant density gradient is decodable from internal representations beyond what temperature alone predicts, (b) a temperature-salinity compensation counterfactual that changes T and S together so surface DENSITY is unchanged shifts the internal overturning signal little, whereas a matched edit that changes density (equal T change, no compensating S change) shifts it strongly, and (c) steering a recovered density-gradient direction causally shifts predicted overturning. The density-compensated-vs-uncompensated counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that SST-based correlation cannot pass.', 'Related Work': "Interpretability of Earth-system foundation models has surged: SAE studies on GraphCast (2025) recover interpretable features (tropical cyclones, atmospheric rivers, sea-ice) and steer them; GraphDOP work (2025) shows unified observation-driven world-model representations and applies data-assimilation diagnostics (FSOI); an ocean-atmosphere emulator-error study (2026) correlates GraphCast forecast errors with SST to infer implicit ocean information. All of these recover single features or global input-output error correlations — none tests whether a model's internal representation encodes the RELATIONAL, density-driven buoyancy mechanism that governs the AMOC, nor uses a temperature-salinity compensation counterfactual to separate density from temperature. Physical oceanography establishes the mechanism precisely: AMOC strength scales with the meridional density gradient via near-geostrophic/thermal-wind balance and is controlled by high-latitude surface buoyancy loss and the nonlinear equation of state (AMOC reviews 2015/2019; 'What Causes the AMOC to Weaken in CMIP5?' 2019 uses thermal wind to attribute weakening to density changes; 'Controls on AMOC strength' 2024 links buoyancy forcing and stratification to overturning). Because SST and density are correlated in ordinary ocean data, correlation-based benchmarks and SST-error diagnostics cannot arbitrate whether a model uses temperature statistics or the density mechanism. Our density-beyond-temperature probe, TS-compensation counterfactual, and causal density-gradient steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, transferred into the safety-critical setting of AMOC tipping-point prediction.", 'Abstract': 'The Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC) is a climate tipping element, and machine-learning ocean/Earth-system foundation models are increasingly used to emulate and forecast ocean state. AMOC dynamics are governed by an exactly-known relational mechanism: overturning strength scales with the meridional density gradient, which is set at high latitudes by surface buoyancy loss, and density depends nonlinearly on both temperature and salinity through the equation of state. Crucially, warming and freshening both reduce density and both weaken overturning, so the same sea-surface-temperature field can accompany strengthening or weakening AMOC depending on salinity. This makes the AMOC an ideal, mechanism-defining testbed for whether ocean foundation models learn the buoyancy mechanism or merely temperature statistics. We probe pretrained ocean/climate foundation models (and observation-driven Earth-system emulators exposing internal states) on reanalysis data (ORAS5/GLORYS, plus CMIP output) where temperature, salinity, density, and the AMOC streamfunction are known. First, a density-beyond-temperature probe: is the AMOC-relevant meridional density gradient decodable from internal representations beyond what temperature alone predicts, along an axis distinct from SST? Second, and most decisively, a temperature-salinity compensation counterfactual: we perturb surface T and S together so that density is unchanged (density-compensated) versus perturbing T without compensating S (density-changing) with matched temperature magnitude; a mechanistic representation must move its overturning signal for the density-changing edit but not the compensated one, while a temperature-statistics model responds to both equally. Third, causal density-gradient steering. Metrics quantify a density-beyond-temperature index, a compensated-vs-uncompensated causal asymmetry, and a buoyancy dose-response test. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed ocean models represent the mechanism behind a major climate tipping point.', 'Experiments': ['1. Models and data: Load pretrained ocean/Earth-system foundation models with public weights that ingest ocean temperature and salinity and expose internal representations (e.g., ocean emulators / Earth-system foundation models such as ocean-state transformers, and observation-driven emulators like GraphDOP-style models where accessible; as a fallback, a modest multi-variable ocean surrogate we pretrain on reanalysis). Assemble reanalysis and model datasets providing 3D temperature, salinity, potential density, and the diagnosed AMOC streamfunction: ORAS5 and GLORYS ocean reanalyses, plus a set of CMIP6 members spanning AMOC strengths. For each field compute ground-truth density via the equation of state, the North Atlantic meridional density gradient, high-latitude surface buoyancy loss, and the AMOC index (e.g., streamfunction maximum at 26N/40N). Extract internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.', '2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode SST/temperature, salinity, potential density, the meridional density gradient, and the AMOC index from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded and select best layers. Establish temperature-only baselines: predict density gradient and AMOC index from temperature fields alone.', '3. Density-beyond-temperature probe (primary probe): Test whether the AMOC-relevant meridional density gradient (and AMOC index) is decodable BEYOND temperature. Regress out the temperature-predictable component of the representation and re-decode the density gradient from the residual. Primary metric: Density-Beyond-Temperature Index = probe R^2 for density gradient (and AMOC index) from the model representation minus what temperature alone achieves. Also measure the angle between the recovered temperature direction and the recovered density direction; a mechanistic representation encodes density as an axis distinct from temperature (i.e., incorporates salinity), a temperature-statistics representation collapses them.', "4. Temperature-salinity compensation counterfactual (primary, decisive test): In the North Atlantic deep-water-formation region, construct two matched surface edits holding all else fixed. (a) Density-compensated edit: change surface temperature by delta_T AND change surface salinity by the delta_S that leaves potential density unchanged (using the equation-of-state expansion coefficients), so density and thus the buoyancy-mechanism signal should not change. (b) Density-changing edit: change surface temperature by the same delta_T with NO compensating salinity change, so density changes. Feed both to the model and measure the change in its internal overturning signal (probed density gradient / AMOC index, or the model's forecast AMOC-relevant fields). Metric: Compensated-vs-Uncompensated Causal Asymmetry = |overturning-signal change under density-changing edit| / |change under density-compensated edit|. Mechanistic representation => asymmetry >> 1 (responds to density, not raw temperature); temperature-statistics representation => asymmetry ~ 1 (responds to temperature regardless of compensation). Report as a function of delta_T magnitude and region.", "5. Buoyancy dose-response (physics test): Sweep the density-changing surface buoyancy perturbation (via temperature and/or salinity edits that alter high-latitude density) across a range and test whether the model's internal AMOC signal responds monotonically and with the physically-correct sign — increasing high-latitude density (cooling/salinification) should strengthen overturning; decreasing it (warming/freshening) should weaken overturning — following the thermal-wind scaling with the meridional density gradient. Metric: Spearman correlation and sign agreement between induced AMOC-signal change and the physically-predicted change; shuffled-perturbation labels serve as a control. Test both freshwater-hosing-like (salinity) and warming-like (temperature) perturbations to confirm the model integrates both into density, not just temperature.", "6. Causal density-gradient steering: Recover a 'meridional density gradient / overturning' direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between high-density-gradient and low-density-gradient states). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's AMOC-relevant readout shifts in the buoyancy-consistent direction, while matched-norm random directions and a pure SST direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of density-gradient direction| / |effect of random direction|, and cross-talk with the SST direction.", "7. Failure-prediction and tipping-point link: Test whether an internal 'density-representation strength' score (density-beyond-temperature confidence / compensation-counterfactual sensitivity) predicts, per case, where the model mis-estimates AMOC strength — especially in freshening-dominated scenarios (Arctic/subpolar freshwater input) where the temperature-only shortcut misleads about overturning. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-AMOC-error cases from the internal signal, versus output-uncertainty and temperature-only baselines. This connects the representational signature to reliability near the AMOC tipping point.", '8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where density/overturning structure emerges across depth and whether recovered density subspaces align across the different ocean foundation models (CKA), assessing whether the buoyancy mechanism is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a thermal-wind/density-gradient estimator that computes overturning from density by construction, and (lower bound) an SST-only AMOC regressor, placing foundation models on the temperature-statistics-to-density-mechanism spectrum.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Temperature and density are strongly correlated in ocean data (temperature dominates density variance in many regions), making the density-beyond-temperature separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with temperature-residualization, the TS-compensation counterfactual (which holds density fixed while varying temperature), the buoyancy dose-response test that a temperature-only model cannot reproduce with correct sign in salinity-dominated regimes, and by focusing decisive tests on the subpolar North Atlantic where salinity strongly modulates density.', "The compensation counterfactual is a synthetic surface edit; unrealistic T-S combinations may push inputs off the model's data distribution, confounding the density-changing response with generic out-of-distribution failure. We mitigate with small physically-plausible perturbations, verify density is (un)changed via the equation of state before feeding to the model, report OOD diagnostics, and compare compensated vs uncompensated edits of matched temperature magnitude so any OOD effect is shared.", 'Many ocean/Earth-system foundation models are proprietary or lack accessible internal states; we prioritize models with public weights and usable representations and, as a fallback, pretrain a modest multi-variable ocean surrogate on reanalysis (academic-scale) to serve as the primary testbed, reporting this scope clearly.', "The AMOC streamfunction is a deep, integrated quantity while foundation models may emphasize surface fields; the internal 'overturning signal' may be weak or only surface-proxied. We probe both the density gradient (a more directly represented quantity) and any AMOC-index readout, and treat the density-gradient result as the primary mechanism evidence.", 'Reanalysis salinity (especially subpolar/estuarine) has known biases, adding label noise; we use consensus regions, multiple reanalysis products, and CMIP output for controlled T-S variation, reporting sensitivity to product choice.', 'Linear probes recovering a density direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/SST-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions — we use both probe directions and delta-representations.', 'A negative result (models encode temperature statistics but not the density-driven buoyancy mechanism, failing the compensation counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant — it would warn against trusting ocean foundation models for AMOC weakening/tipping-point prediction, precisely where freshening breaks the temperature-density correlation; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether ocean foundation models represent the buoyancy-driven AMOC mechanism.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models (or one modest academic-scale surrogate) plus small probes and public reanalysis/CMIP data, keeping compute at academic scale.']}

