Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "pseudoscalar_chirality_probe", "Title": "Sign Flips Under a Mirror: Testing Whether Molecular Foundation Models Represent Chirality as a Pseudoscalar Mechanism, Not Just Enantiomer Labels", "Short Hypothesis": "Chirality is an exactly-known geometric mechanism with a precise mathematical signature: it is a PSEUDOSCALAR quantity - invariant to rotation and translation but flipping SIGN under mirror reflection (parity) - and it is what distinguishes enantiomers, which are identical in every scalar/achiral property yet can differ drastically in binding and toxicity. We hypothesize that if a molecular foundation model represents the chirality MECHANISM rather than a memorized R/S label or surface substructure, then (a) a recoverable internal 'chirality' direction behaves as a pseudoscalar - unchanged under 3D rotation but flipping sign under mirror reflection of the geometry, (b) a mirror-reflection counterfactual that flips ONLY handedness (holding all scalar invariants - distances, angles, connectivity - fixed) flips the internal chirality signal and the model's enantioselective property prediction, whereas an achiral-preserving perturbation of matched magnitude does not, and (c) steering this pseudoscalar direction causally swaps predicted enantiomer-dependent properties. The mirror-flip counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that R/S classification accuracy and substructure correlation cannot provide.", "Related Work": "Two lines bracket the gap. (1) Chirality-aware architectures - ChiENN (2023), tetrahedral-chirality message passing (2020), MolKGNN (2023), ChIRo (2021), ChiDeK (2026), Chi-Geometry benchmark (2025) - are motivated by the proof that standard/E(3)-invariant GNNs CANNOT represent chirality ('E(3)-equivariant models cannot learn chirality', 2024), and they BUILD handedness in by construction, judged by R/S accuracy or enantioselective-property RMSE. (2) FM-representation work hints chirality is decodable: Rem3Di (2026) constructs explicit pseudoscalar features from atomistic-FM latents to distinguish enantiomers, SMILES-heteroencoder latent arithmetic (2025) builds chirality descriptors by subtracting enantiomer latents, and the reaction-analogy GNN work (2025) shows linear structure in atom embeddings. Crucially, all of these either engineer chirality by construction, or measure downstream enantiomer-discrimination accuracy - none tests whether a general molecular foundation model's INTERNAL representation already encodes chirality with its defining pseudoscalar (parity-odd, rotation-even) symmetry, whether that signal is causally load-bearing for enantioselective predictions, or uses a mirror-reflection counterfactual to separate the mechanism from a memorized R/S label. Because R/S labels, chiral substructure, and true handedness are entangled in training data, classification accuracy cannot arbitrate this. Our pseudoscalar-symmetry probe, mirror-flip counterfactual, and causal chirality steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution.", "Abstract": "Chirality - molecular handedness - is one of chemistry's most consequential and exactly-known geometric mechanisms: enantiomers are mirror images identical in every achiral property yet can differ dramatically in biological activity and toxicity (the thalidomide tragedy being the canonical example). Its mathematical signature is precise: chirality is a pseudoscalar, invariant under rotation and translation but flipping sign under mirror reflection. This makes it an ideal, unambiguous probe for whether molecular foundation models learn geometric mechanism or surface statistics - especially since a theorem shows standard E(3)-invariant models cannot represent it at all. We probe pretrained 3D atomistic and molecular foundation models (e.g., atomistic MLIP backbones such as MACE, Uni-Mol, and chirality-capable encoders) plus SMILES/graph chemical FMs, asking three mechanism-defining questions. First, a pseudoscalar-symmetry probe: is a recoverable internal chirality direction invariant under 3D rotation yet sign-flipping under mirror reflection, as physics dictates? Second, and most decisively, a mirror-reflection counterfactual: we reflect a molecule's geometry to flip ONLY handedness while exactly preserving all scalar invariants, and test whether the internal chirality signal and the model's enantioselective property prediction flip, whereas an achiral perturbation of matched magnitude does not. Third, causal steering along the pseudoscalar direction should swap enantiomer-dependent predictions. Metrics quantify a pseudoscalar-fidelity index, a mirror-flip-vs-achiral causal asymmetry, and an enantioselective prediction-swap rate. Either outcome sharpens whether molecular foundation models - increasingly used in drug design where the wrong enantiomer can be toxic - represent the handedness mechanism or are blind to it.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load pretrained molecular foundation models spanning representation types with usable internal states: 3D atomistic FMs (MACE-MP-0 / atomistic MLIP latents as in Rem3Di, Uni-Mol), and 2D/SMILES chemical FMs (ChemBERTa-2, SMI-TED) that DO ingest stereochemistry tags. Assemble datasets of enantiomer pairs with ground truth: R/S-labeled chiral centers (Chi-Geometry synthetic benchmark for clean isolation, plus real drug-like enantiomer pairs), and enantioselective property data (chiral chromatography elution order, optical rotation / ECD sign, enantiosensitive docking scores or protein-binding affinities). For 3D models, generate paired conformers of a molecule and its exact mirror image. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes on internal representations (per layer) to decode (a) achiral scalar features (element counts, connectivity, distances) and (b) R/S configuration / handedness. Confirm what is encoded, select best layers, and establish an achiral-substructure baseline. For E(3)-invariant-by-construction models, verify (as theory predicts) that handedness is NOT decodable - a decisive built-in negative control.", "3. Pseudoscalar-symmetry probe (primary probe): Recover a candidate chirality direction/feature (via a supervised probe separating R from S, or a delta-representation between a molecule and its mirror image). Test its defining symmetry: (a) apply many random 3D rotations to a molecule and confirm the chirality signal is INVARIANT (rotation-even); (b) apply a mirror reflection and confirm the signal FLIPS SIGN (parity-odd). Primary metric: Pseudoscalar-Fidelity Index = (rotation-invariance: 1 - variance under rotation) combined with (mirror sign-flip consistency: fraction of molecules whose chirality signal reverses sign under reflection). A mechanism-representing model gives high rotation-invariance AND consistent sign-flip; an achiral/statistics model shows no consistent parity-odd behavior.", "4. Mirror-reflection counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For each chiral molecule, construct two matched edits. (a) Mirror-flip edit: reflect the 3D geometry to flip handedness (for SMILES/graph models, flip the stereo-descriptor @/@@), holding ALL scalar invariants (distances, angles, connectivity, formula) exactly fixed - this is the enantiomer. (b) Achiral control edit: perturb the geometry by a matched RMSD magnitude in an achiral way (e.g., small conformational change or an isometric distortion that does not change handedness). Measure the change in the internal chirality signal and in the model's enantioselective property prediction (e.g., predicted optical-rotation sign, elution order, docking score) between the two edits. Metric: Mirror-Flip-vs-Achiral Causal Asymmetry = |signal/prediction change under mirror-flip| / |change under matched achiral perturbation|. Mechanistic representation => asymmetry >> 1 (handedness flip specifically moves the chirality signal and the enantioselective prediction); achiral/statistics representation => asymmetry ~ 1.", "5. Enantioselective physics consistency: Test whether the mirror-flip induces the physically-correct SWAP in enantiomer-dependent predictions - e.g., optical rotation should change sign, chiral-column elution order should reverse, and enantiosensitive docking scores should swap between the two pocket-fitting orientations. Metric: fraction of mirror-flips producing the correct sign-reversal / order-swap in the model's readout; a double-dissociation control on ACHIRAL molecules (no chiral center) should show NO such swap.", "6. Causal pseudoscalar steering: Steer the recovered chirality direction with matched magnitude (and with sign +/-) and test whether the model's enantioselective property readout swaps between the R-like and S-like predictions, while matched-norm random directions and an achiral-feature direction do not. Metric: Enantioselective Prediction-Swap Rate under chirality steering vs. control steering; mechanism-selectivity = |effect of chirality direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "7. Central-vs-axial generalization: Test whether the pseudoscalar signal generalizes beyond central (tetrahedral) chirality to axial chirality (atropisomers), where handedness arises from hindered rotation rather than a stereocenter (using ChiDeK's ECD/OR benchmark). This distinguishes a general handedness mechanism from a memorized stereocenter-tag lookup.", "8. Failure-prediction and safety link: Test whether an internal 'chirality-representation strength' score (pseudoscalar fidelity / mirror-flip sensitivity) predicts, per molecule, where the model's enantioselective property prediction is wrong - especially for molecules where the wrong enantiomer is inactive or toxic. Metric: AUROC for flagging enantioselective-prediction errors from the internal signal, vs. output-confidence and achiral-substructure baselines.", "9. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where pseudoscalar chirality structure emerges across depth and whether recovered chirality subspaces align across models (CKA), assessing whether handedness is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a chirality-aware model (ChiENN/ChIRo) that encodes handedness by construction, and (lower bound) an E(3)-invariant model provably blind to chirality, placing FMs on the chirality-blind-to-pseudoscalar-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Many 3D atomistic FMs are E(3)-invariant by construction and therefore provably cannot represent chirality; for these the pseudoscalar probe returns a definitional null, so the rich mechanism tests apply mainly to chirality-capable models (Rem3Di-style pseudoscalar-feature backbones, Uni-Mol with stereochemistry, stereo-aware SMILES/graph FMs). We report the invariant-model null as a decisive, separately-stated positive control and prioritize chirality-capable models for the mechanism tests.", "SMILES/graph models encode chirality via discrete stereo-tags (@/@@) rather than continuous geometry, so 'mirror reflection' is operationalized as a tag flip; this tests whether the model propagates a handedness label to enantioselective predictions (still mechanism-relevant) but is a slightly different claim than geometric pseudoscalar behavior. We run both the 3D-geometry reflection (for 3D models) and the tag-flip (for SMILES models) and clearly distinguish the two claims.", "R/S labels, chiral substructure, and true handedness are entangled, making the mechanism-beyond-label separation demanding; we mitigate with the mirror-flip counterfactual (which holds all scalar invariants and the substructure fixed while flipping only handedness), the achiral-molecule double-dissociation control, and the pseudoscalar-symmetry test that a label-lookup cannot pass under 3D rotations.", "Enantioselective ground-truth data (optical rotation, ECD sign, chiral elution order, enantiosensitive docking) is limited and noisy; we use curated benchmarks (chiral-chromatography datasets, ECD/OR sets, Chi-Geometry) and report sensitivity to data confidence, focusing decisive tests on molecules with a single well-defined chiral element.", "Reflecting a 3D geometry can push conformers off the model's data distribution; but the enantiomer is itself a valid physical molecule, so the reflected geometry is in-distribution by construction - we nonetheless verify conformer validity and match the achiral control perturbation on RMSD so any residual OOD effect is shared.", "Linear probes recovering a chirality direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering, the mirror-flip counterfactual, and random/achiral-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and delta (enantiomer-difference) representations.", "A negative result (models encode R/S labels or achiral substructure but do not represent chirality as a pseudoscalar, failing the mirror-flip counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant - it would warn against trusting molecular FMs for enantioselective property and toxicity prediction, precisely where the wrong enantiomer can be harmful; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether molecular foundation models represent the pseudoscalar chirality mechanism.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public chirality/enantioselective datasets, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
