s across depth and whether recovered chirality subspaces align across models (CKA), assessing whether handedness is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a chirality-aware model (ChiENN/ChIRo) that encodes handedness by construction, and (lower bound) an E(3)-invariant model provably blind to chirality, placing FMs on the chirality-blind-to-pseudoscalar-mechanism spectrum.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Many 3D atomistic FMs are E(3)-invariant by construction and therefore provably cannot represent chirality; for these the pseudoscalar probe returns a definitional null, so the rich mechanism tests apply mainly to chirality-capable models (Rem3Di-style pseudoscalar-feature backbones, Uni-Mol with stereochemistry, stereo-aware SMILES/graph FMs). We report the invariant-model null as a decisive, separately-stated positive control and prioritize chirality-capable models for the mechanism tests.', "SMILES/graph models encode chirality via discrete stereo-tags (@/@@) rather than continuous geometry, so 'mirror reflection' is operationalized as a tag flip; this tests whether the model propagates a handedness label to enantioselective predictions (still mechanism-relevant) but is a slightly different claim than geometric pseudoscalar behavior. We run both the 3D-geometry reflection (for 3D models) and the tag-flip (for SMILES models) and clearly distinguish the two claims.", 'R/S labels, chiral substructure, and true handedness are entangled, making the mechanism-beyond-label separation demanding; we mitigate with the mirror-flip counterfactual (which holds all scalar invariants and the substructure fixed while flipping only handedness), the achiral-molecule double-dissociation control, and the pseudoscalar-symmetry test that a label-lookup cannot pass under 3D rotations.', 'Enantioselective ground-truth data (optical rotation, ECD sign, chiral elution order, enantiosensitive docking) is limited and noisy; we use curated benchmarks (chiral-chromatography datasets, ECD/OR sets, Chi-Geometry) and report sensitivity to data confidence, focusing decisive tests on molecules with a single well-defined chiral element.', "Reflecting a 3D geometry can push conformers off the model's data distribution; but the enantiomer is itself a valid physical molecule, so the reflected geometry is in-distribution by construction - we nonetheless verify conformer validity and match the achiral control perturbation on RMSD so any residual OOD effect is shared.", 'Linear probes recovering a chirality direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering, the mirror-flip counterfactual, and random/achiral-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and delta (enantiomer-difference) representations.', 'A negative result (models encode R/S labels or achiral substructure but do not represent chirality as a pseudoscalar, failing the mirror-flip counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant - it would warn against trusting molecular FMs for enantioselective property and toxicity prediction, precisely where the wrong enantiomer can be harmful; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether molecular foundation models represent the pseudoscalar chirality mechanism.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public chirality/enantioselective datasets, keeping compute at academic scale.']}

