Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "sign_epistasis_context_flip_probe", "Title": "Does the Mutation's Meaning Depend on Its Neighbors? Testing Whether Protein Language Models Internally Represent Context-Dependent Sign Epistasis, Not Just Additive Effects", "Short Hypothesis": "The mechanism-defining signature of epistasis is context-dependence: a mutation's fitness effect is not fixed but depends on the genetic background, and in sign epistasis the effect fully REVERSES (beneficial<->deleterious) when a partner mutation is present. If a protein language model internally represents this mechanism rather than a context-independent additive effect, then the internal representational effect of applying a fixed mutation must CHANGE (and, for sign-epistatic pairs, flip sign relative to fitness) when the background carries a sign-epistatic partner, while remaining invariant when the background carries an additive (non-interacting) partner. The same-mutation/different-background counterfactual, with combinatorial deep-mutational-scanning ground truth, is an exact mechanism-defining test that output-score correlation and single-mutation interpretability cannot provide.", "Related Work": "Recent work shows PLM OUTPUT scores reflect epistasis correlationally: 'PLMs Capture Structural and Functional Epistasis in a Zero-Shot Setting' (2025) finds epistasis emerges in ESM scores, with raw scores aligning to contacts and a nonlinear rescaling shifting toward functional couplings. Epistasis theory establishes SIGN epistasis - where a mutation's effect reverses sign with background - as the mechanistically-defining, hardest, and rarest form ('Mechanistic causes of sign epistasis' 2024; 'Simple sign epistasis and evolutionary detours' 2026; 'The simplicity of protein sequence-function relationships' 2023 and its rebuttal 2024, which agree additive+pairwise terms dominate but that specific interactions are the crux). PLM interpretability (InterPLM, ProtoMech cross-layer transcoders, BERTology attention studies, SAE low-N design, CB-pLM) recovers LOCAL, context-independent features (binding sites, contacts, motifs) and validates against marginal annotations. Crucially, no work tests whether a PLM's INTERNAL representation encodes the CONTEXT-DEPENDENCE that defines epistasis - i.e., whether the internal effect of a fixed mutation changes with background - nor uses a same-mutation/sign-epistatic-vs-additive-background counterfactual to separate context-dependent (mechanistic) from additive (context-free) representation. Because output scores can capture epistasis without the representation encoding context-dependence per se, and because prior interpretability probes local features only, this representational, causal, sign-flip counterfactual is the novel, mechanism-defining contribution.", "Abstract": "Epistasis - the dependence of a mutation's effect on its genetic background - is the phenomenon that makes protein sequence-function landscapes hard, and its sharpest form is sign epistasis, in which a mutation's effect fully reverses from beneficial to deleterious (or vice versa) when a partner mutation is present. Protein language models (PLMs) are known to reflect epistasis in their output scores and to encode local structural features, but whether their internal representations encode the defining property of epistasis - context-dependence itself - has never been tested. We propose a mechanism-defining representational test. For a fixed point mutation, we measure its induced change in ESM-2's internal representations when applied to two matched backgrounds drawn from combinatorial deep-mutational-scanning (DMS) data: a sign-epistatic background (a partner mutation that reverses the fitness sign of our mutation) and an additive background (a partner mutation whose combined effect is the sum of singles, i.e. no interaction), matched on structural/sequence distance. If the PLM represents epistasis mechanistically, the internal mutation-effect vector must change substantially - and align with the fitness sign flip - across the sign-epistatic background, while remaining near-invariant across the additive background; a context-free additive representation shows equal, background-independent effects. We further test whether a recoverable 'context-dependence' direction causally tracks the true sign flip and whether internal context-sensitivity predicts where the model mispredicts combinatorial fitness. Metrics quantify a context-flip asymmetry, a sign-alignment index, and an epistasis failure predictor. Either outcome sharpens whether PLMs, now central to variant-effect prediction and protein design, represent the interactions that dominate real fitness landscapes.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Use ESM-2 (150M and 650M; 3B as compute allows), inference-only plus small probes. Assemble combinatorial deep-mutational-scanning datasets that measure all single and double (and where available higher-order) mutants so ground-truth pairwise epistasis and sign can be computed exactly: GB1, avGFP, PABP/RRM, beta-lactamase, and datasets curated in ProteinGym's multi-mutant tracks and the 20 combinatorial landscapes reanalyzed in the reference-free-analysis literature. For every mutation pair (m1, m2), compute the single effects, the double effect, the pairwise epistasis term, and classify the pair as additive (epistasis ~ 0), magnitude epistasis, or sign epistasis (m1's fitness sign reverses in the presence of m2).", "2. Internal mutation-effect vector: Define the representational effect of mutation m in background B as delta(m|B) = repr(B+m) - repr(B), the per-residue and pooled change in ESM-2 activations (per layer). Confirm baseline that delta(m|wildtype) correlates with single-mutant fitness/DMS scores (replicating known decodability) and select best layers.", "3. Same-mutation/different-background counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For a fixed mutation m1, compare its internal effect across two matched backgrounds: (a) sign-epistatic background wildtype+m2_sign (where m2 reverses m1's fitness sign) and (b) additive control background wildtype+m2_add (where m1 and m2 do not interact), with m2_sign and m2_add matched on sequence separation from m1 and on 3D distance. Measure how much delta(m1|.) changes between backgrounds. Primary metric: Context-Flip Asymmetry = ||delta(m1|sign-epistatic bg) - delta(m1|wildtype)|| / ||delta(m1|additive bg) - delta(m1|wildtype)||. A mechanistic (context-dependent) representation gives asymmetry >> 1 (the effect changes specifically for the interacting background); a context-free additive representation gives asymmetry ~ 1.", "4. Sign-alignment test: Decode a scalar fitness-effect readout from delta(m1|B) (via a linear probe trained on single-mutant fitness). Test whether this readout's SIGN flips across the sign-epistatic background in the direction that matches the true fitness sign reversal, while staying the same sign across the additive background. Metric: Sign-Alignment Index = fraction of sign-epistatic pairs for which the internal effect readout reverses sign consistently with the measured fitness reversal, versus the additive-control rate. A mechanistic representation shows high sign-alignment for sign-epistatic pairs and near-zero flip rate for additive controls (a double dissociation).", "5. Dose-response to epistasis magnitude: Across all pairs, test whether the change in internal mutation-effect ||delta(m|bg) - delta(m|wildtype)|| scales monotonically with the true pairwise epistasis magnitude (from DMS), rather than with mere sequence/3D distance. Metric: partial Spearman correlation between internal effect-change and true epistasis, controlling for sequence separation and 3D distance; a distance-only representation shows no residual correlation.", "6. Contact vs functional-coupling dissection: The output-score literature finds raw PLM signal aligns with contacts while functional couplings appear after nonlinear rescaling. Test at the REPRESENTATION level whether context-dependence for spatially-contacting epistatic pairs and for distant functionally-coupled epistatic pairs is carried in different layers/components, and which dominates. Metric: layerwise context-flip asymmetry split by contact vs long-range epistatic pairs.", "7. Causal context-dependence steering: Recover a 'context-dependence' direction as the delta-representation between delta(m|sign-epistatic bg) and delta(m|wildtype) aggregated across pairs (or a supervised probe direction). Steer this direction with matched magnitude at the relevant residues and test whether ESM-2's masked-prediction / variant-effect readout for m shifts toward the epistasis-consistent (sign-flipped) value, while matched-norm random and distance-matched control directions do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of context direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "8. Epistasis failure prediction: Test whether an internal context-sensitivity score (per-pair context-flip asymmetry or sign-alignment confidence) predicts where ESM-2's zero-shot double-mutant fitness prediction is wrong (especially the sign-epistatic cases it should miss if it is additive). Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error double mutants from the internal signal, vs output-confidence and additive-baseline (sum-of-singles) predictors.", "9. Layer/scale and brackets: Report where context-dependence emerges across layers and how it changes from 150M to 650M to 3B. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) an explicit pairwise-epistasis model (e.g., a fitted reference-free / GEMME-style epistatic model) that encodes interactions by construction, and (lower bound) an additive sum-of-single-mutant-effects model that has no context-dependence, placing ESM-2 on the additive-to-epistatic spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Epistasis magnitude correlates with 3D contact and sequence proximity, so context-dependence could partly reflect distance rather than genuine interaction encoding; we mitigate with distance-matched additive control backgrounds, partial correlations controlling for distance, and the sign-alignment double dissociation (which distance alone cannot produce).", "Combinatorial DMS with true double/higher-order measurements is limited to a modest set of proteins, constraining sample size and the number of clean sign-epistatic pairs (which are rare); we pool across GB1, avGFP, RRM, beta-lactamase and ProteinGym multi-mutant sets and report statistics accounting for small n, emphasizing sign-epistatic pairs as the diagnostic subset.", "DMS measurements are noisy and reference-based epistasis estimates can inflate apparent interactions; we use reference-free / global-nonlinearity-corrected epistasis estimates where possible and restrict decisive tests to high-confidence, replicate-supported pairs, reporting sensitivity to the epistasis threshold.", "Applying two mutations to build the background may push sequences off the model's training distribution; but double mutants near wildtype are common and mild, and we bound the number and severity of background mutations and report OOD diagnostics, with the additive-background control sharing any generic OOD effect.", "Linear probes and delta-representations recovering a context-dependence direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/distance-matched controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we report aggregate-direction results.", "A negative result (ESM-2 represents mutation effects additively and shows no background-specific sign flip, i.e. context-flip asymmetry ~ 1) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting PLMs for combinatorial variant-effect prediction and multi-mutant protein design, where sign epistasis causes catastrophic failures; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining, representational test of whether PLMs encode the context-dependence that defines epistasis.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained ESM-2 plus small probes and public combinatorial DMS datasets, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'sign_epistasis_context_flip_probe', 'Title': "Does the Mutation's Meaning Depend on Its Neighbors? Testing Whether Protein Language Models Internally Represent Context-Dependent Sign Epistasis, Not Just Additive Effects", 'Short Hypothesis': "The mechanism-defining signature of epistasis is context-dependence: a mutation's fitness effect is not fixed but depends on the genetic background, and in sign epistasis the effect fully REVERSES (beneficial<->deleterious) when a partner mutation is present. If a protein language model internally represents this mechanism rather than a context-independent additive effect, then the internal representational effect of applying a fixed mutation must CHANGE (and, for sign-epistatic pairs, flip sign relative to fitness) when the background carries a sign-epistatic partner, while remaining invariant when the background carries an additive (non-interacting) partner. The same-mutation/different-background counterfactual, with combinatorial deep-mutational-scanning ground truth, is an exact mechanism-defining test that output-score correlation and single-mutation interpretability cannot provide.", 'Related Work': "Recent work shows PLM OUTPUT scores reflect epistasis correlationally: 'PLMs Capture Structural and Functional Epistasis in a Zero-Shot Setting' (2025) finds epistasis emerges in ESM scores, with raw scores aligning to contacts and a nonlinear rescaling shifting toward functional couplings. Epistasis theory establishes SIGN epistasis - where a mutation's effect reverses sign with background - as the mechanistically-defining, hardest, and rarest form ('Mechanistic causes of sign epistasis' 2024; 'Simple sign epistasis and evolutionary detours' 2026; 'The simplicity of protein sequence-function relationships' 2023 and its rebuttal 2024, which agree additive+pairwise terms dominate but that specific interactions are the crux). PLM interpretability (InterPLM, ProtoMech cross-layer transcoders, BERTology attention studies, SAE low-N design, CB-pLM) recovers LOCAL, context-independent features (binding sites, contacts, motifs) and validates against marginal annotations. Crucially, no work tests whether a PLM's INTERNAL representation encodes the CONTEXT-DEPENDENCE that defines epistasis - i.e., whether the internal effect of a fixed mutation changes with background - nor uses a same-mutation/sign-epistatic-vs-additive-background counterfactual to separate context-dependent (mechanistic) from additive (context-free) representation. Because output scores can capture epistasis without the representation encoding context-dependence per se, and because prior interpretability probes local features only, this representational, causal, sign-flip counterfactual is the novel, mechanism-defining contribution.", 'Abstract': "Epistasis - the dependence of a mutation's effect on its genetic background - is the phenomenon that makes protein sequence-function landscapes hard, and its sharpest form is sign epistasis, in which a mutation's effect fully reverses from beneficial to deleterious (or vice versa) when a partner mutation is present. Protein language models (PLMs) are known to reflect epistasis in their output scores and to encode local structural features, but whether their internal representations encode the defining property of epistasis - context-dependence itself - has never been tested. We propose a mechanism-defining representational test. For a fixed point mutation, we measure its induced change in ESM-2's internal representations when applied to two matched backgrounds drawn from combinatorial deep-mutational-scanning (DMS) data: a sign-epistatic background (a partner mutation that reverses the fitness sign of our mutation) and an additive background (a partner mutation whose combined effect is the sum of singles, i.e. no interaction), matched on structural/sequence distance. If the PLM represents epistasis mechanistically, the internal mutation-effect vector must change substantially - and align with the fitness sign flip - across the sign-epistatic background, while remaining near-invariant across the additive background; a context-free additive representation shows equal, background-independent effects. We further test whether a recoverable 'context-dependence' direction causally tracks the true sign flip and whether internal context-sensitivity predicts where the model mispredicts combinatorial fitness. Metrics quantify a context-flip asymmetry, a sign-alignment index, and an epistasis failure predictor. Either outcome sharpens whether PLMs, now central to variant-effect prediction and protein design, represent the interactions that dominate real fitness landscapes.", 'Experiments': ["1. Models and data: Use ESM-2 (150M and 650M; 3B as compute allows), inference-only plus small probes. Assemble combinatorial deep-mutational-scanning datasets that measure all single and double (and where available higher-order) mutants so ground-truth pairwise epistasis and sign can be computed exactly: GB1, avGFP, PABP/RRM, beta-lactamase, and datasets curated in ProteinGym's multi-mutant tracks and the 20 combinatorial landscapes reanalyzed in the reference-free-analysis literature. For every mutation pair (m1, m2), compute the single effects, the double effect, the pairwise epistasis term, and classify the pair as additive (epistasis ~ 0), magnitude epistasis, or sign epistasis (m1's fitness sign reverses in the presence of m2).", '2. Internal mutation-effect vector: Define the representational effect of mutation m in background B as delta(m|B) = repr(B+m) - repr(B), the per-residue and pooled change in ESM-2 activations (per layer). Confirm baseline that delta(m|wildtype) correlates with single-mutant fitness/DMS scores (replicating known decodability) and select best layers.', "3. Same-mutation/different-background counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For a fixed mutation m1, compare its internal effect across two matched backgrounds: (a) sign-epistatic background wildtype+m2_sign (where m2 reverses m1's fitness sign) and (b) additive control background wildtype+m2_add (where m1 and m2 do not interact), with m2_sign and m2_add matched on sequence separation from m1 and on 3D distance. Measure how much delta(m1|.) changes between backgrounds. Primary metric: Context-Flip Asymmetry = ||delta(m1|sign-epistatic bg) - delta(m1|wildtype)|| / ||delta(m1|additive bg) - delta(m1|wildtype)||. A mechanistic (context-dependent) representation gives asymmetry >> 1 (the effect changes specifically for the interacting background); a context-free additive representation gives asymmetry ~ 1.", "4. Sign-alignment test: Decode a scalar fitness-effect readout from delta(m1|B) (via a linear probe trained on single-mutant fitness). Test whether this readout's SIGN flips across the sign-epistatic background in the direction that matches the true fitness sign reversal, while staying the same sign across the additive background. Metric: Sign-Alignment Index = fraction of sign-epistatic pairs for which the internal effect readout reverses sign consistently with the measured fitness reversal, versus the additive-control rate. A mechanistic representation shows high sign-alignment for sign-epistatic pairs and near-zero flip rate for additive controls (a double dissociation).", '5. Dose-response to epistasis magnitude: Across all pairs, test whether the change in internal mutation-effect ||delta(m|bg) - delta(m|wildtype)|| scales monotonically with the true pairwise epistasis magnitude (from DMS), rather than with mere sequence/3D distance. Metric: partial Spearman correlation between internal effect-change and true epistasis, controlling for sequence separation and 3D distance; a distance-only representation shows no residual correlation.', '6. Contact vs functional-coupling dissection: The output-score literature finds raw PLM signal aligns with contacts while functional couplings appear after nonlinear rescaling. Test at the REPRESENTATION level whether context-dependence for spatially-contacting epistatic pairs and for distant functionally-coupled epistatic pairs is carried in different layers/components, and which dominates. Metric: layerwise context-flip asymmetry split by contact vs long-range epistatic pairs.', "7. Causal context-dependence steering: Recover a 'context-dependence' direction as the delta-representation between delta(m|sign-epistatic bg) and delta(m|wildtype) aggregated across pairs (or a supervised probe direction). Steer this direction with matched magnitude at the relevant residues and test whether ESM-2's masked-prediction / variant-effect readout for m shifts toward the epistasis-consistent (sign-flipped) value, while matched-norm random and distance-matched control directions do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of context direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "8. Epistasis failure prediction: Test whether an internal context-sensitivity score (per-pair context-flip asymmetry or sign-alignment confidence) predicts where ESM-2's zero-shot double-mutant fitness prediction is wrong (especially the sign-epistatic cases it should miss if it is additive). Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error double mutants from the internal signal, vs output-confidence and additive-baseline (sum-of-singles) predictors.", '9. Layer/scale and brackets: Report where context-dependence emerges across layers and how it changes from 150M to 650M to 3B. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) an explicit pairwise-epistasis model (e.g., a fitted reference-free / GEMME-style epistatic model) that encodes interactions by construction, and (lower bound) an additive sum-of-single-mutant-effects model that has no context-dependence, placing ESM-2 on the additive-to-epistatic spectrum.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Epistasis magnitude correlates with 3D contact and sequence proximity, so context-dependence could partly reflect distance rather than genuine interaction encoding; we mitigate with distance-matched additive control backgrounds, partial correlations controlling for distance, and the sign-alignment double dissociation (which distance alone cannot produce).', 'Combinatorial DMS with true double/higher-order measurements is limited to a modest set of proteins, constraining sample size and the number of clean sign-epistatic pairs (which are rare); we pool across GB1, avGFP, RRM, beta-lactamase and ProteinGym multi-mutant sets and report statistics accounting for small n, emphasizing sign-epistatic pairs as the diagnostic subset.', 'DMS measurements are noisy and reference-based epistasis estimates can inflate apparent interactions; we use reference-free / global-nonlinearity-corrected epistasis estimates where possible and restrict decisive tests to high-confidence, replicate-supported pairs, reporting sensitivity to the epistasis threshold.', "Applying two mutations to build the background may push sequences off the model's training distribution; but double mutants near wildtype are common and mild, and we bound the number and severity of background mutations and report OOD diagnostics, with the additive-background control sharing any generic OOD effect.", 'Linear probes and delta-representations recovering a context-dependence direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/distance-matched controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we report aggregate-direction results.', 'A negative result (ESM-2 represents mutation effects additively and shows no background-specific sign flip, i.e. context-flip asymmetry ~ 1) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting PLMs for combinatorial variant-effect prediction and multi-mutant protein design, where sign epistasis causes catastrophic failures; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining, representational test of whether PLMs encode the context-dependence that defines epistasis.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained ESM-2 plus small probes and public combinatorial DMS datasets, keeping compute at academic scale.']}

