Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "temporal_directionality_ehr_probe", "Title": "A Then B, Not B Then A: Testing Whether EHR Foundation Models Represent the Temporal Directionality of Disease Progression, Not Just Symmetric Co-occurrence", "Short Hypothesis": "Disease progression is a directed, temporally asymmetric mechanism: the trajectory diabetes -> nephropathy -> renal failure is causal and irreversible, whereas its reverse is clinically nonsensical, and this directionality (not mere co-occurrence) is what determines prognosis and treatment. We hypothesize that if EHR foundation models represent the progression mechanism rather than symmetric co-occurrence statistics, then (a) the temporal direction of a diagnosis pair (A-before-B vs B-before-A) is decodable from internal representations beyond what unordered co-occurrence predicts, (b) an exact sequence-reversal counterfactual - reversing only the order of two events while holding their identities, times-between, and all else fixed - flips the internal risk/prognosis signal for directed (progression) pairs but not for order-invariant (comorbidity) pairs, and (c) steering a recovered 'progression-direction' axis causally shifts downstream risk predictions. The reversal counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that predictive accuracy and attention-pathway alignment cannot provide.", "Related Work": "EHR foundation models built on next-event prediction (NEP EHR FM 2025; Sequential Pattern Transformer 2026) report attention patterns that 'align with known disease pathways,' and trajectory-mining work (All of Us ordered-sequence discovery 2025; directed hypergraphs of multimorbidity 2023; THCM-CAL 2025) shows ordered A->B->C sequences carry risk beyond bag-of-codes. Clinical causal-discovery methods (EHR causal structure learning 2021; LLM edge-directionality in NSCLC 2023) and symptom-causality probes (CausaLM/TReATE for chest pain 2025) estimate causal effects at the output level. Crucially, the systematic single-cell FM evaluation (2026) and 'Medical Interpretability and Knowledge Maps' (2025) show these models' internals often capture co-occurrence rather than causal/mechanistic structure, and that disease-progression representations can be circular/non-monotonic. But no work tests whether an EHR foundation model's INTERNAL representation encodes the TEMPORAL DIRECTIONALITY (A-before-B vs B-before-A asymmetry) that defines progression, whether that directionality is causally load-bearing, or uses an exact sequence-reversal counterfactual to separate directed progression from symmetric comorbidity. Because directed pairs and mere comorbid pairs both show high co-occurrence, predictive benchmarks and attention-pathway alignment cannot arbitrate this. Our directionality-beyond-co-occurrence probe, sequence-reversal counterfactual, and causal progression-direction steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, transferred into a safety-critical clinical setting where getting the arrow backwards inverts prognosis.", "Abstract": "Electronic health record (EHR) foundation models trained by next-event prediction are increasingly used to forecast disease progression and clinical risk, and their attention patterns reportedly align with known disease pathways. But a pathway is not merely a set of co-occurring diagnoses: it is a directed, temporally asymmetric mechanism, where diabetes preceding kidney failure carries a prognosis that its reverse does not. Because predictive accuracy and attention-alignment cannot distinguish encoding this directionality from encoding symmetric co-occurrence, whether these models represent the progression mechanism is untested. We propose the first mechanism-defining test. Using public EHR foundation models trained on MIMIC-III/IV and, where available, larger event-sequence models, we ask three questions. First, a directionality-beyond-co-occurrence probe: is the temporal order of a diagnosis pair (A-before-B vs B-before-A) decodable from internal representations beyond what unordered co-occurrence predicts? Second, and most decisively, a sequence-reversal counterfactual: for matched patient trajectories we reverse only the order of two events - holding their identities, inter-event times, and surrounding context fixed - and test whether the internal risk/prognosis signal flips for directed progression pairs (e.g., nephropathy->renal-failure) but stays stable for order-invariant comorbidity pairs (e.g., hypertension and hyperlipidemia); a co-occurrence-statistics model responds identically to both. Third, causal steering along a recovered progression-direction axis should shift downstream risk. Metrics quantify a directionality-beyond-co-occurrence index, a reversal-vs-comorbidity causal asymmetry, and a mechanism-based failure predictor. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed clinical models understand which way the arrow points.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load public EHR foundation models with accessible weights and per-event representations - a next-event-prediction transformer trained on MIMIC-III/IV (e.g., an NEP-style or event-sequence GPT), and where available a larger clinical event-sequence model; also include a strong bag-of-codes baseline model. Assemble timestamped diagnosis/procedure/medication event sequences from MIMIC-III and MIMIC-IV. Curate two pair classes from clinical knowledge and validated trajectory-mining resources (All of Us ordered sequences, directed multimorbidity hypergraphs): (a) DIRECTED progression pairs A->B with a clinically established, largely irreversible order (e.g., diabetic nephropathy -> end-stage renal failure; hypertension -> heart failure; adenoma -> colorectal cancer), and (b) ORDER-INVARIANT comorbidity pairs that co-occur but lack a fixed causal order (e.g., hypertension & hyperlipidemia). For each pair record its class, unordered co-occurrence frequency, and typical inter-event time. Extract per-event internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode (i) event identity and (ii) the temporal order of a diagnosis pair (which came first) from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded, select best layers, and establish an unordered co-occurrence baseline (predict pair-order from co-occurrence and marginal prevalence alone).", "3. Directionality-beyond-co-occurrence probe (primary probe): Test whether the temporal ORDER of a pair is decodable BEYOND unordered co-occurrence and prevalence. Regress out the co-occurrence/prevalence-predictable component of the representation and re-decode order from the residual. Primary metric: Directionality-Beyond-Co-occurrence Index = order-classification accuracy from the model representation minus what unordered co-occurrence features achieve. A mechanism-representing model carries directional information; a co-occurrence-statistics model does not exceed the baseline. Report separately for directed vs order-invariant pairs (directed pairs should show a strong, consistent directional signal; order-invariant pairs should not, giving a double dissociation).", "4. Sequence-reversal counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For matched patient trajectories containing a target pair, construct two versions: (a) the true order A-before-B and (b) a reversed order B-before-A, holding the two event identities, the inter-event time gap, and all surrounding events fixed (swap only the two events' positions/timestamps). Feed both to the model and measure the change in its internal risk/prognosis signal (a probed risk score, or the model's next-event/outcome-prediction head, e.g., predicted mortality or downstream complication). Metric: Reversal-vs-Comorbidity Causal Asymmetry = |signal change under reversal for directed progression pairs| / |signal change under reversal for order-invariant comorbidity pairs|. A mechanistic representation gives asymmetry >> 1 (reversing a genuine progression pair changes prognosis - often nonsensically for the reversed direction - while reversing a comorbidity pair does little); a co-occurrence-statistics representation gives asymmetry ~ 1 (order does not matter because only presence is encoded).", "5. Prognosis-sign consistency: For directed pairs, test whether the reversal induces the CLINICALLY-CORRECT change - the true forward order A->B should carry the elevated-risk prognosis, while the reversed (implausible) order should not, or should read as anomalous. Metric: fraction of directed pairs where the model's forward-order risk exceeds reversed-order risk, versus the near-zero difference expected for comorbidity pairs; this is the double dissociation that separates mechanism from statistics.", "6. Inter-event-time dose-response: Progression carries a latency structure (nephropathy precedes renal failure by years). Test whether the model's internal progression signal responds to the inter-event gap in a clinically plausible way (e.g., implausibly short gaps for a slow progression weaken the signal), rather than being time-agnostic. Metric: correlation between internal progression-signal strength and clinically-plausible time gaps; shuffled-time controls.", "7. Causal progression-direction steering: Recover a 'progression-direction' axis (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between forward-order and reversed-order versions of directed pairs). Steer this axis with matched magnitude and test whether the model's downstream risk readout shifts in the progression-consistent direction, while matched-norm random directions and a co-occurrence-magnitude direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of progression direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "8. Failure-prediction and safety link: Test whether an internal 'directionality-representation strength' score (directionality-beyond-co-occurrence confidence / reversal sensitivity) predicts, per patient, where the model's risk/progression prediction is wrong - especially in atypical trajectories where diagnoses appear out of the usual causal order (data-entry order artifacts, reverse-causation cases). Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error cases from the internal signal, versus output-confidence and co-occurrence-only baselines.", "9. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where temporal-directionality structure emerges across depth and whether recovered progression-direction subspaces align across the EHR foundation models (CKA), assessing whether directionality is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) an explicitly ordered trajectory / directed-graph model that encodes direction by construction, and (lower bound) a bag-of-codes model with no order access, placing EHR FMs on the co-occurrence-to-directional-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Recorded event order in EHRs is a noisy proxy for true clinical onset order (billing/coding order, batched diagnoses, same-admission codes with identical timestamps); we mitigate by restricting directed pairs to those with large, clinically meaningful time gaps across separate admissions, using validated ordered-trajectory resources, and reporting sensitivity to timestamp-quality thresholds.", "Directed progression pairs and order-invariant comorbidity pairs both have high co-occurrence, making the directionality-beyond-co-occurrence separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with co-occurrence residualization, the sequence-reversal counterfactual (which holds identities and timing fixed and varies only order), and the directed-vs-comorbidity double dissociation.", "The reversal counterfactual creates clinically implausible trajectories that may fall off the model's training distribution, confounding the reversal response with generic out-of-distribution behavior; we mitigate by comparing directed-pair reversals against comorbidity-pair reversals of matched implausibility/edit-magnitude (both are edited, so any generic OOD effect is shared) and reporting edit-magnitude sensitivity.", "Some EHR foundation models tokenize events without fine-grained timestamps or use position-invariant pooling, limiting access to order; we document each model's temporal encoding, prioritize models that ingest ordered/timestamped sequences, and treat pooling-invariant models as an informative lower bracket that by construction cannot represent direction.", "Ground-truth causal direction for some pairs is debated or bidirectional; we restrict decisive tests to well-established, largely irreversible progressions and report sensitivity to pair-confidence thresholds.", "Linear probes recovering a progression-direction axis do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/co-occurrence-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and delta-representations.", "A negative result (models encode symmetric co-occurrence and respond identically to forward vs reversed progression, failing the reversal counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant - it would warn that deployed clinical risk models may not distinguish cause from consequence, risking inverted prognoses and inappropriate interventions for atypically-ordered patients; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether EHR foundation models represent the temporal directionality of disease progression.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public EHR datasets (MIMIC-III/IV) with validated trajectory resources, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'temporal_directionality_ehr_probe', 'Title': 'A Then B, Not B Then A: Testing Whether EHR Foundation Models Represent the Temporal Directionality of Disease Progression, Not Just Symmetric Co-occurrence', 'Short Hypothesis': "Disease progression is a directed, temporally asymmetric mechanism: the trajectory diabetes -> nephropathy -> renal failure is causal and irreversible, whereas its reverse is clinically nonsensical, and this directionality (not mere co-occurrence) is what determines prognosis and treatment. We hypothesize that if EHR foundation models represent the progression mechanism rather than symmetric co-occurrence statistics, then (a) the temporal direction of a diagnosis pair (A-before-B vs B-before-A) is decodable from internal representations beyond what unordered co-occurrence predicts, (b) an exact sequence-reversal counterfactual - reversing only the order of two events while holding their identities, times-between, and all else fixed - flips the internal risk/prognosis signal for directed (progression) pairs but not for order-invariant (comorbidity) pairs, and (c) steering a recovered 'progression-direction' axis causally shifts downstream risk predictions. The reversal counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that predictive accuracy and attention-pathway alignment cannot provide.", 'Related Work': "EHR foundation models built on next-event prediction (NEP EHR FM 2025; Sequential Pattern Transformer 2026) report attention patterns that 'align with known disease pathways,' and trajectory-mining work (All of Us ordered-sequence discovery 2025; directed hypergraphs of multimorbidity 2023; THCM-CAL 2025) shows ordered A->B->C sequences carry risk beyond bag-of-codes. Clinical causal-discovery methods (EHR causal structure learning 2021; LLM edge-directionality in NSCLC 2023) and symptom-causality probes (CausaLM/TReATE for chest pain 2025) estimate causal effects at the output level. Crucially, the systematic single-cell FM evaluation (2026) and 'Medical Interpretability and Knowledge Maps' (2025) show these models' internals often capture co-occurrence rather than causal/mechanistic structure, and that disease-progression representations can be circular/non-monotonic. But no work tests whether an EHR foundation model's INTERNAL representation encodes the TEMPORAL DIRECTIONALITY (A-before-B vs B-before-A asymmetry) that defines progression, whether that directionality is causally load-bearing, or uses an exact sequence-reversal counterfactual to separate directed progression from symmetric comorbidity. Because directed pairs and mere comorbid pairs both show high co-occurrence, predictive benchmarks and attention-pathway alignment cannot arbitrate this. Our directionality-beyond-co-occurrence probe, sequence-reversal counterfactual, and causal progression-direction steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, transferred into a safety-critical clinical setting where getting the arrow backwards inverts prognosis.", 'Abstract': 'Electronic health record (EHR) foundation models trained by next-event prediction are increasingly used to forecast disease progression and clinical risk, and their attention patterns reportedly align with known disease pathways. But a pathway is not merely a set of co-occurring diagnoses: it is a directed, temporally asymmetric mechanism, where diabetes preceding kidney failure carries a prognosis that its reverse does not. Because predictive accuracy and attention-alignment cannot distinguish encoding this directionality from encoding symmetric co-occurrence, whether these models represent the progression mechanism is untested. We propose the first mechanism-defining test. Using public EHR foundation models trained on MIMIC-III/IV and, where available, larger event-sequence models, we ask three questions. First, a directionality-beyond-co-occurrence probe: is the temporal order of a diagnosis pair (A-before-B vs B-before-A) decodable from internal representations beyond what unordered co-occurrence predicts? Second, and most decisively, a sequence-reversal counterfactual: for matched patient trajectories we reverse only the order of two events - holding their identities, inter-event times, and surrounding context fixed - and test whether the internal risk/prognosis signal flips for directed progression pairs (e.g., nephropathy->renal-failure) but stays stable for order-invariant comorbidity pairs (e.g., hypertension and hyperlipidemia); a co-occurrence-statistics model responds identically to both. Third, causal steering along a recovered progression-direction axis should shift downstream risk. Metrics quantify a directionality-beyond-co-occurrence index, a reversal-vs-comorbidity causal asymmetry, and a mechanism-based failure predictor. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed clinical models understand which way the arrow points.', 'Experiments': ['1. Models and data: Load public EHR foundation models with accessible weights and per-event representations - a next-event-prediction transformer trained on MIMIC-III/IV (e.g., an NEP-style or event-sequence GPT), and where available a larger clinical event-sequence model; also include a strong bag-of-codes baseline model. Assemble timestamped diagnosis/procedure/medication event sequences from MIMIC-III and MIMIC-IV. Curate two pair classes from clinical knowledge and validated trajectory-mining resources (All of Us ordered sequences, directed multimorbidity hypergraphs): (a) DIRECTED progression pairs A->B with a clinically established, largely irreversible order (e.g., diabetic nephropathy -> end-stage renal failure; hypertension -> heart failure; adenoma -> colorectal cancer), and (b) ORDER-INVARIANT comorbidity pairs that co-occur but lack a fixed causal order (e.g., hypertension & hyperlipidemia). For each pair record its class, unordered co-occurrence frequency, and typical inter-event time. Extract per-event internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.', '2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode (i) event identity and (ii) the temporal order of a diagnosis pair (which came first) from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded, select best layers, and establish an unordered co-occurrence baseline (predict pair-order from co-occurrence and marginal prevalence alone).', '3. Directionality-beyond-co-occurrence probe (primary probe): Test whether the temporal ORDER of a pair is decodable BEYOND unordered co-occurrence and prevalence. Regress out the co-occurrence/prevalence-predictable component of the representation and re-decode order from the residual. Primary metric: Directionality-Beyond-Co-occurrence Index = order-classification accuracy from the model representation minus what unordered co-occurrence features achieve. A mechanism-representing model carries directional information; a co-occurrence-statistics model does not exceed the baseline. Report separately for directed vs order-invariant pairs (directed pairs should show a strong, consistent directional signal; order-invariant pairs should not, giving a double dissociation).', "4. Sequence-reversal counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For matched patient trajectories containing a target pair, construct two versions: (a) the true order A-before-B and (b) a reversed order B-before-A, holding the two event identities, the inter-event time gap, and all surrounding events fixed (swap only the two events' positions/timestamps). Feed both to the model and measure the change in its internal risk/prognosis signal (a probed risk score, or the model's next-event/outcome-prediction head, e.g., predicted mortality or downstream complication). Metric: Reversal-vs-Comorbidity Causal Asymmetry = |signal change under reversal for directed progression pairs| / |signal change under reversal for order-invariant comorbidity pairs|. A mechanistic representation gives asymmetry >> 1 (reversing a genuine progression pair changes prognosis - often nonsensically for the reversed direction - while reversing a comorbidity pair does little); a co-occurrence-statistics representation gives asymmetry ~ 1 (order does not matter because only presence is encoded).", "5. Prognosis-sign consistency: For directed pairs, test whether the reversal induces the CLINICALLY-CORRECT change - the true forward order A->B should carry the elevated-risk prognosis, while the reversed (implausible) order should not, or should read as anomalous. Metric: fraction of directed pairs where the model's forward-order risk exceeds reversed-order risk, versus the near-zero difference expected for comorbidity pairs; this is the double dissociation that separates mechanism from statistics.", "6. Inter-event-time dose-response: Progression carries a latency structure (nephropathy precedes renal failure by years). Test whether the model's internal progression signal responds to the inter-event gap in a clinically plausible way (e.g., implausibly short gaps for a slow progression weaken the signal), rather than being time-agnostic. Metric: correlation between internal progression-signal strength and clinically-plausible time gaps; shuffled-time controls.", "7. Causal progression-direction steering: Recover a 'progression-direction' axis (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between forward-order and reversed-order versions of directed pairs). Steer this axis with matched magnitude and test whether the model's downstream risk readout shifts in the progression-consistent direction, while matched-norm random directions and a co-occurrence-magnitude direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of progression direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "8. Failure-prediction and safety link: Test whether an internal 'directionality-representation strength' score (directionality-beyond-co-occurrence confidence / reversal sensitivity) predicts, per patient, where the model's risk/progression prediction is wrong - especially in atypical trajectories where diagnoses appear out of the usual causal order (data-entry order artifacts, reverse-causation cases). Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error cases from the internal signal, versus output-confidence and co-occurrence-only baselines.", '9. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where temporal-directionality structure emerges across depth and whether recovered progression-direction subspaces align across the EHR foundation models (CKA), assessing whether directionality is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) an explicitly ordered trajectory / directed-graph model that encodes direction by construction, and (lower bound) a bag-of-codes model with no order access, placing EHR FMs on the co-occurrence-to-directional-mechanism spectrum.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Recorded event order in EHRs is a noisy proxy for true clinical onset order (billing/coding order, batched diagnoses, same-admission codes with identical timestamps); we mitigate by restricting directed pairs to those with large, clinically meaningful time gaps across separate admissions, using validated ordered-trajectory resources, and reporting sensitivity to timestamp-quality thresholds.', 'Directed progression pairs and order-invariant comorbidity pairs both have high co-occurrence, making the directionality-beyond-co-occurrence separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with co-occurrence residualization, the sequence-reversal counterfactual (which holds identities and timing fixed and varies only order), and the directed-vs-comorbidity double dissociation.', "The reversal counterfactual creates clinically implausible trajectories that may fall off the model's training distribution, confounding the reversal response with generic out-of-distribution behavior; we mitigate by comparing directed-pair reversals against comorbidity-pair reversals of matched implausibility/edit-magnitude (both are edited, so any generic OOD effect is shared) and reporting edit-magnitude sensitivity.", "Some EHR foundation models tokenize events without fine-grained timestamps or use position-invariant pooling, limiting access to order; we document each model's temporal encoding, prioritize models that ingest ordered/timestamped sequences, and treat pooling-invariant models as an informative lower bracket that by construction cannot represent direction.", 'Ground-truth causal direction for some pairs is debated or bidirectional; we restrict decisive tests to well-established, largely irreversible progressions and report sensitivity to pair-confidence thresholds.', 'Linear probes recovering a progression-direction axis do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/co-occurrence-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and delta-representations.', 'A negative result (models encode symmetric co-occurrence and respond identically to forward vs reversed progression, failing the reversal counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant - it would warn that deployed clinical risk models may not distinguish cause from consequence, risking inverted prognoses and inappropriate interventions for atypically-ordered patients; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether EHR foundation models represent the temporal directionality of disease progression.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public EHR datasets (MIMIC-III/IV) with validated trajectory resources, keeping compute at academic scale.']}

