Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "nernst_overpotential_decomposition_probe", "Title": "Voltage Is Not Charge: Testing Whether Battery Foundation Models Decompose Terminal Voltage into Thermodynamic Nernst OCV and Kinetic Overpotential, Not Just Raw Voltage Statistics", "Short Hypothesis": "A battery's terminal voltage is set by an exactly-known decomposition: V = OCV(SOC) - overpotential(current), where the open-circuit voltage is a purely thermodynamic, current-independent function of state-of-charge (Nernst equation) and the overpotential is a kinetic, current-dependent loss (Butler-Volmer / diffusion). These are physically independent contributions: the same terminal voltage can correspond to very different SOCs depending on the load current, which is exactly why SOC estimation fails under high current. We hypothesize that if a battery/time-series foundation model represents this mechanism rather than raw voltage statistics, then (a) SOC (the thermodynamic OCV component) is decodable from internal representations beyond what instantaneous terminal voltage predicts, along an axis distinct from overpotential, (b) a current-holding vs SOC-holding counterfactual moves the two internal signals independently, and (c) steering a recovered 'overpotential' direction reshapes the voltage-current relationship without changing the SOC readout. The load-current counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that terminal-voltage correlation cannot pass.", "Related Work": "Two lines bracket the gap. (1) Physics-informed and electrochemical battery models - ECINN (2024, embedding Nernst/Butler-Volmer/diffusion equations), single-particle-model + Nernst SOC estimators (2020), and equivalent-circuit models - BUILD the OCV/overpotential decomposition in by construction; they are judged by SOC estimation error, not by what a general model internally represents. (2) Data-driven battery foundation/time-series models (BatteryML, transformer SOC/SOH estimators, general TSFMs like Chronos/TimesFM applied to battery telemetry) predict SOC/state directly from voltage-current-temperature sequences; interpretability of such models is limited to feature-attribution over input channels. Interpretability of scientific foundation models (Physics Steering 2025, showing steerable physical-concept directions; From Neurons to Neutrons 2024) recovers and steers single physical quantities, but none tests whether a battery model internally SEPARATES the thermodynamic Nernst OCV(SOC) from the kinetic overpotential(current), whether that decomposition is causally load-bearing, or uses a load-current counterfactual to arbitrate mechanism vs voltage statistics. Because terminal voltage, SOC, and current are entangled in ordinary discharge data, correlation-based SOC benchmarks cannot separate the two contributions. Our OCV-beyond-voltage probe, current-holding counterfactual, and causal overpotential steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, in a safety-critical setting where confusing kinetic voltage sag with true depletion causes dangerous SOC misestimation.", "Abstract": "Battery management systems increasingly use machine-learning models to estimate state-of-charge (SOC) from voltage, current, and temperature telemetry, and errors are most dangerous precisely under high load, where the terminal voltage sags for kinetic reasons even though the battery is not actually depleted. The physics is exactly known: terminal voltage decomposes into a thermodynamic open-circuit voltage that depends only on SOC (the Nernst equation) and a kinetic overpotential that depends on current (Butler-Volmer and diffusion losses). These are independent contributions - the same voltage can mean very different SOCs at different currents - making batteries an ideal, mechanism-defining testbed for whether foundation models learn the electrochemical mechanism or merely voltage statistics. We probe pretrained battery/time-series foundation models on public cycling datasets (e.g., NASA, Oxford, CALCE, and BatteryML benchmarks) where SOC, current, and OCV are known or computable. First, an OCV-beyond-voltage probe: is SOC (the thermodynamic component) decodable from internal representations beyond what instantaneous terminal voltage predicts, along an axis distinct from overpotential? Second, and most decisively, a current-holding counterfactual: we change the load current at fixed SOC (which changes overpotential, not OCV) versus change SOC at fixed current (which changes OCV, not overpotential), and test whether the two internal signals move independently - a mechanistic representation separates them, a voltage-statistics representation collapses them. Third, causal steering of a recovered overpotential direction should reshape the voltage-current response without shifting the SOC readout. Metrics quantify an OCV-beyond-voltage index, a thermodynamic-kinetic disentanglement ratio, and a SOC-error failure predictor. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed battery models estimate charge for the right physical reason.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load pretrained battery/time-series foundation models with public weights and usable internal representations - a battery-specific model (e.g., a transformer SOC/SOH estimator from BatteryML) and a general time-series foundation model (Chronos/TimesFM) applied to battery telemetry; include an equivalent-circuit / OCV-lookup baseline. Assemble public cycling datasets (NASA PCoE, Oxford Battery Degradation, CALCE, and BatteryML benchmark cells) providing time-series of terminal voltage, load current, temperature, and coulomb-counted SOC. Compute ground-truth OCV(SOC) curves (from low-rate/pseudo-OCV or pause-relaxation data) and, per timestep, the overpotential = terminal voltage - OCV(SOC). Extract per-timestep internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode SOC, OCV, terminal voltage, current, and overpotential from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded and select best layers. Establish an instantaneous-voltage baseline: predict SOC directly from terminal voltage alone.", "3. OCV-beyond-voltage probe (primary probe): Test whether SOC (the thermodynamic OCV component) is decodable BEYOND instantaneous terminal voltage. Regress out the terminal-voltage-predictable component of the representation and re-decode SOC from the residual. Primary metric: OCV-Beyond-Voltage Index = probe R^2 for SOC from the model representation minus what terminal voltage alone achieves. Also measure the angle between the recovered SOC/OCV direction and the recovered overpotential direction; a mechanistic representation encodes them as distinct (near-orthogonal) axes, a voltage-statistics representation collapses them.", "4. Current-holding counterfactual (primary, decisive test): Using controlled telemetry segments and synthetic sequences generated from an equivalent-circuit/single-particle model, construct two matched edits. (a) Overpotential edit: change the load current at fixed SOC (changes overpotential/terminal voltage but NOT OCV). (b) OCV edit: change SOC at fixed current (changes OCV but NOT overpotential). Feed both to the model and measure the change in the internal SOC signal and the internal overpotential signal for each edit. Metric: Thermodynamic-Kinetic Disentanglement Ratio = for the overpotential (current) edit, |change in overpotential signal| / |change in SOC signal|; and for the OCV (SOC) edit, |change in SOC signal| / |change in overpotential signal|. A mechanistic representation gives both ratios >> 1 (each edit moves its own physical axis - a double dissociation); a voltage-statistics representation gives ratios near 1 or moves SOC whenever voltage moves (the dangerous failure mode of reading current-induced voltage sag as depletion).", "5. Physics dose-response: Sweep the load current across a range at fixed SOC and test whether the model's internal overpotential signal grows with current in the physically-correct direction and magnitude (approximately following the Butler-Volmer/ohmic overpotential relationship), while its SOC readout stays constant. Metric: Spearman correlation and sign agreement between induced overpotential-signal change and the physically-predicted overpotential; SOC-readout stability across the current sweep; shuffled-current controls.", "6. Causal overpotential steering: Recover an 'overpotential/kinetic' direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between high-current and low-current states at matched SOC). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's predicted voltage-current response becomes more/less sagged WITHOUT shifting its SOC readout, while a matched SOC/OCV direction shifts SOC without changing overpotential. Controls: matched-norm random directions should do neither. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |overpotential change from kinetic steering| / |overpotential change from random steering|; cross-talk = |SOC shift from kinetic steering| (should be near zero for a disentangled representation).", "7. Safety-relevance / failure-prediction link: SOC misestimation is most dangerous under high load (kinetic voltage sag misread as depletion). Test whether an internal 'OCV/overpotential disentanglement strength' score predicts, per timestep, where the model's SOC estimate is wrong - especially high-current transients and low-temperature (high-overpotential) conditions where the voltage-only shortcut misleads. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-SOC-error timesteps from the internal signal, versus output-confidence and voltage-only baselines.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where thermodynamic vs kinetic decomposition structure emerges across depth and whether recovered OCV and overpotential subspaces align across the battery FM and general TSFM (CKA), assessing whether the electrochemical decomposition is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a physics-informed model (ECINN-style) that encodes OCV/overpotential by construction, and (lower bound) a voltage-lookup SOC estimator with no current correction, placing foundation models on the voltage-statistics-to-electrochemical-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["SOC, terminal voltage, and current are entangled in ordinary discharge data, making the OCV-beyond-voltage separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with voltage-residualization, the current-holding double-dissociation counterfactual (which holds one physical quantity fixed while varying the other), synthetic equivalent-circuit sequences giving exact independent control, and physics dose-response tests a voltage-only model cannot reproduce.", "Ground-truth OCV(SOC) and overpotential require accurate coulomb-counted SOC and low-rate OCV curves, which drift with aging and temperature and are noisy; we use well-characterized benchmark cells with pseudo-OCV/relaxation data, restrict decisive tests to cells with reliable SOC labels, and report sensitivity to OCV-curve and aging assumptions.", "The current-holding counterfactual on real telemetry is constrained by what operating points exist in the data; we supplement with synthetic sequences from a calibrated equivalent-circuit/single-particle model for exact independent manipulation, clearly reporting which results are synthetic vs real and treating cross-validation between them as key.", "Some battery/time-series foundation models take fixed input windows, normalize channels, or output only a scalar SOC, limiting fine-grained decomposition analysis; we document each model's preprocessing, prioritize models exposing per-timestep representations, probe pre-normalization activations where feasible, and treat scalar-only models as an informative lower bracket.", "Linear probes recovering OCV/overpotential directions do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering, cross-talk controls, and random-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and delta-representations.", "A negative result (models encode voltage statistics and collapse the thermodynamic-kinetic decomposition, failing the current-holding counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant - it would warn that deployed battery models may misread kinetic voltage sag as true depletion, causing dangerous SOC misestimation under high load and low temperature; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether battery foundation models decompose terminal voltage into Nernst OCV and kinetic overpotential.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public battery cycling datasets (NASA, Oxford, CALCE, BatteryML) with standard equivalent-circuit simulation, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'nernst_overpotential_decomposition_probe', 'Title': 'Voltage Is Not Charge: Testing Whether Battery Foundation Models Decompose Terminal Voltage into Thermodynamic Nernst OCV and Kinetic Overpotential, Not Just Raw Voltage Statistics', 'Short Hypothesis': "A battery's terminal voltage is set by an exactly-known decomposition: V = OCV(SOC) - overpotential(current), where the open-circuit voltage is a purely thermodynamic, current-independent function of state-of-charge (Nernst equation) and the overpotential is a kinetic, current-dependent loss (Butler-Volmer / diffusion). These are physically independent contributions: the same terminal voltage can correspond to very different SOCs depending on the load current, which is exactly why SOC estimation fails under high current. We hypothesize that if a battery/time-series foundation model represents this mechanism rather than raw voltage statistics, then (a) SOC (the thermodynamic OCV component) is decodable from internal representations beyond what instantaneous terminal voltage predicts, along an axis distinct from overpotential, (b) a current-holding vs SOC-holding counterfactual moves the two internal signals independently, and (c) steering a recovered 'overpotential' direction reshapes the voltage-current relationship without changing the SOC readout. The load-current counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that terminal-voltage correlation cannot pass.", 'Related Work': 'Two lines bracket the gap. (1) Physics-informed and electrochemical battery models - ECINN (2024, embedding Nernst/Butler-Volmer/diffusion equations), single-particle-model + Nernst SOC estimators (2020), and equivalent-circuit models - BUILD the OCV/overpotential decomposition in by construction; they are judged by SOC estimation error, not by what a general model internally represents. (2) Data-driven battery foundation/time-series models (BatteryML, transformer SOC/SOH estimators, general TSFMs like Chronos/TimesFM applied to battery telemetry) predict SOC/state directly from voltage-current-temperature sequences; interpretability of such models is limited to feature-attribution over input channels. Interpretability of scientific foundation models (Physics Steering 2025, showing steerable physical-concept directions; From Neurons to Neutrons 2024) recovers and steers single physical quantities, but none tests whether a battery model internally SEPARATES the thermodynamic Nernst OCV(SOC) from the kinetic overpotential(current), whether that decomposition is causally load-bearing, or uses a load-current counterfactual to arbitrate mechanism vs voltage statistics. Because terminal voltage, SOC, and current are entangled in ordinary discharge data, correlation-based SOC benchmarks cannot separate the two contributions. Our OCV-beyond-voltage probe, current-holding counterfactual, and causal overpotential steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, in a safety-critical setting where confusing kinetic voltage sag with true depletion causes dangerous SOC misestimation.', 'Abstract': 'Battery management systems increasingly use machine-learning models to estimate state-of-charge (SOC) from voltage, current, and temperature telemetry, and errors are most dangerous precisely under high load, where the terminal voltage sags for kinetic reasons even though the battery is not actually depleted. The physics is exactly known: terminal voltage decomposes into a thermodynamic open-circuit voltage that depends only on SOC (the Nernst equation) and a kinetic overpotential that depends on current (Butler-Volmer and diffusion losses). These are independent contributions - the same voltage can mean very different SOCs at different currents - making batteries an ideal, mechanism-defining testbed for whether foundation models learn the electrochemical mechanism or merely voltage statistics. We probe pretrained battery/time-series foundation models on public cycling datasets (e.g., NASA, Oxford, CALCE, and BatteryML benchmarks) where SOC, current, and OCV are known or computable. First, an OCV-beyond-voltage probe: is SOC (the thermodynamic component) decodable from internal representations beyond what instantaneous terminal voltage predicts, along an axis distinct from overpotential? Second, and most decisively, a current-holding counterfactual: we change the load current at fixed SOC (which changes overpotential, not OCV) versus change SOC at fixed current (which changes OCV, not overpotential), and test whether the two internal signals move independently - a mechanistic representation separates them, a voltage-statistics representation collapses them. Third, causal steering of a recovered overpotential direction should reshape the voltage-current response without shifting the SOC readout. Metrics quantify an OCV-beyond-voltage index, a thermodynamic-kinetic disentanglement ratio, and a SOC-error failure predictor. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed battery models estimate charge for the right physical reason.', 'Experiments': ['1. Models and data: Load pretrained battery/time-series foundation models with public weights and usable internal representations - a battery-specific model (e.g., a transformer SOC/SOH estimator from BatteryML) and a general time-series foundation model (Chronos/TimesFM) applied to battery telemetry; include an equivalent-circuit / OCV-lookup baseline. Assemble public cycling datasets (NASA PCoE, Oxford Battery Degradation, CALCE, and BatteryML benchmark cells) providing time-series of terminal voltage, load current, temperature, and coulomb-counted SOC. Compute ground-truth OCV(SOC) curves (from low-rate/pseudo-OCV or pause-relaxation data) and, per timestep, the overpotential = terminal voltage - OCV(SOC). Extract per-timestep internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.', '2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode SOC, OCV, terminal voltage, current, and overpotential from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded and select best layers. Establish an instantaneous-voltage baseline: predict SOC directly from terminal voltage alone.', '3. OCV-beyond-voltage probe (primary probe): Test whether SOC (the thermodynamic OCV component) is decodable BEYOND instantaneous terminal voltage. Regress out the terminal-voltage-predictable component of the representation and re-decode SOC from the residual. Primary metric: OCV-Beyond-Voltage Index = probe R^2 for SOC from the model representation minus what terminal voltage alone achieves. Also measure the angle between the recovered SOC/OCV direction and the recovered overpotential direction; a mechanistic representation encodes them as distinct (near-orthogonal) axes, a voltage-statistics representation collapses them.', '4. Current-holding counterfactual (primary, decisive test): Using controlled telemetry segments and synthetic sequences generated from an equivalent-circuit/single-particle model, construct two matched edits. (a) Overpotential edit: change the load current at fixed SOC (changes overpotential/terminal voltage but NOT OCV). (b) OCV edit: change SOC at fixed current (changes OCV but NOT overpotential). Feed both to the model and measure the change in the internal SOC signal and the internal overpotential signal for each edit. Metric: Thermodynamic-Kinetic Disentanglement Ratio = for the overpotential (current) edit, |change in overpotential signal| / |change in SOC signal|; and for the OCV (SOC) edit, |change in SOC signal| / |change in overpotential signal|. A mechanistic representation gives both ratios >> 1 (each edit moves its own physical axis - a double dissociation); a voltage-statistics representation gives ratios near 1 or moves SOC whenever voltage moves (the dangerous failure mode of reading current-induced voltage sag as depletion).', "5. Physics dose-response: Sweep the load current across a range at fixed SOC and test whether the model's internal overpotential signal grows with current in the physically-correct direction and magnitude (approximately following the Butler-Volmer/ohmic overpotential relationship), while its SOC readout stays constant. Metric: Spearman correlation and sign agreement between induced overpotential-signal change and the physically-predicted overpotential; SOC-readout stability across the current sweep; shuffled-current controls.", "6. Causal overpotential steering: Recover an 'overpotential/kinetic' direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between high-current and low-current states at matched SOC). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's predicted voltage-current response becomes more/less sagged WITHOUT shifting its SOC readout, while a matched SOC/OCV direction shifts SOC without changing overpotential. Controls: matched-norm random directions should do neither. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |overpotential change from kinetic steering| / |overpotential change from random steering|; cross-talk = |SOC shift from kinetic steering| (should be near zero for a disentangled representation).", "7. Safety-relevance / failure-prediction link: SOC misestimation is most dangerous under high load (kinetic voltage sag misread as depletion). Test whether an internal 'OCV/overpotential disentanglement strength' score predicts, per timestep, where the model's SOC estimate is wrong - especially high-current transients and low-temperature (high-overpotential) conditions where the voltage-only shortcut misleads. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-SOC-error timesteps from the internal signal, versus output-confidence and voltage-only baselines.", '8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where thermodynamic vs kinetic decomposition structure emerges across depth and whether recovered OCV and overpotential subspaces align across the battery FM and general TSFM (CKA), assessing whether the electrochemical decomposition is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a physics-informed model (ECINN-style) that encodes OCV/overpotential by construction, and (lower bound) a voltage-lookup SOC estimator with no current correction, placing foundation models on the voltage-statistics-to-electrochemical-mechanism spectrum.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['SOC, terminal voltage, and current are entangled in ordinary discharge data, making the OCV-beyond-voltage separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with voltage-residualization, the current-holding double-dissociation counterfactual (which holds one physical quantity fixed while varying the other), synthetic equivalent-circuit sequences giving exact independent control, and physics dose-response tests a voltage-only model cannot reproduce.', 'Ground-truth OCV(SOC) and overpotential require accurate coulomb-counted SOC and low-rate OCV curves, which drift with aging and temperature and are noisy; we use well-characterized benchmark cells with pseudo-OCV/relaxation data, restrict decisive tests to cells with reliable SOC labels, and report sensitivity to OCV-curve and aging assumptions.', 'The current-holding counterfactual on real telemetry is constrained by what operating points exist in the data; we supplement with synthetic sequences from a calibrated equivalent-circuit/single-particle model for exact independent manipulation, clearly reporting which results are synthetic vs real and treating cross-validation between them as key.', "Some battery/time-series foundation models take fixed input windows, normalize channels, or output only a scalar SOC, limiting fine-grained decomposition analysis; we document each model's preprocessing, prioritize models exposing per-timestep representations, probe pre-normalization activations where feasible, and treat scalar-only models as an informative lower bracket.", 'Linear probes recovering OCV/overpotential directions do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering, cross-talk controls, and random-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and delta-representations.', 'A negative result (models encode voltage statistics and collapse the thermodynamic-kinetic decomposition, failing the current-holding counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant - it would warn that deployed battery models may misread kinetic voltage sag as true depletion, causing dangerous SOC misestimation under high load and low temperature; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether battery foundation models decompose terminal voltage into Nernst OCV and kinetic overpotential.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public battery cycling datasets (NASA, Oxford, CALCE, BatteryML) with standard equivalent-circuit simulation, keeping compute at academic scale.']}

