Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "teleconnection_remote_forcing_probe", "Title": "Weather Here Because of the Ocean There: Testing Whether Weather/Climate Foundation Models Represent the Non-Local ENSO Teleconnection Mechanism, Not Just Local Persistence", "Short Hypothesis": "A defining, causally-directional, non-local mechanism of climate is the teleconnection: a remote tropical Pacific SST anomaly (ENSO) forces a Rossby-wave train that reorganizes distant mid-latitude weather (e.g., the Pacific-North American pattern) with a characteristic lag and phase, whereas local persistence carries no such distant, directional signal. We hypothesize that if data-driven weather/climate foundation models represent this teleconnection mechanism rather than local autocorrelation, then (a) the remote-forcing state (ENSO phase) is decodable from internal representations and linked to distant mid-latitude circulation beyond what local antecedents predict, (b) a remote-forcing counterfactual that perturbs ONLY the tropical Pacific state propagates the physically-correct downstream teleconnection pattern (correct sign, region, and lag), while a distance-matched control perturbation elsewhere does not, and (c) steering a recovered 'ENSO-forcing' direction causally shifts distant weather in the teleconnection-consistent direction. The tropical-Pacific-vs-elsewhere perturbation is an exact, mechanism-defining test that local-statistics correlation cannot pass.", "Related Work": "Interpretability of weather foundation models recovers single physical OBJECTS/features: SAE studies on GraphCast (2025) find tropical cyclones, atmospheric rivers, and sea-ice and steer them; GraphDOP work (2025) shows a unified observation-driven world model that infers non-local structures (jet streams) and applies FSOI/DA diagnostics; Physics Steering (2025) steers vorticity/diffusion directions. Separately, an ocean-role study (2026) correlates GraphCast OUTPUT errors with SST (an input-output diagnostic, not an internal test), and a coupled DL emulator study (2025) finds AMPLIFIED ENSO teleconnections at the OUTPUT level. Climate science establishes the mechanism precisely: ENSO remotely forces mid-latitude circulation via Rossby-wave teleconnections, distinct from local air-sea coupling (QTCM 2006; tropical-Atlantic remote-vs-local studies 2001-2021). Crucially, no work tests whether a weather/climate foundation model's INTERNAL representation encodes the non-local, causally-directional teleconnection mechanism (remote cause -> distant effect with correct lag/phase), whether it is causally load-bearing, or uses a remote-forcing counterfactual to separate teleconnection from local persistence. Because ENSO state and local antecedents are correlated, output-level benchmarks and error-SST correlations cannot arbitrate this. Our teleconnection-beyond-local probe, tropical-Pacific-vs-elsewhere counterfactual, and causal ENSO-forcing steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution.", "Abstract": "Data-driven weather and climate foundation models now rival physical models and have been shown to encode recognizable physical objects such as cyclones and atmospheric rivers. But one of the deepest organizing mechanisms of the climate system is non-local and causally directional: teleconnections, in which a remote tropical Pacific sea-surface-temperature anomaly (El Nino/La Nina) forces a Rossby-wave train that reorganizes weather thousands of kilometers away, with a characteristic lag and phase. A model can forecast well by exploiting local persistence without ever internalizing that distant weather is caused by the ocean elsewhere, making teleconnections an ideal, mechanism-defining probe. We test whether pretrained weather/climate foundation models (GraphCast, Pangu-Weather, and a coupled ocean-atmosphere emulator such as DLESyM where available) internally represent the ENSO teleconnection, using ERA5 and coupled-emulator states. First, a teleconnection-beyond-local probe: is the remote-forcing state (ENSO phase) decodable from internal representations and predictive of distant mid-latitude circulation beyond what local antecedents allow? Second, and most decisively, a remote-forcing counterfactual: we perturb ONLY the tropical Pacific state (imposing an ENSO anomaly) and measure whether the model's internal representation and rollout produce the physically-correct downstream teleconnection (e.g., the Pacific-North American pattern) with the right sign, region, and lag, compared to a distance-matched control perturbation elsewhere that should not trigger it. Third, causal steering along a recovered ENSO-forcing direction. Metrics quantify a teleconnection-beyond-local index, a remote-vs-control propagation asymmetry, and a lag/phase fidelity score. Either outcome sharpens whether these operationally-deployed models represent the non-local causality behind seasonal climate.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load pretrained weather/climate foundation models with public weights (GraphCast, Pangu-Weather; and a coupled ocean-atmosphere DL emulator such as DLESyM, or GraphDOP-style observation-driven model, where accessible for ENSO experiments). Assemble ERA5 fields (SST, geopotential at 500 hPa, winds, temperature) spanning many years including El Nino/La Nina/neutral states. Compute ground-truth ENSO indices (Nino3.4), the established teleconnection patterns (e.g., PNA, and tropical-Atlantic/monsoon responses) and their canonical lags. Extract per-grid internal representations across layers/blocks. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode local fields (SST, geopotential) and the remote-forcing ENSO index from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded and select best layers. Establish a local-persistence baseline: predict a distant region's circulation from its own and neighboring recent antecedents only.", "3. Teleconnection-beyond-local probe (primary probe): Test whether distant mid-latitude circulation (e.g., North Pacific/North American geopotential) is predictable from the model's internal representation BEYOND local persistence, specifically via the tropical-Pacific (ENSO) component. Regress out the local-antecedent-predictable component and re-decode the distant response from the residual, and test whether the residual signal aligns with the ENSO-forcing direction rather than local features. Primary metric: Teleconnection-Beyond-Local Index = distant-circulation decoding R^2 from the model representation minus what local antecedents alone achieve. A mechanism-representing model carries non-local ENSO-driven information; a persistence model does not exceed the local baseline.", "4. Remote-forcing counterfactual (primary, decisive test): Impose an ENSO SST/state anomaly ONLY in the tropical Pacific (a warm El-Nino-like or cold La-Nina-like pattern), holding the rest of the initial state fixed, and roll the model forward. Measure whether the model's internal representation and forecast develop the physically-correct downstream teleconnection (e.g., the canonical PNA geopotential dipole) in the correct distant region, with the correct sign and lag. Compare against a distance-matched control perturbation of equal magnitude imposed in a region that does NOT drive that teleconnection (e.g., a mid-latitude patch or a tropical basin without the relevant remote link). Metric: Remote-vs-Control Propagation Asymmetry = |distant teleconnection-pattern response to tropical-Pacific perturbation| / |distant response to matched control perturbation|. Mechanistic representation => asymmetry >> 1 with the correct spatial pattern; local-statistics representation => asymmetry ~ 1 (perturbations only affect their immediate vicinity).", "5. Sign and lag/phase fidelity: Test whether the response reverses when the ENSO perturbation sign flips (El Nino vs La Nina should produce opposite-sign PNA responses) and whether the downstream teleconnection emerges at the physically-correct lag (weeks to a season, within the model's rollout horizon). Metric: sign-reversal consistency and correlation between the model's response-onset lag and the canonical teleconnection lag; shuffled-region controls.", "6. Causal ENSO-forcing steering: Recover an 'ENSO-forcing' direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between El-Nino and La-Nina states). Steer this direction with matched magnitude in the internal state and test whether the distant mid-latitude readout shifts in the teleconnection-consistent direction, while matched-norm random directions and a local-feature direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of ENSO-forcing direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "7. Failure-prediction link: Test whether an internal 'teleconnection-representation strength' score predicts, per case, where the model's seasonal/extended-range distant forecast is wrong, especially in strong-ENSO years where the teleconnection dominates. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error distant forecasts from the internal signal, vs output-uncertainty and local-persistence baselines.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where teleconnection structure emerges across depth and whether recovered ENSO-forcing subspaces align across the different foundation models (CKA), assessing whether the teleconnection mechanism is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a physical AGCM pacemaker experiment / linear-baroclinic-model teleconnection response that encodes the mechanism by construction, and (lower bound) a local-persistence forecaster with no remote access, placing foundation models on the local-statistics-to-teleconnection-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["ENSO state and local antecedents are correlated, making the teleconnection-beyond-local separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with local-antecedent residualization, the tropical-Pacific-vs-matched-control counterfactual (which holds perturbation magnitude fixed and varies only location), and the sign/lag fidelity tests that a local-statistics model cannot reproduce.", "Many atmosphere-only weather foundation models (GraphCast, Pangu) do not ingest evolving SST as an input variable, so imposing an ENSO SST anomaly may be limited to perturbing the atmospheric state consistent with ENSO or requires an SST-aware/coupled emulator; we prioritize the coupled emulator (DLESyM) and SST-inclusive models for the decisive counterfactual, and for atmosphere-only models perturb the ENSO-associated atmospheric pattern, clearly reporting per-model what is manipulable.", "Medium-range weather models have short rollout horizons (days), while teleconnections develop over weeks to a season; the downstream response may be truncated. We use models/emulators with extended or subseasonal-to-seasonal rollout where possible, focus on the fast component of the wave-train response, and report horizon caveats.", "Imposing a tropical-Pacific anomaly may push the state off the model's training distribution, confounding the response with generic OOD failure; we mitigate with physically-plausible anomaly magnitudes, compare against a matched-magnitude control perturbation (so any generic OOD effect is shared), and report sensitivity to anomaly strength.", "Teleconnection ground truth (PNA response, lags) has internal-variability noise; we composite over many ENSO events and use canonical patterns, reporting statistical uncertainty.", "Linear probes recovering an ENSO-forcing direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/local-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and delta-representations.", "A negative result (models exploit local persistence and fail to propagate the correct non-local teleconnection) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting these operationally-deployed models for subseasonal-to-seasonal and ENSO-year forecasts, though it may be perceived as confirmatory of skepticism; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether weather/climate foundation models represent the non-local teleconnection mechanism.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public ERA5 / coupled-emulator data, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
