Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "selection_rule_forbidden_transition_probe", "Title": "Bright or Dark? Testing Whether Molecular/Materials Foundation Models Represent Symmetry-Based Optical Selection Rules, Not Just the Energy Gap", "Short Hypothesis": "Whether an electronic transition absorbs light is set by an exactly-known symmetry mechanism\u2014optical selection rules (Laporte/parity, spin, and orbital-symmetry rules)\u2014that determines the oscillator strength (bright vs dark) largely independently of the energy-gap size: two molecules can have nearly identical HOMO-LUMO gaps yet one is intensely absorbing and the other symmetry-forbidden (dark). We hypothesize that if a molecular/materials foundation model represents this selection-rule mechanism rather than treating optical activity as a monotone function of the energy gap, then (a) transition brightness (oscillator strength) is decodable from internal representations along an axis distinct from the gap, (b) a symmetry-breaking counterfactual\u2014a small distortion that lowers molecular symmetry and turns a forbidden (dark) transition allowed (bright) at nearly-fixed gap\u2014shifts the internal brightness signal accordingly, whereas a symmetry-preserving perturbation of matched magnitude does not, and (c) steering a recovered 'allowedness' direction causally reshapes predicted absorption without changing the gap. The forbidden-vs-allowed symmetry counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that gap correlation cannot pass.", "Related Work": "Two lines bracket the gap. (1) ML for optical/excited-state properties\u2014TD-DFT surrogate models, oscillator-strength and UV-Vis spectrum predictors (e.g., message-passing and transformer models on QM8/QM9-spectra, Oled/optical datasets), and excited-state foundation models\u2014are trained to predict excitation energies and intensities but are judged by regression error, not by whether they internalize the selection-rule mechanism. (2) Interpretability of chemistry/atomistic foundation models\u2014SAE studies on chemistry FMs, 'Circuits, Features, and Heuristics in Molecular Transformers' (2025), MoleX (2024), and periodic-law/spin-state probes\u2014recover substructure/element/valence features validated against ground/scalar annotations, none of which concern excited-state symmetry. Physics/interpretability work recovers and steers single scalar quantities (energy, vorticity) but never the parity/spin symmetry that decides whether a transition is bright or dark. Crucially, no work tests whether a foundation model internally represents optical selection rules\u2014the symmetry mechanism separating oscillator strength from the energy gap\u2014whether that representation is causally load-bearing, or uses a symmetry-breaking counterfactual to arbitrate mechanism vs gap correlation. Because bright transitions also tend to have accessible gaps in typical data, correlation-based benchmarks cannot separate the two. Our brightness-beyond-gap probe, forbidden-vs-allowed symmetry counterfactual, and causal allowedness steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, distinct from the chirality (pseudoscalar) and spin-state probes in also targeting the parity/orbital-symmetry selection mechanism of excited states.", "Abstract": "Whether a molecule absorbs light at a given color is governed not by the size of its energy gap alone but by an exactly-known symmetry mechanism: optical selection rules. Parity (Laporte), spin, and orbital-symmetry rules decide whether an electronic transition is allowed (bright, large oscillator strength) or forbidden (dark, vanishing intensity), and this can flip a transition from dark to bright with a tiny symmetry-lowering distortion that barely changes the gap. This makes selection rules an ideal, mechanism-defining probe for whether molecular/materials foundation models learn excited-state symmetry or merely a gap-to-intensity correlate. We probe pretrained molecular foundation models and optical-property predictors on datasets with computed oscillator strengths and excitation energies (e.g., QM8, QM9-spectra, and curated symmetric/near-symmetric molecule sets). First, a brightness-beyond-gap probe: is transition oscillator strength decodable from internal representations beyond what the energy gap predicts, along a distinct axis? Second, and most decisively, a symmetry-breaking counterfactual: we apply a small distortion that lowers molecular symmetry and turns a forbidden transition allowed at nearly-fixed gap, versus a matched symmetry-preserving distortion that keeps it dark, and test whether the internal brightness signal responds to the symmetry change, not the gap; a gap-correlate model responds identically. Third, causal allowedness steering reshapes predicted absorption without shifting the gap. Metrics quantify a brightness-beyond-gap index, a symmetry-break-vs-preserve causal asymmetry, and an intensity-error failure predictor. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed optical-property models represent selection rules or are blind to them\u2014critical for photochemistry and materials discovery.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load pretrained molecular foundation models spanning representation types with usable internal states\u2014a 3D atomistic FM (Uni-Mol / MACE latents), a SMILES/graph chemical FM (ChemBERTa-2, SMI-TED)\u2014and, where available, an excited-state / optical-property predictor. Assemble datasets with computed excited-state properties: QM8 and QM9-spectra (TD-DFT excitation energies and oscillator strengths), plus a curated set of high-symmetry and near-symmetric molecules (e.g., centrosymmetric species, symmetric aromatics) where Laporte/parity-forbidden transitions occur. For every low-lying transition record its excitation energy (gap), oscillator strength f (brightness), spin/symmetry-forbidden classification, and molecular point group. Extract internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode excitation energy/gap, oscillator strength (brightness), and forbidden/allowed classification from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded and select best layers. Establish a gap-only baseline: predict oscillator strength directly from the excitation energy/gap.", "3. Brightness-beyond-gap probe (primary probe): Test whether oscillator strength is decodable BEYOND the energy gap. Regress out the gap-predictable component of the representation and re-decode brightness from the residual. Primary metric: Brightness-Beyond-Gap Index = probe R^2 for oscillator strength from the model representation minus what the gap alone achieves. Also measure the angle between the recovered gap direction and the recovered brightness direction; a mechanistic representation encodes them as distinct axes, a gap-correlate representation collapses them. Report separately for forbidden-vs-allowed pairs at matched gap (the hardest, most diagnostic cases).", "4. Symmetry-breaking counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For high-symmetry molecules with a symmetry-forbidden (dark) transition, construct two matched geometric edits. (a) Symmetry-breaking edit: a small distortion that lowers the point group (e.g., breaks centrosymmetry / a mirror plane), which by selection rules turns the forbidden transition allowed (brightness rises) at nearly-unchanged gap. (b) Symmetry-preserving control edit: a distortion of matched RMSD that preserves the relevant symmetry element (transition stays dark). Feed both to the model and measure the change in the internal brightness signal (probed oscillator strength / optical-head output) between the two edits. Metric: Symmetry-Break-vs-Preserve Causal Asymmetry = |brightness-signal change under symmetry-breaking edit| / |change under matched symmetry-preserving edit|. Mechanistic representation => asymmetry >> 1 (symmetry lowering brightens the transition, matched distortion does not); gap-correlate representation => asymmetry ~ 1 (both edits change the gap similarly and little else).", "5. Physics consistency (parity/spin dissociation): Test whether the model reproduces the correct qualitative selection-rule structure: (a) parity-forbidden (Laporte) transitions in centrosymmetric molecules read dark and brighten upon symmetry breaking; (b) spin-forbidden transitions (singlet-triplet) read dark regardless of geometric symmetry breaking (a different rule), giving a double dissociation between the parity and spin mechanisms. Metric: fraction of parity-forbidden transitions correctly predicted to brighten under symmetry lowering while spin-forbidden ones remain dark; shuffled-symmetry-label controls.", "6. Causal allowedness steering: Recover an 'allowedness/brightness' direction (a supervised probe direction, an SAE feature separating bright from dark transitions, or a delta-representation between symmetry-broken and symmetric versions of the same molecule). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's predicted absorption intensity increases/decreases WITHOUT shifting its predicted gap, while a matched gap direction shifts the gap without changing brightness. Controls: matched-norm random directions do neither. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |brightness change from allowedness steering| / |brightness change from random steering|; cross-talk = |gap shift from allowedness steering| (near zero for a disentangled representation).", "7. Failure-prediction and application link: Test whether an internal 'selection-rule representation strength' score (brightness-beyond-gap confidence / symmetry-counterfactual sensitivity) predicts, per molecule, where the model mis-estimates oscillator strength\u2014especially for symmetric, weakly-absorbing chromophores where the gap shortcut misleads. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-intensity-error transitions from the internal signal, vs output-confidence and gap-only baselines. This connects the representational signature to reliability in optical-materials/photochemistry screening.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where selection-rule/brightness structure emerges across depth and whether recovered allowedness subspaces align across the FMs (CKA), assessing whether the selection-rule mechanism is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a symmetry-aware / transition-dipole model that computes oscillator strength from symmetry by construction, and (lower bound) a gap-to-intensity regressor with no symmetry access, placing FMs on the gap-correlate-to-selection-rule-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Oscillator strength and energy gap are correlated in typical datasets (accessible bright transitions dominate), making the brightness-beyond-gap separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with gap-residualization, the symmetry-breaking double-dissociation counterfactual (which holds the gap nearly fixed while varying symmetry), and the parity-vs-spin dissociation that a gap-correlate model cannot reproduce.", "Constructing symmetry-breaking vs symmetry-preserving distortions with matched magnitude but different selection-rule consequences requires care with point-group assignment and verifying the intended brightness flip via reference TD-DFT; we compute reference oscillator strengths for the distorted geometries and report sensitivity to distortion magnitude and to point-group-assignment confidence.", "Small geometric distortions may push conformers off the model's training distribution, confounding the symmetry-breaking response with generic out-of-distribution failure; we bound distortion RMSD, verify the gap stays nearly fixed, and compare against the matched symmetry-preserving distortion so any generic OOD effect is shared.", "Many molecular FMs predict ground-state/scalar properties and may not natively output excited-state brightness; for these we probe whether the representation encodes brightness/selection-rule structure at all and prioritize models with excited-state or optical heads for the causal-steering readout, reporting per-model scope and treating scalar-only models as an informative lower bracket.", "Excited-state reference data (TD-DFT oscillator strengths) is functional- and basis-set-dependent and noisy for weak transitions; we use curated consensus datasets (QM8, QM9-spectra), restrict decisive tests to well-characterized symmetric molecules, and emphasize the qualitative bright/dark flip over exact intensity magnitude.", "Linear probes recovering an allowedness direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering, cross-talk controls, and random-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions\u2014we use both probe directions and SAE features.", "A negative result (models predict intensity as a gap correlate and fail the symmetry-breaking counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting molecular/materials FMs for photochemistry, OLED/dye, and optical-materials discovery, where symmetry-forbidden dark states are a central design consideration; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether foundation models represent optical selection rules.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes/SAEs and public excited-state datasets (QM8, QM9-spectra), with reference TD-DFT for counterfactual geometries, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'selection_rule_forbidden_transition_probe', 'Title': 'Bright or Dark? Testing Whether Molecular/Materials Foundation Models Represent Symmetry-Based Optical Selection Rules, Not Just the Energy Gap', 'Short Hypothesis': "Whether an electronic transition absorbs light is set by an exactly-known symmetry mechanism—optical selection rules (Laporte/parity, spin, and orbital-symmetry rules)—that determines the oscillator strength (bright vs dark) largely independently of the energy-gap size: two molecules can have nearly identical HOMO-LUMO gaps yet one is intensely absorbing and the other symmetry-forbidden (dark). We hypothesize that if a molecular/materials foundation model represents this selection-rule mechanism rather than treating optical activity as a monotone function of the energy gap, then (a) transition brightness (oscillator strength) is decodable from internal representations along an axis distinct from the gap, (b) a symmetry-breaking counterfactual—a small distortion that lowers molecular symmetry and turns a forbidden (dark) transition allowed (bright) at nearly-fixed gap—shifts the internal brightness signal accordingly, whereas a symmetry-preserving perturbation of matched magnitude does not, and (c) steering a recovered 'allowedness' direction causally reshapes predicted absorption without changing the gap. The forbidden-vs-allowed symmetry counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that gap correlation cannot pass.", 'Related Work': "Two lines bracket the gap. (1) ML for optical/excited-state properties—TD-DFT surrogate models, oscillator-strength and UV-Vis spectrum predictors (e.g., message-passing and transformer models on QM8/QM9-spectra, Oled/optical datasets), and excited-state foundation models—are trained to predict excitation energies and intensities but are judged by regression error, not by whether they internalize the selection-rule mechanism. (2) Interpretability of chemistry/atomistic foundation models—SAE studies on chemistry FMs, 'Circuits, Features, and Heuristics in Molecular Transformers' (2025), MoleX (2024), and periodic-law/spin-state probes—recover substructure/element/valence features validated against ground/scalar annotations, none of which concern excited-state symmetry. Physics/interpretability work recovers and steers single scalar quantities (energy, vorticity) but never the parity/spin symmetry that decides whether a transition is bright or dark. Crucially, no work tests whether a foundation model internally represents optical selection rules—the symmetry mechanism separating oscillator strength from the energy gap—whether that representation is causally load-bearing, or uses a symmetry-breaking counterfactual to arbitrate mechanism vs gap correlation. Because bright transitions also tend to have accessible gaps in typical data, correlation-based benchmarks cannot separate the two. Our brightness-beyond-gap probe, forbidden-vs-allowed symmetry counterfactual, and causal allowedness steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, distinct from the chirality (pseudoscalar) and spin-state probes in also targeting the parity/orbital-symmetry selection mechanism of excited states.", 'Abstract': 'Whether a molecule absorbs light at a given color is governed not by the size of its energy gap alone but by an exactly-known symmetry mechanism: optical selection rules. Parity (Laporte), spin, and orbital-symmetry rules decide whether an electronic transition is allowed (bright, large oscillator strength) or forbidden (dark, vanishing intensity), and this can flip a transition from dark to bright with a tiny symmetry-lowering distortion that barely changes the gap. This makes selection rules an ideal, mechanism-defining probe for whether molecular/materials foundation models learn excited-state symmetry or merely a gap-to-intensity correlate. We probe pretrained molecular foundation models and optical-property predictors on datasets with computed oscillator strengths and excitation energies (e.g., QM8, QM9-spectra, and curated symmetric/near-symmetric molecule sets). First, a brightness-beyond-gap probe: is transition oscillator strength decodable from internal representations beyond what the energy gap predicts, along a distinct axis? Second, and most decisively, a symmetry-breaking counterfactual: we apply a small distortion that lowers molecular symmetry and turns a forbidden transition allowed at nearly-fixed gap, versus a matched symmetry-preserving distortion that keeps it dark, and test whether the internal brightness signal responds to the symmetry change, not the gap; a gap-correlate model responds identically. Third, causal allowedness steering reshapes predicted absorption without shifting the gap. Metrics quantify a brightness-beyond-gap index, a symmetry-break-vs-preserve causal asymmetry, and an intensity-error failure predictor. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed optical-property models represent selection rules or are blind to them—critical for photochemistry and materials discovery.', 'Experiments': ['1. Models and data: Load pretrained molecular foundation models spanning representation types with usable internal states—a 3D atomistic FM (Uni-Mol / MACE latents), a SMILES/graph chemical FM (ChemBERTa-2, SMI-TED)—and, where available, an excited-state / optical-property predictor. Assemble datasets with computed excited-state properties: QM8 and QM9-spectra (TD-DFT excitation energies and oscillator strengths), plus a curated set of high-symmetry and near-symmetric molecules (e.g., centrosymmetric species, symmetric aromatics) where Laporte/parity-forbidden transitions occur. For every low-lying transition record its excitation energy (gap), oscillator strength f (brightness), spin/symmetry-forbidden classification, and molecular point group. Extract internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.', '2. Baseline decodability: Train linear