ymmetry ~ 1 (both edits change the gap similarly and little else).', '5. Physics consistency (parity/spin dissociation): Test whether the model reproduces the correct qualitative selection-rule structure: (a) parity-forbidden (Laporte) transitions in centrosymmetric molecules read dark and brighten upon symmetry breaking; (b) spin-forbidden transitions (singlet-triplet) read dark regardless of geometric symmetry breaking (a different rule), giving a double dissociation between the parity and spin mechanisms. Metric: fraction of parity-forbidden transitions correctly predicted to brighten under symmetry lowering while spin-forbidden ones remain dark; shuffled-symmetry-label controls.', "6. Causal allowedness steering: Recover an 'allowedness/brightness' direction (a supervised probe direction, an SAE feature separating bright from dark transitions, or a delta-representation between symmetry-broken and symmetric versions of the same molecule). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's predicted absorption intensity increases/decreases WITHOUT shifting its predicted gap, while a matched gap direction shifts the gap without changing brightness. Controls: matched-norm random directions do neither. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |brightness change from allowedness steering| / |brightness change from random steering|; cross-talk = |gap shift from allowedness steering| (near zero for a disentangled representation).", "7. Failure-prediction and application link: Test whether an internal 'selection-rule representation strength' score (brightness-beyond-gap confidence / symmetry-counterfactual sensitivity) predicts, per molecule, where the model mis-estimates oscillator strength—especially for symmetric, weakly-absorbing chromophores where the gap shortcut misleads. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-intensity-error transitions from the internal signal, vs output-confidence and gap-only baselines. This connects the representational signature to reliability in optical-materials/photochemistry screening.", '8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where selection-rule/brightness structure emerges across depth and whether recovered allowedness subspaces align across the FMs (CKA), assessing whether the selection-rule mechanism is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a symmetry-aware / transition-dipole model that computes oscillator strength from symmetry by construction, and (lower bound) a gap-to-intensity regressor with no symmetry access, placing FMs on the gap-correlate-to-selection-rule-mechanism spectrum.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Oscillator strength and energy gap are correlated in typical datasets (accessible bright transitions dominate), making the brightness-beyond-gap separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with gap-residualization, the symmetry-breaking double-dissociation counterfactual (which holds the gap nearly fixed while varying symmetry), and the parity-vs-spin dissociation that a gap-correlate model cannot reproduce.', 'Constructing symmetry-breaking vs symmetry-preserving distortions with matched magnitude but different selection-rule consequences requires care with point-group assignment and verifying the intended brightness flip via reference TD-DFT; we compute reference oscillator strengths for the distorted geometries and report sensitivity to distortion magnitude and to point-group-assignment confidence.', "Small geometric distortions may push conformers off the model's training distribution, confounding the symmetry-breaking response with generic out-of-distribution failure; we bound distortion RMSD, verify the gap stays nearly fixed, and compare against the matched symmetry-preserving distortion so any generic OOD effect is shared.", 'Many molecular FMs predict ground-state/scalar properties and may not natively output excited-state brightness; for these we probe whether the representation encodes brightness/selection-rule structure at all and prioritize models with excited-state or optical heads for the causal-steering readout, reporting per-model scope and treating scalar-only models as an informative lower bracket.', 'Excited-state reference data (TD-DFT oscillator strengths) is functional- and basis-set-dependent and noisy for weak transitions; we use curated consensus datasets (QM8, QM9-spectra), restrict decisive tests to well-characterized symmetric molecules, and emphasize the qualitative bright/dark flip over exact intensity magnitude.', 'Linear probes recovering an allowedness direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering, cross-talk controls, and random-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions—we use both probe directions and SAE features.', 'A negative result (models predict intensity as a gap correlate and fail the symmetry-breaking counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting molecular/materials FMs for photochemistry, OLED/dye, and optical-materials discovery, where symmetry-forbidden dark states are a central design consideration; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether foundation models represent optical selection rules.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes/SAEs and public excited-state datasets (QM8, QM9-spectra), with reference TD-DFT for counterfactual geometries, keeping compute at academic scale.']}

