Response Content: {"total": 3, "offset": 0, "data": [{"paperId": "649b99c9fa0b92239c9365742e5ad1bedc3160e5", "title": "Paradigms, innovations, and biological applications of RNA velocity: a comprehensive review", "venue": "Briefings Bioinform.", "year": 2025, "citationCount": 9, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12265890, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provid
Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "developmental_arrow_directionality_probe", "Title": "Which Way Is Forward? Testing Whether Single-Cell Foundation Models Represent the Irreversible Arrow of Differentiation, Not Just Cell-State Identity", "Short Hypothesis": "Differentiation has a defining, directional, quasi-irreversible mechanism: cells flow along lineages from high-potency progenitors to low-potency differentiated states, with transcriptional potency (entropy) decreasing monotonically and the reverse transition physically disfavored. Crucially, a static expression snapshot is directionless - RNA-velocity and trajectory tools must inject direction from splicing/metabolic-labeling signals the model never sees. We hypothesize that if a single-cell foundation model (scFM) represents the developmental-arrow mechanism rather than merely cell-state identity, then (a) the signed developmental direction between two adjacent states along a known lineage is decodable from internal representations beyond what marginal cell-state similarity predicts, (b) its internal 'developmental progression' axis reproduces the monotonic, irreversible decrease of transcriptional potency/entropy (a dose-response), and (c) an in-silico transition that moves a cell in the forward (differentiation) direction is treated differently from the equal-magnitude reverse (dedifferentiation) transition. The forward-vs-reverse asymmetry is a mechanism-defining test that identity-clustering and pseudotime-correlation cannot pass.", "Related Work": "RNA-velocity and trajectory-inference tools (Velocyto, scVelo, CellRank, dynamo, LatentVelo, VelvetVAE) all establish that snapshot expression is directionless: they must obtain the arrow of differentiation from EXTERNAL spliced/unspliced ratios or metabolic labeling, and Hodge-decomposition/dynamical-systems treatments formalize the directional (curl-free/gradient) structure of the velocity field. scFM interpretability instead recovers static concepts: SAE/transcoder studies (scFeatureLens 2024, Concept-SAE 2025, transcoder circuits 2025) find features matching cell types, tissues, and cell-cycle phase, and a systematic evaluation (2026) shows scGPT/Geneformer attention captures co-expression, not causal regulation. Causal single-cell VAEs (sVAE+, SAMS-VAE) learn intervention structure but are bespoke, not tests of pretrained scFMs. Crucially, no work tests whether a pretrained scFM's INTERNAL representation encodes the temporal DIRECTIONALITY and quasi-irreversibility of differentiation (the developmental arrow) from snapshot input alone, whether that direction is causally load-bearing, or uses a forward-vs-reverse transition asymmetry to separate the arrow mechanism from static cell-state identity. Because adjacent developmental states are also the most similar in expression, identity-based clustering and raw pseudotime correlation cannot arbitrate this; the signed-direction-beyond-identity probe, potency dose-response, and forward-vs-reverse counterfactual are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution.", "Abstract": "Single-cell foundation models (scFMs) are increasingly used to annotate cell states and infer developmental trajectories, and their internal features are known to encode cell-type identity. But differentiation is defined not by identity alone but by a directional, quasi-irreversible mechanism: cells flow from high-potency progenitors to low-potency differentiated fates, transcriptional potency decreases along the way, and the reverse transition is physically disfavored. A static expression snapshot is directionless - this is precisely why RNA-velocity tools must inject the arrow from splicing signals the model never receives - making developmental directionality an ideal, mechanism-defining probe for whether scFMs internalize developmental priors or merely cluster cell states. We probe pretrained scFMs (scGPT, Geneformer, cell2sentence) on well-characterized developmental atlases with ground-truth lineage order and pseudotime/velocity (pancreatic endocrinogenesis, hematopoiesis, dentate gyrus neurogenesis). First, a direction-beyond-identity probe: is the signed developmental direction between two adjacent lineage states decodable from internal representations beyond what marginal cell-state similarity predicts? Second, a potency dose-response: does a recovered 'developmental progression' axis reproduce the monotonic decrease of transcriptional entropy/potency, rather than tracking arbitrary cell-type labels? Third, and most decisively, a forward-vs-reverse counterfactual: an in-silico gene-program shift that moves a cell in the differentiation direction versus the equal-magnitude dedifferentiation direction should be treated asymmetrically by a mechanism-representing model. We validate causally by steering a recovered direction axis. Metrics quantify a direction-beyond-identity index, a potency-monotonicity score, and a forward-vs-reverse asymmetry. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed scFMs understand which way development runs - critical for reprogramming, regeneration, and cancer.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load pretrained scFMs with public weights and accessible per-cell/per-gene representations (scGPT, Geneformer, cell2sentence). Assemble developmental atlases with established lineage order and ground-truth directional signal from RNA velocity / metabolic labeling: pancreatic endocrinogenesis, mouse hematopoiesis, and dentate gyrus neurogenesis (scVelo/CellRank-processed). For each cell record its lineage/branch, ground-truth pseudotime and velocity-derived direction, and transcriptional potency (e.g. CytoTRACE score / expression entropy). Extract internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode cell-type identity, pseudotime, and potency/entropy from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded and select best layers. Establish a static identity/similarity baseline (predict relative order from cell-state similarity in the raw expression space).", "3. Direction-beyond-identity probe (primary probe): For pairs of adjacent cells/states along a known lineage, test whether the SIGNED developmental direction (which is earlier vs later) is decodable BEYOND marginal cell-state similarity. Regress out the identity/similarity-predictable component and re-decode direction from the residual. Primary metric: Direction-Beyond-Identity Index = signed-order classification accuracy from the model representation minus what static cell-state similarity achieves. A mechanism-representing model carries directional information; an identity-clustering model does not exceed the similarity baseline. Report separately along the lineage axis (should show a strong consistent direction) vs. across unrelated cell types (control, should show none).", "4. Potency dose-response test: Recover a 'developmental progression' direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between progenitor and differentiated states). Test whether the model's internal progression signal reproduces the monotonic, signed decrease of transcriptional potency/entropy along the lineage, rather than a non-monotonic or identity-tracking response. Metric: Spearman correlation and monotonicity of the internal progression signal vs. ground-truth potency; shuffled-order labels serve as a control. A mechanistic representation reproduces the irreversible potency gradient.", "5. Forward-vs-reverse counterfactual (primary, decisive test): Construct in-silico transitions along the lineage: (a) forward edit - shift a cell's expression toward the next (more differentiated) state along the known trajectory (up/down-regulating lineage-driver genes in the differentiation direction); (b) reverse edit - an equal-magnitude shift toward the previous (progenitor) state. Feed both to the model and measure how it treats each: change in internal progression signal, change in the model's next-state / masked-gene prediction, and (where a generative head exists) plausibility of the resulting profile. Metric: Forward-vs-Reverse Asymmetry = |model response to forward edit| / |model response to reverse edit|, and whether the forward edit yields an in-distribution, lineage-consistent continuation while the reverse edit is treated as anomalous/implausible. A mechanistic (arrow-representing) model shows asymmetry (dedifferentiation is disfavored/anomalous); an identity-only model treats both symmetrically as mere state changes. Control: for a non-developmental axis (e.g. cell-cycle phase, which is cyclic and reversible) the asymmetry should vanish, giving a double dissociation.", "6. Causal direction-axis steering: Steer the recovered developmental-progression axis with matched magnitude in the forward vs reverse direction and test whether the model's downstream cell-state / potency readout shifts consistently with differentiation, while matched-norm random directions and a static identity-difference direction do not reproduce the signed, asymmetric effect. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of progression direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "7. Branch-point specificity: At known lineage bifurcations, test whether the model's directional signal correctly separates the divergent fates (rather than collapsing them by similarity), and whether it encodes the pre-commitment progenitor as upstream of both branches. Metric: fraction of branch points where the internal direction orders progenitor before both fates.", "8. Failure-prediction and discovery link: Test whether an internal 'direction-representation strength' score predicts where the model's trajectory ordering disagrees with velocity ground truth - especially in regeneration/dedifferentiation regimes where the arrow reverses. Metric: AUROC for flagging mis-ordered cells from the internal signal vs. identity-only baselines. This connects the representational signature to reliability (and potential discovery of unexpected transitions).", "9. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where developmental-direction structure emerges across depth and whether recovered progression subspaces align across scGPT, Geneformer, and cell2sentence (CKA), assessing whether the arrow of differentiation is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) an RNA-velocity/CellRank directional model that encodes the arrow by construction, and (lower bound) a static-identity classifier with no directional access, placing scFMs on the identity-clustering-to-arrow-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Adjacent developmental states are the most similar in expression, so directional signal is entangled with cell-state identity, making the direction-beyond-identity separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with identity/similarity residualization, the forward-vs-reverse counterfactual (which holds edit magnitude fixed and varies only direction), and the cell-cycle (reversible) double-dissociation control.", "Ground-truth developmental direction itself comes from RNA velocity/metabolic labeling, which is noisy and can be unreliable in some regions; we use datasets where velocity/pseudotime is well-validated (pancreas, hematopoiesis, dentate gyrus), cross-check with independent potency (CytoTRACE) and known lineage biology, and report sensitivity to ground-truth confidence.", "The forward/reverse in-silico edits are synthetic and may push profiles off the model's data distribution, confounding the asymmetry with generic out-of-distribution behavior; we mitigate by matching forward and reverse edit magnitudes (so any generic OOD effect is shared), using real neighboring-state profiles as edit targets where possible, and reporting edit-magnitude sensitivity.", "scFMs use different tokenizations (rank-based for Geneformer, value-binned for scGPT, sentence for cell2sentence) and may pool to a single cell embedding, limiting fine-grained edits; we document each model's input scheme, prioritize models exposing usable per-cell representations, and report per-model caveats.", "Linear probes recovering a progression direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/identity-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and delta-representations.", "A negative result (scFMs cluster cell states but do not represent the directional, irreversible arrow of differentiation, treating forward and reverse transitions symmetrically) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting scFMs for trajectory inference, reprogramming, regeneration, and cancer dedifferentiation - precisely the settings where getting the arrow backwards is catastrophic; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether single-cell foundation models represent the developmental arrow.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public developmental atlases with velocity/pseudotime ground truth, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
