Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "le_chatelier_shift_probe", "Title": "Does the Model Shift the Right Way? Testing Whether Reaction Foundation Models Represent Le Chatelier Equilibrium Shifts, Not Just Static Reaction Identity", "Short Hypothesis": "Chemical equilibrium has an exactly-known, directional, relational mechanism: Le Chatelier's principle plus the law of mass action say that when an equilibrium is stressed (add/remove a species, change pressure/volume, change temperature for an exo/endothermic reaction), the equilibrium shifts in a specific, sign-determined direction to counteract the stress, with a magnitude set by the reaction quotient relative to K. We hypothesize that if a reaction/thermodynamics foundation model represents this mechanism rather than static reaction identity, then (a) the SIGNED shift direction under a given stress is decodable from internal representations beyond what the reaction's identity/stoichiometry alone predicts, (b) a stress-direction counterfactual - adding versus removing the same species, or the same temperature change for an exothermic versus endothermic reaction - flips the internal shift signal as thermodynamics dictates, whereas a stress that leaves the equilibrium unchanged (e.g., adding an inert species at constant volume, or a pressure change for a reaction with equal moles on both sides) does not, and (c) steering a recovered 'shift-direction' axis causally moves predicted product/reactant balance in the Le-Chatelier-consistent direction. The add-vs-remove and exo-vs-endo counterfactuals are exact, mechanism-defining tests that static reaction-identity correlation cannot pass.", "Related Work": "Two lines bracket the gap. (1) Reaction/thermodynamics ML - equilibrium-constant predictors, reaction-thermodynamics models, and chemistry foundation models (SMI-TED/FM4M, ChemBERTa, reaction transformers) - predict K, dG, or products but are judged by regression/accuracy, not by whether they internalize the directional Le Chatelier response to a stress. (2) Chemistry-model interpretability - SAEs on chemistry LMs ('Unveiling Latent Knowledge', 2025) recovering static substructure/physicochemical/pharmacological features, attribution for solubility (2024), design-choice/latent-space studies (2025), and the SchNet reaction-analogy work (2025) showing constant static 'reaction vectors' (alcohol->carbonyl = '-H2') - all recover static, non-relational structure. Physics-model steering (Physics Steering 2025; emergent energy features 2025) recovers and steers single scalar quantities, not a directional equilibrium-shift response. Crucially, no work tests whether a model internally represents the RELATIONAL, directional Le Chatelier mechanism - that stressing an equilibrium produces a sign-determined shift - whether that shift representation is causally load-bearing, or uses an add-vs-remove / exo-vs-endo counterfactual to separate the mechanism from static reaction identity. The reaction-analogy work is the closest, but it encodes a fixed transformation, not the response of an equilibrium to a perturbation. Our shift-direction-beyond-identity probe, stress-direction counterfactual, and causal shift-axis steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution.", "Abstract": "Chemical equilibrium is governed by one of chemistry's most fundamental and exactly-known relational mechanisms: Le Chatelier's principle and the law of mass action, which dictate that stressing an equilibrium - by adding or removing a species, changing pressure/volume, or changing temperature - shifts it in a specific, sign-determined direction to counteract the stress. The direction is not a property of the reaction alone but of the reaction-and-stress together: the same reaction shifts toward products when a reactant is added and toward reactants when it is removed. This makes Le Chatelier an ideal, mechanism-defining probe for whether reaction foundation models learn equilibrium mechanism or merely static reaction identity. We probe pretrained reaction/thermodynamics and chemistry foundation models on curated and synthetic equilibrium systems where the equilibrium constant and the true shift direction under each stress are exactly known. First, a shift-direction-beyond-identity probe: is the signed equilibrium-shift direction under a given stress decodable from internal representations beyond what the reaction's identity and stoichiometry predict? Second, and most decisively, a stress-direction counterfactual: adding versus removing the same species (opposite shifts) and the same temperature change applied to an exothermic versus endothermic reaction (opposite shifts) should flip the internal shift signal, while an equilibrium-neutral stress (inert-species addition, or pressure change for equal-mole reactions) should not. Third, causal steering of a recovered shift-direction axis. Metrics quantify a shift-beyond-identity index, a stress-direction causal asymmetry, and a mass-action dose-response test. Either outcome sharpens whether reaction models represent the equilibrium mechanism that underlies reaction design and process chemistry.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load pretrained chemistry/reaction foundation models with usable internal representations - a masked chemistry FM (SMI-TED/FM4M), a reaction transformer, and, where available, a reaction-thermodynamics / equilibrium-constant predictor. Assemble equilibrium systems with exactly-known thermodynamics: (a) synthetic gas/solution equilibria with specified stoichiometry, K, and reaction enthalpy sign (so shift directions under add/remove, pressure, and temperature stresses are analytically known), and (b) curated real equilibria (acid-base, esterification/hydrolysis, Haber-type, solubility) with literature K and dH signs. Encode each (reaction, current-composition, stress) case as input appropriate to each model. Extract internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode reaction identity/stoichiometry, K/dG, enthalpy sign, and the signed shift direction under a stress from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded and select best layers. Establish a static-identity baseline: predict shift direction from reaction identity and stoichiometry alone (which cannot determine add-vs-remove direction).", "3. Shift-direction-beyond-identity probe (primary probe): Test whether the SIGNED shift direction under a specific stress is decodable BEYOND static reaction identity/stoichiometry. Regress out the identity-predictable component and re-decode shift direction from the residual. Primary metric: Shift-Beyond-Identity Index = signed-shift-direction accuracy from the model representation minus what static identity/stoichiometry achieve. A mechanism-representing model carries the stress-conditioned directional information; a static-identity model does not exceed the baseline.", "4. Stress-direction counterfactual (primary, decisive test): Construct matched stress pairs holding the reaction fixed. (a) Add-vs-remove: add a reactant (shift toward products) versus remove the same reactant (shift toward reactants) - opposite directions. (b) Exo-vs-endo temperature: apply the same temperature increase to an exothermic reaction (shift toward reactants) versus an endothermic reaction (shift toward products). Measure the change in the model's internal shift signal (probed shift direction / predicted product-reactant balance) for each. Metric: Stress-Direction Causal Asymmetry = |shift-signal change under one stress direction| relative to its opposite, testing for a consistent sign flip. Mechanistic representation => the internal signal flips sign correctly across add/remove and exo/endo; static-identity representation => the signal is stress-agnostic (no flip). Report fraction of cases with correct sign flip vs. a shuffled-stress control.", "5. Equilibrium-neutral control (double dissociation): Apply stresses that thermodynamics says do NOT shift the equilibrium: adding an inert species at constant volume, or changing pressure for a reaction with equal moles of gas on both sides. A mechanistic representation should show a near-zero shift signal for these, while showing a strong signal for genuine stresses - a double dissociation that a stress-agnostic or naive model cannot produce. Metric: ratio of shift-signal magnitude for genuine vs. equilibrium-neutral stresses.", "6. Mass-action dose-response: Vary the magnitude of a stress (amount of species added, size of temperature change) and test whether the internal shift signal grows monotonically and tracks the reaction-quotient-vs-K prediction (larger stress -> larger shift, saturating as the new equilibrium is approached). Metric: Spearman correlation and sign agreement between internal shift-signal magnitude and the mass-action-predicted shift; shuffled-magnitude controls.", "7. Causal shift-axis steering: Recover a 'shift-direction' axis (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between add-stress and remove-stress cases). Steer this axis with matched magnitude and test whether the model's predicted product-reactant balance moves in the Le-Chatelier-consistent direction, while matched-norm random directions and a static-reaction-identity direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of shift-direction axis| / |effect of random direction|.", "8. Failure-prediction link: Test whether an internal 'shift-representation strength' score predicts, per case, where the model mis-predicts the new equilibrium - especially for cases where static heuristics mislead (equilibrium-neutral stresses, or temperature effects requiring the enthalpy sign). Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error cases from the internal signal vs. output-confidence and static-identity baselines.", "9. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where shift-direction structure emerges across depth and whether recovered shift-direction subspaces align across the chemistry and reaction-thermodynamics models (CKA), assessing whether the Le Chatelier mechanism is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) an explicit mass-action/equilibrium solver that computes shifts by construction, and (lower bound) a static reaction-identity classifier with no stress access, placing foundation models on the static-identity-to-equilibrium-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Reaction identity and shift behavior are entangled in real data, making the shift-beyond-identity separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with identity-residualization, the add-vs-remove and exo-vs-endo counterfactuals (which hold the reaction fixed and vary only the stress), the equilibrium-neutral double-dissociation control, and mass-action dose-response tests a static model cannot pass.", "Most chemistry FMs take a single molecule or reaction as input and no explicit stress/composition, so 'applying a stress' must be operationalized via the model's input format (e.g., altered composition tokens, temperature/pressure conditioning where available, or reaction-condition inputs for reaction-thermodynamics models); we prioritize models that ingest reaction conditions or composition, clearly report per-model how stress is supplied, and treat condition-agnostic models as an informative lower bracket that by construction cannot represent shifts.", "Synthetic equilibria give exact control but may differ from what models saw in training, risking off-distribution behavior; we cross-validate with curated real equilibria (acid-base, esterification, Haber) and report sensitivity to synthetic-vs-real agreement.", "Real equilibrium constants and enthalpy signs are condition-dependent and noisy; we use well-characterized reactions, restrict decisive tests to cases with confident K and dH signs, and report sensitivity to thermodynamic-data confidence.", "Linear probes recovering a shift-direction axis do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/identity-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and delta-representations.", "A negative result (models encode static reaction identity but not the directional Le Chatelier shift, failing the stress-direction counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting reaction models for reaction design, yield optimization, and process chemistry - where predicting how conditions shift equilibrium is central; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether reaction foundation models represent Le Chatelier equilibrium shifts.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public/synthetic equilibrium datasets, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
