Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "orphan_feature_triage", "Title": "Signal or Artifact? A Prospective, Externally-Grounded Protocol for Triaging Unannotated Discovery Features in Scientific Foundation Models", "Short Hypothesis": "Sparse-autoencoder features of scientific foundation models that do NOT map onto any existing annotation ('orphan features') fall into two separable classes: (a) genuine latent scientific signals that predict independent, held-out external measurements the model never saw, and (b) technical/dataset artifacts. We hypothesize these two classes are distinguishable in advance by a small set of cheap, model-internal and cross-modal criteria - external-modality predictivity, cross-dataset stability, and causal ablation specificity - yielding a reusable triage protocol that turns interpretability from post-hoc confirmation into prospective, validated discovery.", "Related Work": "A wave of dictionary-learning work extracts SAE features from scientific FMs and validates them against KNOWN annotations: InterPLM (2024) and Adams et al. (2025) on protein LMs, Discovering Interpretable Biological Concepts in scRNA-seq FMs (2025), SAEs Reveal Interpretable Features in Single-Cell FMs (2025), ICFL on microscopy FMs (2024), and Open-Ended Visual Scientific Discovery with SAEs (2025). All of these note that MANY features do NOT map to existing annotations, and InterPLM/ICFL propose auto-interpretation to label them - but auto-interpretation still hallucinates and cannot distinguish a real undiscovered signal from an artifact. The Pleiades Alzheimer's biomarker study (2026) shows interpretability CAN surface a genuinely novel biomarker class (fragmentomics), but it is a single bespoke success validated by heavy downstream work, not a general triage rule. 'On the definition and importance of interpretability in SciML' (2025) argues that without prior knowledge one cannot tell whether a discovered structure is meaningful. The critical, unaddressed gap is a GENERAL, PROSPECTIVE decision procedure that, for an arbitrary orphan (unannotated) feature, predicts whether it is a real latent signal or an artifact BEFORE expensive follow-up. Our protocol - using cross-modal external ground truth the model never ingested plus cross-dataset stability and causal ablation as a triage classifier - is that missing piece, and is distinct from all prior annotation-matching and single-case discovery work.", "Abstract": "Sparse autoencoders now routinely extract thousands of interpretable features from scientific foundation models of proteins, cells, and images, and the field has begun to celebrate features that align with known biological concepts. But every such study reports a large fraction of 'orphan' features that map onto no existing annotation, and interpretability's promise for discovery hinges precisely on these: some are undiscovered scientific signals, others are dataset artifacts (batch effects, sequencing depth, staining, plate position), and current practice - auto-interpretation by a stronger LLM - cannot reliably tell them apart. We propose the first general, prospective triage protocol that decides, for an arbitrary unannotated feature, whether it is a genuine latent signal or an artifact, and validates the decision against independent evidence the model never saw. Working with public single-cell RNA-seq foundation models (scGPT, Geneformer) and protein language models (ESM-2), we train SAEs, remove features matching known annotations, and score the remaining orphans on three cheap criteria: (1) external-modality predictivity - does the feature predict a held-out measurement from a DIFFERENT modality/assay the model was never trained on (e.g., a scRNA feature predicting matched CITE-seq surface-protein levels, or an ESM-2 feature predicting an experimental deep-mutational-scanning quantity)? (2) cross-dataset stability - does an equivalent feature reappear across independent datasets/labs? (3) causal ablation specificity - does ablating it selectively change model outputs for a coherent biological subset, not diffusely? We test whether these three criteria jointly separate a curated set of known-signal orphans from planted-artifact orphans, calibrate a triage classifier, and then apply it prospectively to surface and externally confirm candidate novel signals. The result is a reusable instrument that converts interpretability from confirmation into validated discovery.", "Experiments": ["1. Setup and feature extraction: Train sparse autoencoders (TopK/standard) on hidden representations of two scRNA-seq FMs (scGPT, scFoundation or Geneformer) and a protein LM (ESM-2 650M), following InterPLM/single-cell SAE recipes. For each SAE, obtain the feature dictionary and per-sample feature activations. All model access is inference-only plus SAE training (academic-scale).", "2. Annotation matching and orphan set: Match each feature to known annotations (cell type, tissue, cell-cycle phase, GO terms, secondary structure, binding sites) via activation-vs-label alignment (F1/AUROC), following prior work. Features exceeding a strong match threshold are 'annotated'; the remainder form the ORPHAN set to be triaged.", "3. Curated ground-truth labels for triage development: Construct a labeled development set of orphans with known status. KNOWN-SIGNAL orphans: features that correlate with a scientifically real quantity that was NOT in the annotation set used above (e.g., a validated signature held out on purpose). PLANTED-ARTIFACT orphans: identify features that align with technical covariates - sequencing depth, batch/lab ID, plate/well position, staining intensity, mitochondrial fraction - which are real artifacts. This gives a two-class development set for calibrating the triage criteria.", "4. Criterion 1 - external-modality predictivity: For each orphan, test whether its activation predicts a HELD-OUT measurement from a different modality the model never ingested. scRNA case: use paired CITE-seq datasets (matched cells with surface-protein antibody readouts) and multi-omic (ATAC) data - does the orphan predict surface-protein or chromatin-accessibility values beyond what matched annotated features predict? Protein case: does an ESM-2 orphan predict an experimental deep-mutational-scanning fitness or a biophysical measurement (stability ddG) not derivable from sequence annotations? Metric: cross-modal predictive R^2/AUROC, residualized against annotated features.", "5. Criterion 2 - cross-dataset stability: Train independent SAEs on independent datasets from different labs/protocols and test whether an orphan has a stable counterpart (high activation-correlation / matched top-activating examples) across datasets. Metric: cross-dataset stability score. Hypothesis: genuine signals are stable; artifacts are dataset-specific (or, if a stable artifact, it will co-localize with a technical covariate, caught by Criterion 4).", "6. Criterion 3 - causal ablation specificity: Ablate each orphan feature in the SAE reconstruction and measure how the model's downstream outputs change. Metric: specificity index = concentration of the ablation effect on a coherent biological subset (cell type / protein family) versus diffuse change; combined with whether the affected subset aligns with a technical covariate. Genuine signals produce coherent, biology-aligned effects; artifacts produce covariate-aligned or diffuse effects.", "7. Triage classifier and calibration: Fit a simple, interpretable classifier (logistic regression) on the three criteria to separate known-signal from planted-artifact orphans on the development set. Report AUROC, precision/recall, and per-criterion ablations (which criterion carries the signal). Cross-validate across model families and modalities to test generality of the triage rule.", "8. Prospective discovery and external confirmation: Apply the calibrated triage to the remaining unlabeled orphans, rank the top predicted 'genuine signals', and attempt EXTERNAL confirmation not used in triage - e.g., enrichment against a newer/independent database, matched-cell external assay, or a small held-out DMS set. Report how many top-ranked orphans confirm versus a random-orphan baseline (discovery precision) and present 2-3 candidate novel signals as case studies.", "9. Baseline comparison: Compare the triage protocol against the current default - LLM auto-interpretation (Claude/GPT labeling of top-activating examples) - on the same development set, testing whether auto-interpretation confidence predicts genuine-vs-artifact status (we expect it does not, motivating the protocol)."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["The 'known-signal vs planted-artifact' development labels may be imperfect; some presumed artifacts could carry biology and vice versa. We mitigate by using clearly technical covariates (depth, batch, plate) for artifacts and independently-validated signatures for signals, and report sensitivity to label noise.", "Paired cross-modal data (CITE-seq/multi-omic matched cells, DMS with sequence) is limited in quantity and coverage, which constrains Criterion 1; we use the best available public paired datasets and treat cross-modal predictivity as one of three criteria rather than the sole arbiter.", "Some genuine signals may be dataset-specific (cross-dataset stability fails) and some artifacts may be stable across labs; the three criteria are designed to be complementary so no single failure is decisive, but the triage classifier may still misclassify edge cases - we report calibration and error analysis rather than claim perfect separation.", "SAE features can be split/merged across dictionaries, complicating cross-dataset matching; we use matched-top-example and activation-correlation matching and report robustness to SAE hyperparameters and width.", "Ablation in SAE reconstruction may push activations off-manifold; we bound edits and report specificity relative to matched random-feature ablations.", "External confirmation of prospectively-surfaced novel signals is inherently harder to guarantee within one paper; we frame the contribution primarily as a validated TRIAGE INSTRUMENT (evaluated on the development set with ground truth) plus a small number of externally-confirmed case studies, so the paper stands even if only a few novel signals confirm.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus academic-scale SAE training and public paired datasets, keeping compute feasible for an academic lab."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'orphan_feature_triage', 'Title': 'Signal or Artifact? A Prospective, Externally-Grounded Protocol for Triaging Unannotated Discovery Features in Scientific Foundation Models', 'Short Hypothesis': "Sparse-autoencoder features of scientific foundation models that do NOT map onto any existing annotation ('orphan features') fall into two separable classes: (a) genuine latent scientific signals that predict independent, held-out external measurements the model never saw, and (b) technical/dataset artifacts. We hypothesize these two classes are distinguishable in advance by a small set of cheap, model-internal and cross-modal criteria - external-modality predictivity, cross-dataset stability, and causal ablation specificity - yielding a reusable triage protocol that turns interpretability from post-hoc confirmation into prospective, validated discovery.", 'Related Work': "A wave of dictionary-learning work extracts SAE features from scientific FMs and validates them against KNOWN annotations: InterPLM (2024) and Adams et al. (2025) on protein LMs, Discovering Interpretable Biological Concepts in scRNA-seq FMs (2025), SAEs Reveal Interpretable Features in Single-Cell FMs (2025), ICFL on microscopy FMs (2024), and Open-Ended Visual Scientific Discovery with SAEs (2025). All of these note that MANY features do NOT map to existing annotations, and InterPLM/ICFL propose auto-interpretation to label them - but auto-interpretation still hallucinates and cannot distinguish a real undiscovered signal from an artifact. The Pleiades Alzheimer's biomarker study (2026) shows interpretability CAN surface a genuinely novel biomarker class (fragmentomics), but it is a single bespoke success validated by heavy downstream work, not a general triage rule. 'On the definition and importance of interpretability in SciML' (2025) argues that without prior knowledge one cannot tell whether a discovered structure is meaningful. The critical, unaddressed gap is a GENERAL, PROSPECTIVE decision procedure that, for an arbitrary orphan (unannotated) feature, predicts whether it is a real latent signal or an artifact BEFORE expensive follow-up. Our protocol - using cross-modal external ground truth the model never ingested plus cross-dataset stability and causal ablation as a triage classifier - is that missing piece, and is distinct from all prior annotation-matching and single-case discovery work.", 'Abstract': "Sparse autoencoders now routinely extract thousands of interpretable features from scientific foundation models of proteins, cells, and images, and the field has begun to celebrate features that align with known biological concepts. But every such study reports a large fraction of 'orphan' features that map onto no existing annotation, and interpretability's promise for discovery hinges precisely on these: some are undiscovered scientific signals, others are dataset artifacts (batch effects, sequencing depth, staining, plate position), and current practice - auto-interpretation by a stronger LLM - cannot reliably tell them apart. We propose the first general, prospective triage protocol that decides, for an arbitrary unannotated feature, whether it is a genuine latent signal or an artifact, and validates the decision against independent evidence the model never saw. Working with public single-cell RNA-seq foundation models (scGPT, Geneformer) and protein language models (ESM-2), we train SAEs, remove features matching known annotations, and score the remaining orphans on three cheap criteria: (1) external-modality predictivity - does the feature predict a held-out measurement from a DIFFERENT modality/assay the model was never trained on (e.g., a scRNA feature predicting matched CITE-seq surface-protein levels, or an ESM-2 feature predicting an experimental deep-mutational-scanning quantity)? (2) cross-dataset stability - does an equivalent feature reappear across independent datasets/labs? (3) causal ablation specificity - does ablating it selectively change model outputs for a coherent biological subset, not diffusely? We test whether these three criteria jointly separate a curated set of known-signal orphans from planted-artifact orphans, calibrate a triage classifier, and then apply it prospectively to surface and externally confirm candidate novel signals. The result is a reusable instrument that converts interpretability from confirmation into validated discovery.", 'Experiments': ['1. Setup and feature extraction: Train sparse autoencoders (TopK/standard) on hidden representations of two scRNA-seq FMs (scGPT, scFoundation or Geneformer) and a protein LM (ESM-2 650M), following InterPLM/single-cell SAE recipes. For each SAE, obtain the feature dictionary and per-sample feature activations. All model access is inference-only plus SAE training (academic-scale).', "2. Annotation matching and orphan set: Match each feature to known annotations (cell type, tissue, cell-cycle phase, GO terms, secondary structure, binding sites) via activation-vs-label alignment (F1/AUROC), following prior work. Features exceeding a strong match threshold are 'annotated'; the remainder form the ORPHAN set to be triaged.", '3. Curated ground-truth labels for triage development: Construct a labeled development set of orphans with known status. KNOWN-SIGNAL orphans: features that correlate with a scientifically real quantity that was NOT in the annotation set used above (e.g., a validated signature held out on purpose). PLANTED-ARTIFACT orphans: identify features that align with technical covariates - sequencing depth, batch/lab ID, plate/well position, staining intensity, mitochondrial fraction - which are real artifacts. This gives a two-class development set for calibrating the triage criteria.', '4. Criterion 1 - external-modality predictivity: For each orphan, test whether its activation predicts a HELD-OUT measurement from a different modality the model never ingested. scRNA case: use paired CITE-seq datasets (matched cells with surface-protein antibody readouts) and multi-omic (ATAC) data - does the orphan predict surface-protein or chromatin-accessibility values beyond what matched annotated features predict? Protein case: does an ESM-2 orphan predict an experimental deep-mutational-scanning fitness or a biophysical measurement (stability ddG) not derivable from sequence annotations? Metric: cross-modal predictive R^2/AUROC, residualized against annotated features.', '5. Criterion 2 - cross-dataset stability: Train independent SAEs on independent datasets from different labs/protocols and test whether an orphan has a stable counterpart (high activation-correlation / matched top-activating examples) across datasets. Metric: cross-dataset stability score. Hypothesis: genuine signals are stable; artifacts are dataset-specific (or, if a stable artifact, it will co-localize with a technical covariate, caught by Criterion 4).', "6. Criterion 3 - causal ablation specificity: Ablate each orphan feature in the SAE reconstruction and measure how the model's downstream outputs change. Metric: specificity index = concentration of the ablation effect on a coherent biological subset (cell type / protein family) versus diffuse change; combined with whether the affected subset aligns with a technical covariate. Genuine signals produce coherent, biology-aligned effects; artifacts produce covariate-aligned or diffuse effects.", '7. Triage classifier and calibration: Fit a simple, interpretable classifier (logistic regression) on the three criteria to separate known-signal from planted-artifact orphans on the development set. Report AUROC, precision/recall, and per-criterion ablations (which criterion carries the signal). Cross-validate across model families and modalities to test generality of the triage rule.', "8. Prospective discovery and external confirmation: Apply the calibrated triage to the remaining unlabeled orphans, rank the top predicted 'genuine signals', and attempt EXTERNAL confirmation not used in triage - e.g., enrichment against a newer/independent database, matched-cell external assay, or a small held-out DMS set. Report how many top-ranked orphans confirm versus a random-orphan baseline (discovery precision) and present 2-3 candidate novel signals as case studies.", '9. Baseline comparison: Compare the triage protocol against the current default - LLM auto-interpretation (Claude/GPT labeling of top-activating examples) - on the same development set, testing whether auto-interpretation confidence predicts genuine-vs-artifact status (we expect it does not, motivating the protocol).'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ["The 'known-signal vs planted-artifact' development labels may be imperfect; some presumed artifacts could carry biology and vice versa. We mitigate by using clearly technical covariates (depth, batch, plate) for artifacts and independently-validated signatures for signals, and report sensitivity to label noise.", 'Paired cross-modal data (CITE-seq/multi-omic matched cells, DMS with sequence) is limited in quantity and coverage, which constrains Criterion 1; we use the best available public paired datasets and treat cross-modal predictivity as one of three criteria rather than the sole arbiter.', 'Some genuine signals may be dataset-specific (cross-dataset stability fails) and some artifacts may be stable across labs; the three criteria are designed to be complementary so no single failure is decisive, but the triage classifier may still misclassify edge cases - we report calibration and error analysis rather than claim perfect separation.', 'SAE features can be split/merged across dictionaries, complicating cross-dataset matching; we use matched-top-example and activation-correlation matching and report robustness to SAE hyperparameters and width.', 'Ablation in SAE reconstruction may push activations off-manifold; we bound edits and report specificity relative to matched random-feature ablations.', 'External confirmation of prospectively-surfaced novel signals is inherently harder to guarantee within one paper; we frame the contribution primarily as a validated TRIAGE INSTRUMENT (evaluated on the development set with ground truth) plus a small number of externally-confirmed case studies, so the paper stands even if only a few novel signals confirm.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus academic-scale SAE training and public paired datasets, keeping compute feasible for an academic lab.']}

