Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "space_group_absence_probe", "Title": "Forbidden Reflections: Testing Whether Crystal Foundation Models Represent Space-Group Symmetry Operations and Their Systematic-Absence Selection Rules, Not Just Atomic Coordinate Statistics", "Short Hypothesis": "Crystal structure is governed by an exactly-known discrete-group mechanism: the crystallographic space group, whose symmetry operations (screw axes, glide planes, centering) impose exact selection rules on diffraction \u2014 systematic absences (certain reflections have identically zero intensity) \u2014 and fix atomic multiplicities/degeneracies via Wyckoff positions. These are relational consequences of the group operations, not readable from any single atom's coordinates. We hypothesize that if a crystal foundation model fed only raw atomic positions (no symmetry labels) represents the space-group mechanism rather than coordinate statistics, then (a) space-group symmetry class and systematic-absence structure are decodable from internal representations beyond what local coordinate/composition features predict, (b) a symmetry-breaking counterfactual \u2014 a tiny displacement that lifts a systematic absence (turns a forbidden reflection allowed) at nearly-fixed geometry \u2014 shifts the internal symmetry signal, whereas a symmetry-preserving displacement of matched magnitude does not, and (c) steering a recovered symmetry-operation direction causally reshapes symmetry-dependent predictions. The absence-lifting vs symmetry-preserving counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that coordinate-magnitude correlation cannot pass.", "Related Work": "Two lines bracket the gap. (1) Symmetry is BUILT IN by construction: space-group-equivariant generative models (SGEquiDiff 2025, Space Group Conditional Flow Matching 2025, DiffCSP++ 2024) and equivariant property predictors constrain outputs to obey the group by design; they never ask whether a symmetry-agnostic model internally discovers it. (2) Space group is PREDICTED as a supervised label from XRD patterns (Suzuki 2020; MSDN 2020; contrastive powder-XRD 2025) or from composition (Cosy 2024) \u2014 these are classifiers, not tests of what a coordinate-based foundation model internally encodes. Interpretability of crystal models (CGCNN interpretability 2018 extracting site energies; Site-Net attention 2022; 'Comparing latent features of uMLIPs' 2025; my own periodic-law and convex-hull probes) recovers pointwise/energetic or generic latent structure. 'On the Symmetries of Deep Learning Models and their Internal Representations' (2022) and 'Interpreting Equivariant Representations' (2024) analyze architectural (weight-space) symmetry, not whether a model represents the DATA's crystallographic space group. Crucially, no work tests whether a general crystal FM's internal representation encodes the space-group OPERATIONS and their exact systematic-absence selection rules, whether that representation is causally load-bearing, or uses an absence-lifting counterfactual to separate the symmetry mechanism from coordinate statistics. That relational, causal, selection-rule-grounded test is our novel contribution.", "Abstract": "The atomic arrangement of every crystal is organized by an exactly-known discrete-group mechanism: its crystallographic space group. The group's symmetry operations \u2014 screw axes, glide planes, and lattice centering \u2014 have precise, testable consequences: they force systematic absences (specific diffraction reflections are identically zero) and fix atomic multiplicities through Wyckoff positions. These are relational properties of the whole structure, invisible in any single atom's coordinates, making space-group symmetry an ideal mechanism-defining probe for whether crystal foundation models learn crystallographic mechanism or merely coordinate statistics \u2014 especially models fed only raw positions with no symmetry labels. We probe pretrained crystal foundation models (universal MLIP backbones such as MACE-MP-0 and a crystal representation FM such as CLOUD/CGCNN) on Materials Project structures with known space groups and computable structure factors. First, a symmetry-beyond-coordinates probe: are the space-group class and systematic-absence pattern decodable from internal representations beyond what local coordinate and composition features predict? Second, and most decisively, an absence-lifting counterfactual: we apply a tiny atomic displacement that breaks a symmetry operation and turns a systematically-absent (forbidden) reflection allowed, holding geometry nearly fixed, versus a symmetry-preserving displacement of matched magnitude; a mechanism-representing model must shift its internal symmetry signal for the absence-lifting edit but not the preserving one, while a coordinate-statistics model responds to displacement magnitude regardless. Third, causal steering of a recovered symmetry-operation direction. Metrics quantify a symmetry-beyond-coordinates index, an absence-lifting causal asymmetry, and a Wyckoff-multiplicity fidelity test. Either outcome sharpens whether deployed crystal models represent the symmetry that governs materials properties.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load pretrained crystal foundation models with public weights and usable internal representations \u2014 a universal MLIP (MACE-MP-0 / SevenNet) and a crystal representation FM (CGCNN / CLOUD) \u2014 all of which ingest raw atomic coordinates and species with NO explicit space-group input. From the Materials Project, assemble structures spanning the 230 space groups (balanced across crystal systems where possible), each with its space group, Wyckoff assignments, and computed structure factors / systematic-absence pattern (from the space-group extinction rules). Extract per-structure and per-atom internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode crystal system, point group, and space group from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded and select best layers. Establish strict baselines: predict the same labels from local coordinate/composition features only (nearest-neighbor distances, angles, element counts) to quantify how much symmetry information the model contributes beyond trivial correlates.", "3. Symmetry-beyond-coordinates probe (primary probe): Test whether the systematic-absence pattern (which reflections are forbidden, the diagnostic fingerprint of screw/glide/centering operations) is decodable from internal representations BEYOND local coordinate features. Regress out the coordinate/composition-predictable component and re-decode the absence pattern (and space-group class) from the residual. Primary metric: Symmetry-Beyond-Coordinates Index = decoding accuracy (absence-pattern F1 / space-group accuracy) from the model representation minus what local coordinate features achieve. A mechanism-representing model carries relational symmetry information; a coordinate-statistics model does not exceed the baseline.", "4. Absence-lifting counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For structures with a symmetry operation that produces a systematic absence (e.g., a 2_1 screw axis forcing 0k0 reflections with odd k to be absent), construct two matched atomic-displacement edits of equal RMSD. (a) Absence-lifting edit: displace atoms in a way that breaks the specific operation, making the previously-forbidden reflection acquire nonzero structure factor (verified by recomputing structure factors) \u2014 the space group is lowered. (b) Symmetry-preserving control edit: displace atoms by the same RMSD in a way that preserves the operation (the reflection stays absent) \u2014 e.g., a symmetric distortion consistent with the space group. Feed both to the model and measure the change in its internal symmetry signal (probed space-group/absence readout, or symmetry-dependent property head). Metric: Absence-Lifting Causal Asymmetry = |symmetry-signal change under absence-lifting edit| / |change under matched symmetry-preserving edit|. Mechanistic representation => asymmetry >> 1 (responds to the symmetry change, not the raw displacement); coordinate-statistics representation => asymmetry ~ 1.", "5. Wyckoff-multiplicity fidelity: Test whether the model's representation encodes that symmetry-equivalent atoms (same Wyckoff orbit) are related \u2014 e.g., whether it treats atoms on a high-multiplicity special position as a coupled, degenerate set. Perturb one atom of a symmetry-equivalent set versus a general-position atom and test whether the model's response respects the multiplicity/degeneracy structure predicted by the space group. Metric: correlation between the model's induced response pattern and the Wyckoff-predicted equivalence structure; shuffled-orbit controls.", "6. Causal symmetry-operation steering: Recover a symmetry-class direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between a high-symmetry structure and its symmetry-broken counterpart). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's symmetry-dependent readout (space-group class, or a property known to depend on symmetry such as piezoelectric/optical activity requiring non-centrosymmetry) shifts in the symmetry-consistent direction, while matched-norm random directions and a coordinate-magnitude direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of symmetry direction| / |effect of random direction|. A decisive sub-test: steer toward/away from centrosymmetry and check whether the model's centrosymmetry-gated property predictions turn on/off accordingly.", "7. Failure-prediction link: Test whether an internal 'symmetry-representation strength' score (symmetry-beyond-coordinates confidence / absence-lifting sensitivity) predicts, per structure, where the model mis-predicts symmetry-dependent properties \u2014 especially near symmetry-boundary cases (pseudo-symmetric structures, low-symmetry distortions of high-symmetry parents) where a coordinate shortcut misleads. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error structures from the internal signal, vs coordinate-only and output-confidence baselines.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where space-group/absence structure emerges across depth and whether recovered symmetry subspaces align across the different crystal foundation models (CKA), assessing whether crystallographic symmetry is a convergent internal property or absent/model-specific. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a space-group-equivariant model (SGEquiDiff/DiffCSP++ representations) that encodes the group by construction, and (lower bound) a coordinate-only classifier with no relational access, placing coordinate-based FMs on the coordinate-statistics-to-symmetry-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Space-group class correlates with local coordinate statistics (high-symmetry structures have characteristic bond geometries), making the symmetry-beyond-coordinates separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with coordinate-residualization, the absence-lifting counterfactual (which holds RMSD fixed and varies only the symmetry operation), the symmetry-preserving control edit, and the Wyckoff-multiplicity double dissociation.", "The absence-lifting and symmetry-preserving edits are synthetic distortions; unrealistic displacements may push structures off the model's training distribution, confounding the symmetry response with generic out-of-distribution failure. We mitigate with small displacements, verify the intended absence change via recomputed structure factors, and compare edits of matched RMSD so any generic OOD effect is shared, reporting sensitivity to displacement magnitude.", "Equivariant MLIP features are tensorial; probing must respect invariant vs equivariant channels. We focus probes on invariant scalar channels first and report per-channel results, and note that a strongly equivariant model may encode symmetry implicitly in a way that requires careful readout.", "Some crystal FMs pool to a single structure embedding, limiting per-atom Wyckoff analysis; we prioritize models exposing per-atom representations for the multiplicity test and report pooling caveats, using whole-structure probes as a coarser fallback.", "Ground-truth space-group and Wyckoff assignments from Materials Project can have tolerance-dependent ambiguity for near-symmetric structures; we use standard symmetry-finder tolerances, restrict decisive tests to unambiguous cases, and report sensitivity to the symmetry tolerance.", "Linear probes recovering a symmetry direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/coordinate-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions \u2014 we use both probe directions and delta-representations.", "A negative result (models encode coordinate statistics but not the space-group symmetry mechanism, failing the absence-lifting counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting crystal foundation models for symmetry-dependent properties (ferroelectricity, piezoelectricity, optical activity, all gated by point-group/centrosymmetry) and for symmetry-aware materials discovery; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether coordinate-based crystal FMs represent crystallographic space-group operations and their systematic-absence selection rules.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public Materials Project data with standard structure-factor/symmetry tools, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
