 group from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded and select best layers. Establish strict baselines: predict the same labels from local coordinate/composition features only (nearest-neighbor distances, angles, element counts) to quantify how much symmetry information the model contributes beyond trivial correlates.', '3. Symmetry-beyond-coordinates probe (primary probe): Test whether the systematic-absence pattern (which reflections are forbidden, the diagnostic fingerprint of screw/glide/centering operations) is decodable from internal representations BEYOND local coordinate features. Regress out the coordinate/composition-predictable component and re-decode the absence pattern (and space-group class) from the residual. Primary metric: Symmetry-Beyond-Coordinates Index = decoding accuracy (absence-pattern F1 / space-group accuracy) from the model representation minus what local coordinate features achieve. A mechanism-representing model carries relational symmetry information; a coordinate-statistics model does not exceed the baseline.', '4. Absence-lifting counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For structures with a symmetry operation that produces a systematic absence (e.g., a 2_1 screw axis forcing 0k0 reflections with odd k to be absent), construct two matched atomic-displacement edits of equal RMSD. (a) Absence-lifting edit: displace atoms in a way that breaks the specific operation, making the previously-forbidden reflection acquire nonzero structure factor (verified by recomputing structure factors) — the space group is lowered. (b) Symmetry-preserving control edit: displace atoms by the same RMSD in a way that preserves the operation (the reflection stays absent) — e.g., a symmetric distortion consistent with the space group. Feed both to the model and measure the change in its internal symmetry signal (probed space-group/absence readout, or symmetry-dependent property head). Metric: Absence-Lifting Causal Asymmetry = |symmetry-signal change under absence-lifting edit| / |change under matched symmetry-preserving edit|. Mechanistic representation => asymmetry >> 1 (responds to the symmetry change, not the raw displacement); coordinate-statistics representation => asymmetry ~ 1.', "5. Wyckoff-multiplicity fidelity: Test whether the model's representation encodes that symmetry-equivalent atoms (same Wyckoff orbit) are related — e.g., whether it treats atoms on a high-multiplicity special position as a coupled, degenerate set. Perturb one atom of a symmetry-equivalent set versus a general-position atom and test whether the model's response respects the multiplicity/degeneracy structure predicted by the space group. Metric: correlation between the model's induced response pattern and the Wyckoff-predicted equivalence structure; shuffled-orbit controls.", "6. Causal symmetry-operation steering: Recover a symmetry-class direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between a high-symmetry structure and its symmetry-broken counterpart). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's symmetry-dependent readout (space-group class, or a property known to depend on symmetry such as piezoelectric/optical activity requiring non-centrosymmetry) shifts in the symmetry-consistent direction, while matched-norm random directions and a coordinate-magnitude direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of symmetry direction| / |effect of random direction|. A decisive sub-test: steer toward/away from centrosymmetry and check whether the model's centrosymmetry-gated property predictions turn on/off accordingly.", "7. Failure-prediction link: Test whether an internal 'symmetry-representation strength' score (symmetry-beyond-coordinates confidence / absence-lifting sensitivity) predicts, per structure, where the model mis-predicts symmetry-dependent properties — especially near symmetry-boundary cases (pseudo-symmetric structures, low-symmetry distortions of high-symmetry parents) where a coordinate shortcut misleads. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error structures from the internal signal, vs coordinate-only and output-confidence baselines.", '8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where space-group/absence structure emerges across depth and whether recovered symmetry subspaces align across the different crystal foundation models (CKA), assessing whether crystallographic symmetry is a convergent internal property or absent/model-specific. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a space-group-equivariant model (SGEquiDiff/DiffCSP++ representations) that encodes the group by construction, and (lower bound) a coordinate-only classifier with no relational access, placing coordinate-based FMs on the coordinate-statistics-to-symmetry-mechanism spectrum.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Space-group class correlates with local coordinate statistics (high-symmetry structures have characteristic bond geometries), making the symmetry-beyond-coordinates separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with coordinate-residualization, the absence-lifting counterfactual (which holds RMSD fixed and varies only the symmetry operation), the symmetry-preserving control edit, and the Wyckoff-multiplicity double dissociation.', "The absence-lifting and symmetry-preserving edits are synthetic distortions; unrealistic displacements may push structures off the model's training distribution, confounding the symmetry response with generic out-of-distribution failure. We mitigate with small displacements, verify the intended absence change via recomputed structure factors, and compare edits of matched RMSD so any generic OOD effect is shared, reporting sensitivity to displacement magnitude.", 'Equivariant MLIP features are tensorial; probing must respect invariant vs equivariant channels. We focus probes on invariant scalar channels first and report per-channel results, and note that a strongly equivariant model may encode symmetry implicitly in a way that requires careful readout.', 'Some crystal FMs pool to a single structure embedding, limiting per-atom Wyckoff analysis; we prioritize models exposing per-atom representations for the multiplicity test and report pooling caveats, using whole-structure probes as a coarser fallback.', 'Ground-truth space-group and Wyckoff assignments from Materials Project can have tolerance-dependent ambiguity for near-symmetric structures; we use standard symmetry-finder tolerances, restrict decisive tests to unambiguous cases, and report sensitivity to the symmetry tolerance.', 'Linear probes recovering a symmetry direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/coordinate-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions — we use both probe directions and delta-representations.', 'A negative result (models encode coordinate statistics but not the space-group symmetry mechanism, failing the absence-lifting counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting crystal foundation models for symmetry-dependent properties (ferroelectricity, piezoelectricity, optical activity, all gated by point-group/centrosymmetry) and for symmetry-aware materials discovery; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether coordinate-based crystal FMs represent crystallographic space-group operations and their systematic-absence selection rules.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public Materials Project data with standard structure-factor/symmetry tools, keeping compute at academic scale.']}

