Response Content: {"total": 1, "offset": 0, "data": [{"paperId": "4d2979a7aec9d207954b92cf7c18d8009b1b6ff3", "title": "The importance of diabatic processes for the dynamics of synoptic-scale extratropical weather systems \u2013 a review", "venue": "Weather and Climate Dynamics", "year": 2024, "citationCount": 22, "openAccessPdf": {"url": "https://doi.org/10.5194/wcd-5-1299-2024", "status": "GOLD", "license": "CCBY", "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/10.5194/wcd-5-1
Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "lagrangian_pv_conservation_probe", "Title": "Conserved Along the Flow? Testing Whether Weather Foundation Models Represent Potential Vorticity as a Lagrangian Material Invariant, Not Just a Snapshot Field", "Short Hypothesis": "Potential vorticity (PV) is defined by an exactly-known relational mechanism: it is a material Lagrangian invariant, exactly conserved FOLLOWING an air parcel in adiabatic/frictionless flow, and modified only by diabatic/frictional processes in a specific, quantifiable way. A single snapshot of PV is directionless about conservation; the invariant only exists along trajectories. We hypothesize that if a weather foundation model represents this mechanism rather than storing PV as an instantaneous field, then (a) the model's internal PV signal is conserved along its OWN predicted parcel trajectories in adiabatic regions, (b) a diabatic-vs-adiabatic counterfactual makes the internal PV drift specifically where latent heating/friction act (and stay conserved elsewhere), and (c) steering a recovered 'diabatic-PV-source' direction causally injects PV anomalies in the physically-correct sense. The along-trajectory conservation and diabatic-source counterfactual are exact, mechanism-defining tests that snapshot-field decodability cannot pass.", "Related Work": "Weather-model interpretability recovers single instantaneous features and steers them: SAEs on GraphCast (2025) find tropical cyclones, atmospheric rivers, sea-ice; GraphDOP work (2025) shows a unified world model inferring non-local fields (jet streams) and applies FSOI data-assimilation diagnostics; Physics Steering (2025) steers vorticity/diffusion directions; 'Uncovering Emergent Physics Representations' (2025) finds steerable energy features. All recover a quantity at a point/instant. Separately, atmospheric dynamics establishes PV as a MATERIAL Lagrangian invariant (Haynes-McIntyre 1990 impermeability/conservation theorems; Lagrangian PV-tracer conservation tests 1994; PV-tracer diabatic-attribution technique 2015/2019; Wernli-Gray 2024 review of diabatic PV modification), exactly conserved along trajectories except for quantifiable diabatic/frictional sources. Crucially, no interpretability work tests whether a weather foundation model's INTERNAL representation encodes PV as a trajectory-conserved invariant with its diabatic-source structure, whether that is causally load-bearing, or uses a diabatic-vs-adiabatic counterfactual to separate the Lagrangian-conservation mechanism from a mere snapshot field. Prior work answers 'is a quantity encoded'; the material-invariant question asks 'is it conserved following the flow, and modified only by the right processes'. Our along-trajectory conservation probe, diabatic-source counterfactual, and causal PV-source steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, distinct from spatial-balance or total-integral conservation tests.", "Abstract": "Data-driven weather foundation models now rival physical solvers, and interpretability studies show they encode recognizable instantaneous fields such as cyclones, vorticity, and energy. But atmospheric dynamics is defined by relational invariants, chief among them potential vorticity (PV): a material Lagrangian quantity that is exactly conserved following an air parcel in adiabatic, frictionless flow, and altered only by diabatic heating and friction in a precise, attributable way. A single snapshot cannot express this conservation mechanism, which lives along trajectories, making PV an ideal mechanism-defining probe. We ask whether pretrained weather foundation models (GraphCast and Pangu-Weather) internally represent PV as a trajectory-conserved invariant rather than an instantaneous field. Using ERA5 and the model's own rollouts, we first probe whether PV is decodable from internal representations. Then, most decisively, we test along-trajectory conservation: following the model's predicted parcel trajectories, does the internal PV signal remain constant in adiabatic regions and drift only where diabatic processes act? A diabatic-vs-adiabatic counterfactual perturbs latent heating in a target region and tests whether the internal PV drift appears specifically there, with the physically-correct sign, while remote adiabatic parcels stay conserved; a spatially-scrambled control of matched magnitude should not. Finally, causal steering of a recovered diabatic-PV-source direction should inject PV anomalies in the correct sense. Metrics quantify along-trajectory conservation, a diabatic-vs-adiabatic drift asymmetry, and steering selectivity. Either outcome sharpens whether operational weather models represent the material invariant behind extratropical dynamics.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load pretrained GraphCast and Pangu-Weather (public weights). Assemble ERA5 fields (winds, temperature, geopotential, humidity) across seasons and regions. Compute ground-truth Ertel PV on isentropic surfaces at each timestep, air-parcel trajectories (from the wind field), and diabatic tendencies (latent heating from moisture/precipitation, friction near the surface) so adiabatic vs diabatic regions are labeled. Extract per-grid internal representations across layers/blocks. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode PV (and its constituents, absolute vorticity and static stability) from internal representations across layers; confirm PV is encoded and select best layers. Establish a snapshot-field baseline (PV predictable from the instantaneous field itself).", "3. Along-trajectory conservation test (primary): During the model's own autoregressive rollout, follow predicted parcel trajectories and read the internal PV signal (probed PV) at successive positions along each trajectory. In adiabatic regions (away from latent heating/friction), measure the drift of the probed PV following the parcel (coefficient of variation / slope along the trajectory). Compare against (a) the drift of the model's actual predicted-field PV and (b) the true Lagrangian PV (approximately constant). Primary metric: internal-representation Lagrangian conservation error = along-trajectory drift of probed PV in adiabatic parcels; a mechanistic representation shows near-zero drift following the flow, beyond what Eulerian (fixed-point) PV variance would predict.", "4. Diabatic lawful-drift test: In diabatic regions (latent-heating cores, boundary-layer friction), test whether the probed PV changes along trajectories in the physically-correct sign and magnitude (PV generated below and destroyed above a latent-heating maximum, per the PV-tracer diabatic-attribution framework). Metric: Spearman correlation and sign agreement between along-trajectory probed-PV change and the true diabatic PV source; shuffled-region controls.", "5. Diabatic-vs-adiabatic counterfactual (primary, decisive): Perturb the model's initial state to add/remove latent heating in a target region (e.g., enhance a moist warm-conveyor-belt region), holding remote adiabatic regions fixed, and roll out. Test whether the internal PV signal develops a drift SPECIFICALLY in the perturbed diabatic region with the correct dipole sign, while remote adiabatic parcels retain along-trajectory conservation. Compare against a spatially-scrambled control perturbation of matched magnitude placed where it should not generate PV. Metric: Diabatic-vs-Adiabatic Drift Asymmetry = |probed-PV drift in perturbed diabatic region| / |drift under matched control|; a mechanistic model gives asymmetry >> 1 with the physically-correct sign.", "6. Causal diabatic-PV-source steering: Recover a 'diabatic PV source' direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between diabatic and adiabatic parcel states). Steer this direction with matched magnitude at a target region and test whether the model's PV readout and rollout develop the physically-correct PV anomaly dipole, while matched-norm random directions and an adiabatic-advection direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of diabatic-source direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "7. Failure-prediction link: Test whether an internal 'PV-conservation strength' score (along-trajectory conservation quality) predicts where the model's rollout diverges from reanalysis, especially in strongly diabatic, rapidly-developing cyclones. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error forecasts from the internal signal, vs output-uncertainty and snapshot-PV baselines.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where Lagrangian-PV structure emerges across depth and whether recovered PV-conservation subspaces align across GraphCast and Pangu (CKA), assessing convergence. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a PV-tracer diagnostic that conserves PV by construction and (lower bound) a snapshot-PV regressor with no trajectory information."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["PV along trajectories correlates with instantaneous field structure, making the along-trajectory-conservation-beyond-snapshot separation demanding; we mitigate by comparing Lagrangian (following-flow) drift against Eulerian variance, using the diabatic-vs-adiabatic counterfactual (which holds magnitude fixed and varies only whether diabatic processes act), and the spatially-scrambled control.", "Parcel trajectories must be computed from the model's own wind field and are subject to integration error, especially at long lead times; we use short trajectory segments, standard trajectory-integration schemes, and report sensitivity to integration accuracy.", "Perturbing latent heating in the initial state may push inputs off the model's training distribution, confounding the diabatic drift with generic OOD failure; we mitigate with physically-plausible perturbation magnitudes, compare against a matched-magnitude scrambled control (so any generic OOD effect is shared), and report sensitivity to perturbation strength.", "GraphCast operates on an icosahedral mesh with grid-locked features; we control for grid position, aggregate along isentropic-surface parcels, and prioritize invariant scalar channels for probing.", "PV is only approximately conserved numerically even in reanalysis (dynamical-core non-conservation is comparable to physical processes, per the PV-tracer literature); we restrict adiabatic conservation tests to regions and lead times where numerical PV conservation is well-controlled and report the reanalysis conservation floor as a reference.", "Linear probes recovering a PV/diabatic-source direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/advection-direction controls, though distributed encodings may blur single-direction interventions.", "A negative result (models store PV as a snapshot field but do not conserve it following the flow, failing the along-trajectory and diabatic-source tests) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting weather foundation models for extratropical cyclone and Rossby-wave dynamics where PV thinking is central, and for long rollouts; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether weather foundation models represent PV as a Lagrangian material invariant.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public ERA5 data with standard PV/trajectory diagnostics, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
