l PV signal remain constant in adiabatic regions and drift only where diabatic processes act? A diabatic-vs-adiabatic counterfactual perturbs latent heating in a target region and tests whether the internal PV drift appears specifically there, with the physically-correct sign, while remote adiabatic parcels stay conserved; a spatially-scrambled control of matched magnitude should not. Finally, causal steering of a recovered diabatic-PV-source direction should inject PV anomalies in the correct sense. Metrics quantify along-trajectory conservation, a diabatic-vs-adiabatic drift asymmetry, and steering selectivity. Either outcome sharpens whether operational weather models represent the material invariant behind extratropical dynamics.", 'Experiments': ['1. Models and data: Load pretrained GraphCast and Pangu-Weather (public weights). Assemble ERA5 fields (winds, temperature, geopotential, humidity) across seasons and regions. Compute ground-truth Ertel PV on isentropic surfaces at each timestep, air-parcel trajectories (from the wind field), and diabatic tendencies (latent heating from moisture/precipitation, friction near the surface) so adiabatic vs diabatic regions are labeled. Extract per-grid internal representations across layers/blocks. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.', '2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode PV (and its constituents, absolute vorticity and static stability) from internal representations across layers; confirm PV is encoded and select best layers. Establish a snapshot-field baseline (PV predictable from the instantaneous field itself).', "3. Along-trajectory conservation test (primary): During the model's own autoregressive rollout, follow predicted parcel trajectories and read the internal PV signal (probed PV) at successive positions along each trajectory. In adiabatic regions (away from latent heating/friction), measure the drift of the probed PV following the parcel (coefficient of variation / slope along the trajectory). Compare against (a) the drift of the model's actual predicted-field PV and (b) the true Lagrangian PV (approximately constant). Primary metric: internal-representation Lagrangian conservation error = along-trajectory drift of probed PV in adiabatic parcels; a mechanistic representation shows near-zero drift following the flow, beyond what Eulerian (fixed-point) PV variance would predict.", '4. Diabatic lawful-drift test: In diabatic regions (latent-heating cores, boundary-layer friction), test whether the probed PV changes along trajectories in the physically-correct sign and magnitude (PV generated below and destroyed above a latent-heating maximum, per the PV-tracer diabatic-attribution framework). Metric: Spearman correlation and sign agreement between along-trajectory probed-PV change and the true diabatic PV source; shuffled-region controls.', "5. Diabatic-vs-adiabatic counterfactual (primary, decisive): Perturb the model's initial state to add/remove latent heating in a target region (e.g., enhance a moist warm-conveyor-belt region), holding remote adiabatic regions fixed, and roll out. Test whether the internal PV signal develops a drift SPECIFICALLY in the perturbed diabatic region with the correct dipole sign, while remote adiabatic parcels retain along-trajectory conservation. Compare against a spatially-scrambled control perturbation of matched magnitude placed where it should not generate PV. Metric: Diabatic-vs-Adiabatic Drift Asymmetry = |probed-PV drift in perturbed diabatic region| / |drift under matched control|; a mechanistic model gives asymmetry >> 1 with the physically-correct sign.", "6. Causal diabatic-PV-source steering: Recover a 'diabatic PV source' direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between diabatic and adiabatic parcel states). Steer this direction with matched magnitude at a target region and test whether the model's PV readout and rollout develop the physically-correct PV anomaly dipole, while matched-norm random directions and an adiabatic-advection direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of diabatic-source direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "7. Failure-prediction link: Test whether an internal 'PV-conservation strength' score (along-trajectory conservation quality) predicts where the model's rollout diverges from reanalysis, especially in strongly diabatic, rapidly-developing cyclones. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-error forecasts from the internal signal, vs output-uncertainty and snapshot-PV baselines.", '8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where Lagrangian-PV structure emerges across depth and whether recovered PV-conservation subspaces align across GraphCast and Pangu (CKA), assessing convergence. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a PV-tracer diagnostic that conserves PV by construction and (lower bound) a snapshot-PV regressor with no trajectory information.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['PV along trajectories correlates with instantaneous field structure, making the along-trajectory-conservation-beyond-snapshot separation demanding; we mitigate by comparing Lagrangian (following-flow) drift against Eulerian variance, using the diabatic-vs-adiabatic counterfactual (which holds magnitude fixed and varies only whether diabatic processes act), and the spatially-scrambled control.', "Parcel trajectories must be computed from the model's own wind field and are subject to integration error, especially at long lead times; we use short trajectory segments, standard trajectory-integration schemes, and report sensitivity to integration accuracy.", "Perturbing latent heating in the initial state may push inputs off the model's training distribution, confounding the diabatic drift with generic OOD failure; we mitigate with physically-plausible perturbation magnitudes, compare against a matched-magnitude scrambled control (so any generic OOD effect is shared), and report sensitivity