chanism: proteins fold via ordered, cooperative foldon units in a defined stepwise pathway (Englander & Mayne 'The nature of protein folding pathways' 2014; 'The case for defined protein folding pathways' 2017), and folding kinetics/order are encoded in sequence and can evolve independently of thermodynamic stability (RNases H ancestral-folding study 2016 shows kinetics and thermodynamics tuned independently; philosophy-of-folding review 2023 stresses thermodynamic vs kinetic explanations are separable). Native-state hydrogen-exchange gives residue-resolved folding-stage/foldon ground truth. Crucially, no interpretability work tests whether a PLM internally represents the kinetic folding ORDER (foldon pathway) as opposed to the native structure, whether that representation is causally load-bearing, or uses a same-native-contact/different-fold-stage counterfactual to separate the two mechanisms. Because native importance and folding stage are correlated (both track structural centrality), native-annotation matching cannot arbitrate this. Our folding-stage-beyond-native probe, foldon-co-assembly coupling test, and folding-order counterfactual are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution.", 'Abstract': "Protein language models (PLMs) are known to encode the thermodynamic native structure of proteins — which residues contact, where binding sites and secondary-structure elements sit. But how a protein reaches that structure is governed by a distinct kinetic mechanism: the folding pathway, in which cooperative units called foldons assemble in a defined temporal order, early foldons forming before late ones. These two mechanisms are separable — folding order can evolve independently of native stability, and a residue's folding stage is a relational, temporal property that native-contact statistics do not reveal — making the kinetic pathway an ideal mechanism-defining probe for whether PLMs learn folding mechanism or merely equilibrium structure. We probe ESM-2 using proteins with residue-resolved folding-stage ground truth from native-state hydrogen-exchange and defined foldon pathways. First, a folding-stage-beyond-native probe: is a residue's foldon-order (early-to-late folding stage) decodable from internal representations beyond what native-structure importance (contacts, solvent accessibility, conservation) predicts? Second, a foldon-co-assembly coupling test: is representational coupling stronger between residues that fold together in the same foldon than between distance-matched native-contacting residues that fold at different stages? Third, and most decisively, a folding-order counterfactual: comparing mutations/perturbations that alter the kinetic pathway (change foldon order or stability) versus native-preserving controls, we test whether the internal folding-order signal responds to kinetic changes specifically. We validate causally by steering a recovered folding-order axis. Metrics quantify a stage-beyond-native index, a co-assembly coupling ratio, and a kinetic-vs-native causal asymmetry. Either outcome sharpens whether PLMs, used for stability and design, represent how proteins fold or only what they fold into.", 'Experiments': ['1. Models and data: Use ESM-2 (150M and 650M; 3B as compute allows), inference-only plus small probes. Assemble proteins with residue-resolved kinetic folding ground truth: native-state hydrogen-exchange (HX) datasets giving per-residue protection/opening free energies and foldon assignments (e.g., cytochrome c, RNase H, and other proteins with published foldon pathways and HX-derived folding order), plus Φ-value analysis datasets giving per-residue transition-state involvement. For each residue record its foldon/folding-stage (early-to-late order), and native-structure features (native contacts, relative solvent accessibility, secondary structure, evolutionary conservation). Extract per-residue internal representations across layers.', '2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode native-structure features (contacts, RSA, secondary structure) and folding stage / foldon-order from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded and select best layers. Establish a native-structure baseline: predict folding stage from native-structure features alone (which capture centrality but not temporal order).', "3. Folding-stage-beyond-native probe (primary probe): Test whether a residue's folding stage / foldon-order is decodable BEYOND native-structure importance. Regress out the native-structure-predictable component of the representation and re-decode folding stage from the residual. Primary metric: Stage-Beyond-Native Index = probe R^2 (and early-vs-late classification accuracy) for folding stage from the model representation minus what native-structure features alone achieve. A mechanism-representing model carries temporal folding-order information; a native-structure model does not exceed the baseline.", '4. Foldon co-assembly coupling test (primary, decisive test): For residue pairs, compare representational coupling (measured by how a masked/mutated perturbation at residue i propagates to residue j, or by conditional representational dependence) between (a) same-foldon pairs that co-fold as a cooperative unit and (b) distance-matched, native-contacting control pairs that belong to DIFFERENT foldons (fold at different stages). Matching on 3D distance and native-contact status ensures the only difference is kinetic co-assembly. Metric: Co-Assembly Coupling Ratio = coupling(same-foldon pairs) / coupling(distance-matched different-foldon native-contacting pairs). A mechanistic (pathway-representing) model gives ratio >> 1 (co-folding residues are internally coupled beyond mere spatial proximity); a native-structure model gives ratio ~ 1 (coupling tracks only contact/distance).', '5. Folding-order counterfactual: Construct mutations with known kinetic effects. (a) Pathway-altering mutations that change foldon stability/order (e.g., mutations shown by HX/Φ-analysis to shift folding order or destabilize a specific foldon) while minimally changing the native structure. (b) Native-preserving/kinetics-neutral control mutations matched on native-structure and conservation change. Measure the change in the internal folding-order signal (probed stage / foldon-coupling) at the affected foldon. Metric: Kinetic-vs-Native Causal Asymmetry = |folding-order-signal change under pathway-altering mutation| / |change under kinetics-neutral control|. A mechanistic representation responds specifically to kinetic changes; a native-structure representation does not.', "6. Causal folding-order steering: Recover a 'folding-order / foldon-stage' axis (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between early-folding and late-folding residues). Steer this axis with matched magnitude at target residues and test whether ESM-2's masked-prediction / variant-effect readout shifts in a stage-consistent direction, while matched-norm random directions and a native-structure-importance direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of folding-order direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "7. Kinetics-vs-thermodynamics dissociation: Using proteins/datasets where folding kinetics (folding rate, foldon order) and thermodynamic stability were shown to vary independently (e.g., the RNase H ancestral series), test whether the model's folding-order signal tracks the kinetic axis rather than the stability axis. Metric: partial correlation of the internal folding-order signal with folding order controlling for stability, and vice versa — a double dissociation would strongly support a kinetic representation distinct from thermodynamics.", '8. Layer/scale and cross-model analysis: Report where folding-order structure emerges across layers and how it changes from ESM-2 150M to 650M to 3B; test whether AF2 single-sequence Evoformer representations carry stronger kinetic-pathway signal than ESM-2 (CKA on recovered folding-order subspaces). Brackets: compare against (upper bound) an explicit foldon-pathway / HX-derived folding-order model, and (lower bound) a native-structure-only predictor with no temporal information, placing PLMs on the native-structure-to-kinetic-pathway spectrum.'], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ['Folding stage correlates with native-structure centrality (buried, highly-connected residues often fold early), making the stage-beyond-native separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with native-structure residualization, the distance-matched different-foldon control pairs, the folding-order counterfactual, and the kinetics-vs-thermodynamics double dissociation.', 'Residue-resolved kinetic ground truth (native-state HX foldon assignments, Φ-values) exists for only a modest set of well-studied proteins, limiting sample size; we pool across all available foldon-mapped proteins (cytochrome c, RNase H, and others) and report statistics accounting for small n, treating the co-assembly and counterfactual tests as the diagnostic core.', 'Foldon assignments and HX-derived folding order are themselves experimental inferences with uncertainty and some debate (defined-pathway vs energy-landscape views); we restrict decisive tests to well-established foldon pathways, report sensitivity to foldon-assignment confidence, and frame results relative to the specific ground-truth used.', 'PLMs are trained on evolutionary sequences that reflect native structure, and it is genuinely plausible they encode no kinetic-pathway information; a null result is expected under skepticism. We treat this as scientifically decisive (PLMs represent thermodynamic structure but not the kinetic folding pathway) and include AF2 and an explicit foldon model as brackets.', 'Linear probes recovering a folding-order axis do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering, the counterfactual, and random/native-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions — we use both probe directions and delta-representations.', 'In-silico perturbations may push representations off-manifold; we bound perturbation magnitude, use single mild mutations and masking, and report sensitivity, relying on the input-space mutation counterfactual as primary causal-adjacent evidence.', 'A negative result is directly relevant to trusting PLMs for folding-kinetics-dependent tasks (misfolding, aggregation, folding-rate and pathway design), where the native structure alone is insufficient; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether PLMs represent the kinetic foldon folding pathway distinct from the thermodynamic native structure.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models (ESM-2, optionally AF2 single-sequence) plus small probes and public HX/Φ-value/structure datasets, keeping compute at academic scale.']}

