Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "radiative_feedback_pattern_probe", "Title": "Where the Warming Is, Not Just How Warm: Testing Whether Weather Foundation Models Represent the Pattern-Dependent Radiative Feedback Mechanism, Not Just Global-Mean Temperature", "Short Hypothesis": "Earth's radiative response to warming is governed by an exactly-known relational mechanism: the top-of-atmosphere (TOA) radiation anomaly is not a function of global-mean surface temperature alone but of the SPATIAL PATTERN of warming (the 'pattern effect'), because local feedbacks (Planck, lapse-rate, cloud, water-vapor) differ by region - e.g., warming concentrated in tropical stratocumulus decks produces a far more negative radiative feedback than the same global-mean warming spread elsewhere. A single scalar mean temperature is degenerate about feedback strength. We hypothesize that if a machine-learning weather/climate emulator represents the radiative-feedback mechanism rather than a mean-temperature-to-radiation shortcut, then (a) the TOA radiation anomaly is decodable from internal representations beyond what global-mean surface temperature predicts, along an axis carrying warming-pattern information, (b) a pattern-holding counterfactual - rearranging the spatial pattern of a surface-temperature anomaly while holding its global mean fixed - shifts the internal radiation/feedback signal as physics dictates, whereas a pattern-preserving amplitude change moves it along the Planck line, and (c) steering a recovered 'pattern-effect' direction causally shifts predicted TOA radiation in the feedback-consistent direction. The same-mean/different-pattern counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that mean-temperature correlation cannot pass.", "Related Work": "Two lines bracket the gap. (1) ML for radiative feedbacks: 'CNNs Trained on Internal Variability Predict Forced Response of TOA Radiation by Learning the Pattern Effect' (2025) shows a CNN can predict TOA radiation from surface-temperature MAPS and, via XAI saliency, draws skill from physically-meaningful regions; 'Examining Fast Radiative Feedbacks Using ML Weather Emulators' (2026) shows historically-trained emulators reproduce fast CO2 radiative responses at the OUTPUT level; causal-emulation work (Bayesian filtering 2025; forced-response limitations 2025) studies forced responses externally. (2) Weather-model interpretability: SAE studies on GraphCast (2025) recover single instantaneous features (cyclones, atmospheric rivers, sea-ice) and steer them; GraphDOP work (2025) shows a unified world-model representation; the ocean-role study (2026) correlates OUTPUT errors with SST. Crucially, all of these operate at the input-output / saliency level or recover single instantaneous fields - none tests whether a foundation model's INTERNAL representation encodes the RELATIONAL pattern-effect mechanism (that radiative feedback depends on WHERE warming occurs, not just the global mean), whether that representation is causally load-bearing, or uses a same-mean/different-pattern counterfactual to separate the radiative-feedback mechanism from a mean-temperature shortcut. The CNN pattern-effect paper uses saliency to show WHICH regions matter for a prediction; it does not test whether the internal representation encodes feedback as a pattern-dependent quantity distinct from the mean, nor perform causal pattern-holding interventions. Our pattern-effect-beyond-mean probe, same-mean/different-pattern counterfactual, and causal pattern-effect steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, in a safety-critical climate-sensitivity setting.", "Abstract": "Machine-learning weather and climate emulators are increasingly used to study Earth's response to greenhouse-gas forcing, and interpretability studies have shown they encode recognizable physical fields. But the quantity that ultimately sets climate sensitivity - the top-of-atmosphere (TOA) radiative feedback - is governed by a relational mechanism that a single number cannot express: the 'pattern effect'. The TOA radiation anomaly depends not on global-mean surface temperature alone but on the spatial PATTERN of warming, because local Planck, lapse-rate, cloud, and water-vapor feedbacks differ sharply by region; the same global-mean warming can imply strongly or weakly stabilizing feedback depending on where it is concentrated. This makes the pattern effect an ideal, mechanism-defining probe for whether emulators learn the feedback mechanism or a mean-temperature shortcut. We probe pretrained weather/climate foundation models (GraphCast, Pangu-Weather, and a fast-feedback-capable emulator) on reanalysis and climate-model data where surface-temperature fields, TOA radiation, and diagnosed feedbacks are known. First, a feedback-beyond-mean probe: is the TOA radiation anomaly decodable from internal representations beyond what global-mean surface temperature predicts, along an axis carrying warming-pattern information? Second, and most decisively, a same-mean/different-pattern counterfactual: we rearrange the spatial pattern of a surface-temperature anomaly holding its global mean fixed (which physics says changes the radiative feedback), versus a pattern-preserving amplitude change (which moves along the Planck line), and test whether the internal radiation/feedback signal responds to the pattern - a mean-shortcut model responds only to amplitude. Third, causal steering of a recovered pattern-effect direction. Metrics quantify a feedback-beyond-mean index, a pattern-vs-amplitude causal asymmetry, and a regional-feedback fidelity test. Either outcome sharpens whether emulators represent the mechanism behind climate sensitivity.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load pretrained weather/climate foundation models with public weights that ingest surface temperature and expose internal representations (GraphCast, Pangu-Weather; and a fast-feedback-capable emulator as used in the 2026 fast-radiative-feedback study). Assemble data pairing surface-temperature fields with TOA radiation and diagnosed feedbacks: ERA5/CERES for reanalysis, and CMIP6 amip-piForcing / abrupt-4xCO2 members plus the Green's-function / patterned-SST experiments that provide regional radiative-feedback (Green's-function) kernels. For each state record the global-mean surface-temperature anomaly, the warming-pattern field, the TOA radiation anomaly, and (from Green's functions/kernels) the pattern-implied feedback. Extract internal representations across layers/blocks. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode global-mean surface temperature, the TOA radiation anomaly, and regional feedback strength from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded and select best layers. Establish a mean-temperature baseline: predict TOA radiation directly from global-mean surface temperature (the Planck/shortcut predictor).", "3. Feedback-beyond-mean probe (primary probe): Test whether the TOA radiation anomaly is decodable BEYOND global-mean surface temperature. Regress out the global-mean-predictable component of the representation and re-decode TOA radiation from the residual. Primary metric: Feedback-Beyond-Mean Index = probe R^2 for TOA radiation from the model representation minus R^2 from global-mean temperature alone. Also test whether the recovered radiation signal aligns with warming-pattern information (correlate its direction with a pattern probe vs a mean probe). A mechanism-representing model carries pattern-dependent feedback information; a shortcut model does not exceed the mean baseline.", "4. Same-mean/different-pattern counterfactual (primary, decisive test): Construct matched surface-temperature-anomaly edits holding all else fixed. (a) Pattern edit: rearrange the spatial pattern of the anomaly (e.g., move warming from tropical stratocumulus/east-Pacific regions to elsewhere) while holding the GLOBAL MEAN exactly fixed - which by the pattern effect changes the radiative feedback. (b) Amplitude control edit: scale the anomaly's amplitude (changing the mean) while holding the PATTERN fixed - which moves radiation along the Planck line. Feed both to the model and measure the change in its internal radiation/feedback signal (probed TOA radiation, or the model's TOA-relevant field output). Metric: Pattern-vs-Amplitude Causal Asymmetry = |radiation-signal change under same-mean pattern edit| / |radiation-signal change per unit mean under amplitude edit, matched on total anomaly magnitude|. A mechanistic representation responds substantially to the pattern edit (feedback depends on where warming is); a mean-shortcut representation responds negligibly to the pattern edit at fixed mean.", "5. Regional-feedback fidelity (physics test): For localized warming patches in different regions (holding global mean fixed), test whether the model's induced radiation change tracks the region-specific Green's-function/kernel feedback - strongly negative for tropical low-cloud regions, weaker elsewhere - rather than a uniform (pattern-agnostic) response. Metric: Spearman correlation and sign agreement between the model's regional radiation response and the ground-truth regional feedback; shuffled-region controls.", "6. Causal pattern-effect steering: Recover a 'pattern-effect' direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between high-feedback and low-feedback patterns at matched global mean). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's TOA radiation readout shifts in the feedback-consistent direction, while matched-norm random directions and a global-mean-temperature direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of pattern-effect direction| / |effect of random direction|; cross-talk with the mean direction.", "7. Failure-prediction and climate-sensitivity link: Test whether an internal 'pattern-effect representation strength' score predicts, per state, where the model mis-estimates TOA radiation - especially for warming patterns unlike the historical record (e.g., 4xCO2-like patterns) where the mean-temperature shortcut misleads. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-radiation-error states from the internal signal, versus output-uncertainty and mean-only baselines. This connects the representational signature to reliability of emulator-based climate-sensitivity estimates.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where pattern-effect structure emerges across depth and whether recovered pattern-effect subspaces align across the different emulators (CKA), assessing whether the radiative-feedback mechanism is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a Green's-function/radiative-kernel model that computes feedback from the pattern by construction, and (lower bound) a global-mean-temperature-to-radiation regressor with no pattern access, placing emulators on the mean-shortcut-to-pattern-effect-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Global-mean surface temperature and warming pattern are correlated in ordinary data, making the feedback-beyond-mean separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with mean-residualization, the same-mean/different-pattern counterfactual (which holds the global mean fixed and varies only the pattern), the regional-feedback fidelity test using Green's-function ground truth, and the amplitude-control edit.", "The pattern-rearrangement counterfactual is a synthetic edit; physically-implausible warming patterns may push inputs off the model's training distribution, confounding the pattern response with generic out-of-distribution failure. We mitigate with physically-plausible patterns (drawn from CMIP pattern-effect experiments), verify the global mean is held fixed, compare against amplitude edits of matched magnitude (so any generic OOD effect is shared), and report sensitivity to pattern-edit magnitude.", "Many medium-range weather emulators (GraphCast, Pangu) may not directly output TOA radiation and have short rollout horizons, while the pattern effect is usually a decadal/forced concept; we focus on the FAST radiative feedback that is present on weekly timescales (per the 2026 fast-feedback study, historically-trained emulators reproduce it), prioritize emulators that expose or can be probed for radiation-relevant fields, and report per-model what is measurable, treating the fast-feedback-capable emulator as the primary testbed.", "Ground-truth regional feedbacks (Green's functions/kernels) are themselves model-derived and uncertain; we use consensus Green's-function experiments and radiative kernels, restrict decisive tests to regions with robust feedback estimates, and report sensitivity to the feedback reference.", "Emulators normalize/tokenize inputs, which may remove absolute-anomaly information relevant to radiation; we document each model's preprocessing, probe pre-normalization activations where feasible, and prioritize models retaining the surface-temperature field.", "Linear probes recovering a pattern-effect direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering, cross-talk controls, and random/mean-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and delta-representations.", "A negative result (models predict radiation from global-mean temperature and fail the same-mean/different-pattern counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting ML emulators for climate-sensitivity and pattern-effect studies - precisely where the mean-temperature shortcut breaks for novel warming patterns; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether weather/climate foundation models represent the pattern-dependent radiative feedback mechanism.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public reanalysis (ERA5/CERES) and CMIP pattern-effect data, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'radiative_feedback_pattern_probe', 'Title': 'Where the Warming Is, Not Just How Warm: Testing Whether Weather Foundation Models Represent the Pattern-Dependent Radiative Feedback Mechanism, Not Just Global-Mean Temperature', 'Short Hypothesis': "Earth's radiative response to warming is governed by an exactly-known relational mechanism: the top-of-atmosphere (TOA) radiation anomaly is not a function of global-mean surface temperature alone but of the SPATIAL PATTERN of warming (the 'pattern effect'), because local feedbacks (Planck, lapse-rate, cloud, water-vapor) differ by region - e.g., warming concentrated in tropical stratocumulus decks produces a far more negative radiative feedback than the same global-mean warming spread elsewhere. A single scalar mean temperature is degenerate about feedback strength. We hypothesize that if a machine-learning weather/climate emulator represents the radiative-feedback mechanism rather than a mean-temperature-to-radiation shortcut, then (a) the TOA radiation anomaly is decodable from internal representations beyond what global-mean surface temperature predicts, along an axis carrying warming-pattern information, (b) a pattern-holding counterfactual - rearranging the spatial pattern of a surface-temperature anomaly while holding its global mean fixed - shifts the internal radiation/feedback signal as physics dictates, whereas a pattern-preserving amplitude change moves it along the Planck line, and (c) steering a recovered 'pattern-effect' direction causally shifts predicted TOA radiation in the feedback-consistent direction. The same-mean/different-pattern counterfactual is an exact, mechanism-defining test that mean-temperature correlation cannot pass.", 'Related Work': "Two lines bracket the gap. (1) ML for radiative feedbacks: 'CNNs Trained on Internal Variability Predict Forced Response of TOA Radiation by Learning the Pattern Effect' (2025) shows a CNN can predict TOA radiation from surface-temperature MAPS and, via XAI saliency, draws skill from physically-meaningful regions; 'Examining Fast Radiative Feedbacks Using ML Weather Emulators' (2026) shows historically-trained emulators reproduce fast CO2 radiative responses at the OUTPUT level; causal-emulation work (Bayesian filtering 2025; forced-response limitations 2025) studies forced responses externally. (2) Weather-model interpretability: SAE studies on GraphCast (2025) recover single instantaneous features (cyclones, atmospheric rivers, sea-ice) and steer them; GraphDOP work (2025) shows a unified world-model representation; the ocean-role study (2026) correlates OUTPUT errors with SST. Crucially, all of these operate at the input-output / saliency level or recover single instantaneous fields - none tests whether a foundation model's INTERNAL representation encodes the RELATIONAL pattern-effect mechanism (that radiative feedback depends on WHERE warming occurs, not just the global mean), whether that representation is causally load-bearing, or uses a same-mean/different-pattern counterfactual to separate the radiative-feedback mechanism from a mean-temperature shortcut. The CNN pattern-effect paper uses saliency to show WHICH regions matter for a prediction; it does not test whether the internal representation encodes feedback as a pattern-dependent quantity distinct from the mean, nor perform causal pattern-holding interventions. Our pattern-effect-beyond-mean probe, same-mean/different-pattern counterfactual, and causal pattern-effect steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution, in a safety-critical climate-sensitivity setting.", 'Abstract': "Machine-learning weather and climate emulators are increasingly used to study Earth's response to greenhouse-gas forcing, and interpretability studies have shown they encode recognizable physical fields. But the quantity that ultimately sets climate sensitivity - the top-of-atmosphere (TOA) radiative feedback - is governed by a relational mechanism that a single number cannot express: the 'pattern effect'. The TOA radiation anomaly depends not on global-mean surface temperature alone but on the spatial PATTERN of warming, because local Planck, lapse-rate, cloud, and water-vapor feedbacks differ sharply by region; the same global-mean warming can imply strongly or weakly stabilizing feedback depending on where it is concentrated. This makes the pattern effect an ideal, mechanism-defining probe for whether emulators learn the feedback mechanism or a mean-temperature shortcut. We probe pretrained weather/climate foundation models (GraphCast, Pangu-Weather, and a fast-feedback-capable emulator) on reanalysis and climate-model data where surface-temperature fields, TOA radiation, and diagnosed feedbacks are known. First, a feedback-beyond-mean probe: is the TOA radiation anomaly decodable from internal representations beyond what global-mean surface temperature predicts, along an axis carrying warming-pattern information? Second, and most decisively, a same-mean/different-pattern counterfactual: we rearrange the spatial pattern of a surface-temperature anomaly holding its global mean fixed (which physics says changes the radiative feedback), versus a pattern-preserving amplitude change (which moves along the Planck line), and test whether the internal radiation/feedback signal responds to the pattern - a mean-shortcut model responds only to amplitude. Third, causal steering of a recovered pattern-effect direction. Metrics quantify a feedback-beyond-mean index, a pattern-vs-amplitude causal asymmetry, and a regional-feedback fidelity test. Either outcome sharpens whether emulators represent the mechanism behind climate sensitivity.", 'Experiments': ["1. Models and data: Load pretrained weather/climate foundation models with public weights that ingest surface temperature and expose internal representations (GraphCast, Pangu-Weather; and a fast-feedback-capable emulator as used in the 2026 fast-radiative-feedback study). Assemble data pairing surface-temperature fields with TOA radiation and diagnosed feedbacks: ERA5/CERES for reanalysis, and CMIP6 amip-piForcing / abrupt-4xCO2 members plus the Green's-function / patterned-SST experiments that provide regional radiative-feedback (Green's-function) kernels. For each state record the global-mean surface-temperature anomaly, the warming-pattern field, the TOA radiation anomaly, and (from Green's functions/kernels) the pattern-implied feedback. Extract internal representations across layers/blocks. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.", '2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode global-mean surface temperature, the TOA radiation anomaly, and regional feedback strength from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded and select best layers. Establish a mean-temperature baseline: predict TOA radiation directly from global-mean surface temperature (the Planck/shortcut predictor).', '3. Feedback-beyond-mean probe (primary probe): Test whether the TOA radiation anomaly is decodable BEYOND global-mean surface temperature. Regress out the global-mean-predictable component of the representation and re-decode TOA radiation from the residual. Primary metric: Feedback-Beyond-Mean Index = probe R^2 for TOA radiation from the model representation minus R^2 from global-mean temperature alone. Also test whether the recovered radiation signal aligns with warming-pattern information (correlate its direction with a pattern probe vs a mean probe). A mechanism-representing model carries pattern-dependent feedback information; a shortcut model does not exceed the mean baseline.', "4. Same-mean/different-pattern counterfactual (primary, decisive test): Construct matched surface-temperature-anomaly edits holding all else fixed. (a) Pattern edit: rearrange the spatial pattern of the anomaly (e.g., move warming from tropical stratocumulus/east-Pacific regions to elsewhere) while holding the GLOBAL MEAN exactly fixed - which by the pattern effect changes the radiative feedback. (b) Amplitude control edit: scale the anomaly's amplitude (changing the mean) while holding the PATTERN fixed - which moves radiation along the Planck line. Feed both to the model and measure the change in its internal radiation/feedback signal (probed TOA radiation, or the model's TOA-relevant field output). Metric: Pattern-vs-Amplitude Causal Asymmetry = |radiation-signal change under same-mean pattern edit| / |radiation-signal change per unit mean under amplitude edit, matched on total anomaly magnitude|. A mechanistic representation responds substantially to the pattern edit (feedback depends on where warming is); a mean-shortcut representation responds negligibly to the pattern edit at fixed mean.", "5. Regional-feedback fidelity (physics test): For localized warming patches in different regions (holding global mean fixed), test whether the model's induced radiation change tracks the region-specific Green's-function/kernel feedback - strongly negative for tropical low-cloud regions, weaker elsewhere - rather than a uniform (pattern-agnostic) response. Metric: Spearman correlation and sign agreement between the model's regional radiation response and the ground-truth regional feedback; shuffled-region controls.", "6. Causal pattern-effect steering: Recover a 'pattern-effect' direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between high-feedback and low-feedback patterns at matched global mean). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's TOA radiation readout shifts in the feedback-consistent direction, while matched-norm random directions and a global-mean-temperature direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of pattern-effect direction| / |effect of random direction|; cross-talk with the mean direction.", "7. Failure-prediction and climate-sensitivity link: Test whether an internal 'pattern-effect representation strength' score predicts, per state, where the model mis-estimates TOA radiation - especially for warming patterns unlike the historical record (e.g., 4xCO2-like patterns) where the mean-temperature shortcut misleads. Metric: AUROC for flagging high-radiation-error states from the internal signal, versus output-uncertainty and mean-only baselines. This connects the representational signature to reliability of emulator-based climate-sensitivity estimates.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where pattern-effect structure emerges across depth and whether recovered pattern-effect subspaces align across the different emulators (CKA), assessing whether the radiative-feedback mechanism is a convergent internal property. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) a Green's-function/radiative-kernel model that computes feedback from the pattern by construction, and (lower bound) a global-mean-temperature-to-radiation regressor with no pattern access, placing emulators on the mean-shortcut-to-pattern-effect-mechanism spectrum."], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ["Global-mean surface temperature and warming pattern are correlated in ordinary data, making the feedback-beyond-mean separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with mean-residualization, the same-mean/different-pattern counterfactual (which holds the global mean fixed and varies only the pattern), the regional-feedback fidelity test using Green's-function ground truth, and the amplitude-control edit.", "The pattern-rearrangement counterfactual is a synthetic edit; physically-implausible warming patterns may push inputs off the model's training distribution, confounding the pattern response with generic out-of-distribution failure. We mitigate with physically-plausible patterns (drawn from CMIP pattern-effect experiments), verify the global mean is held fixed, compare against amplitude edits of matched magnitude (so any generic OOD effect is shared), and report sensitivity to pattern-edit magnitude.", 'Many medium-range weather emulators (GraphCast, Pangu) may not directly output TOA radiation and have short rollout horizons, while the pattern effect is usually a decadal/forced concept; we focus on the FAST radiative feedback that is present on weekly timescales (per the 2026 fast-feedback study, historically-trained emulators reproduce it), prioritize emulators that expose or can be probed for radiation-relevant fields, and report per-model what is measurable, treating the fast-feedback-capable emulator as the primary testbed.', "Ground-truth regional feedbacks (Green's functions/kernels) are themselves model-derived and uncertain; we use consensus Green's-function experiments and radiative kernels, restrict decisive tests to regions with robust feedback estimates, and report sensitivity to the feedback reference.", "Emulators normalize/tokenize inputs, which may remove absolute-anomaly information relevant to radiation; we document each model's preprocessing, probe pre-normalization activations where feasible, and prioritize models retaining the surface-temperature field.", 'Linear probes recovering a pattern-effect direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering, cross-talk controls, and random/mean-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and delta-representations.', 'A negative result (models predict radiation from global-mean temperature and fail the same-mean/different-pattern counterfactual) is scientifically decisive and directly relevant to trusting ML emulators for climate-sensitivity and pattern-effect studies - precisely where the mean-temperature shortcut breaks for novel warming patterns; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining test of whether weather/climate foundation models represent the pattern-dependent radiative feedback mechanism.', 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models plus small probes and public reanalysis (ERA5/CERES) and CMIP pattern-effect data, keeping compute at academic scale.']}

