Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "backdoor_confounder_flip_probe", "Title": "Does the Model Adjust for Confounders? Testing Whether Tabular/Scientific Foundation Models Represent the Backdoor Simpson's-Flip Mechanism, Not Just Observational Association", "Short Hypothesis": "The mechanism-defining feature that separates causation from correlation is confounder adjustment: the backdoor criterion says the interventional effect P(Y|do(X)) can differ in sign from the observational association P(Y|X) once a common cause (confounder) is adjusted for - the Simpson's-paradox sign flip. We hypothesize that if a tabular/scientific foundation model (e.g., TabPFN, pretrained over structural causal models) internally represents this adjustment mechanism rather than raw observational association, then (a) the adjusted (interventional) effect direction is decodable from internal representations beyond what the observational association predicts, (b) a confounder-presence counterfactual - supplying versus withholding the confounding variable, holding all else fixed - flips the model's internal effect signal in the Simpson's-flip cases but not in confounder-free cases, and (c) steering a recovered 'adjustment' direction causally moves predictions toward the do-calculus answer. The confounder-in-vs-out sign-flip is an exact, mechanism-defining test that predictive accuracy and observational correlation cannot pass.", "Related Work": "TabPFN, pretrained on synthetic structural-causal-model datasets, is the natural testbed: 'Does TabPFN Understand Causal Structures?' (2025) shows its mid-layer embeddings contain decodable causal-graph information and beat statistical causal-discovery baselines, but it decodes the STATIC adjacency graph - it never tests whether the model represents the ADJUSTMENT mechanism (backdoor/Simpson's-flip) that distinguishes observational from interventional effects, nor uses a confounder-presence counterfactual. 'How Transformers Learn Causal Structure' (2024) and 'Causal Interpretation of Self-Attention' (2023) show transformers encode latent causal graphs in attention, again structural not adjustment-focused. Benchmark studies (CLadder 2023, CausalPitfalls 2025, 'Ice Cream Doesn't Cause Drowning' 2025, contingency-judgment 2025) show LLMs FAIL at confounding and Simpson's paradox at the OUTPUT/prompt level, but none probes internal representations or performs a causal confounder-in/out counterfactual. My own single-cell d-separation probe tested conditional-independence structure but not the sign-flipping backdoor-adjustment mechanism or a confounder-presence intervention. Because observational association and adjusted effect coincide in confounder-free data, correlation-based benchmarks cannot arbitrate this; the adjusted-effect-beyond-observational probe, confounder-in-vs-out sign-flip counterfactual, and causal adjustment-direction steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution.", "Abstract": "The single most important distinction in causal inference - and the dominant failure mode of data-driven science - is confounder adjustment: an observed association between a variable X and outcome Y can vanish or even reverse sign once a common cause (confounder) is accounted for, the phenomenon known as Simpson's paradox, formalized by the backdoor criterion. Because a model can predict Y well while relying entirely on the confounded observational association, whether foundation models internally represent the adjustment mechanism is untested and safety-critical: confounding by indication, batch, or population dominates errors in drug-response, clinical, and materials data. We propose the first mechanism-defining, representational test. Using TabPFN - a tabular foundation model pretrained over structural causal models - and a general tabular baseline, we generate datasets with known causal graphs where the observational X-Y association and the true interventional (backdoor-adjusted) effect are controllably concordant or sign-discordant (Simpson's-flip). First, an adjusted-effect-beyond-observational probe: is the true interventional effect direction decodable from internal representations beyond what the observational association predicts, along a distinct axis? Second, and most decisively, a confounder-presence counterfactual: we supply versus withhold the confounding variable, holding all else fixed, and test whether the model's internal effect signal flips toward the adjusted answer specifically in Simpson's-flip cases, while confounder-free cases stay stable; an association-only model responds identically. Third, causal steering of a recovered adjustment direction. Metrics quantify an adjustment-beyond-observational index, a confounder-in-vs-out flip asymmetry, and an interventional-fidelity dose-response. Either outcome sharpens whether these models distinguish do(X) from X.", "Experiments": ["1. Models and data: Load TabPFN (pretrained over structural causal models, with accessible per-layer embeddings) and a general tabular baseline (e.g., a supervised MLP/gradient-boosting embedding, or a tabular transformer). Generate synthetic datasets from known structural causal models spanning three regimes: (a) confounder-free (observational X-Y association equals the true interventional effect), (b) magnitude-confounded (adjustment shrinks but preserves the effect sign), and (c) Simpson's-flip (a confounder Z is a common cause of X and Y such that the observational X-Y association has the OPPOSITE sign to the true backdoor-adjusted P(Y|do(X)) effect). For each dataset record the true causal graph, the observational association, and the exact do-calculus interventional effect. Extract internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode the observational X-Y association, the confounder's role, and the true interventional effect direction from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded and select best layers (replicating and extending TabPFN causal-structure findings). Establish an observational baseline: predict the interventional effect direction from the observational association alone (which is wrong in Simpson's-flip cases).", "3. Adjusted-effect-beyond-observational probe (primary probe): Test whether the true interventional (backdoor-adjusted) effect direction is decodable BEYOND the observational association. Regress out the observational-association-predictable component of the representation and re-decode the adjusted effect from the residual. Primary metric: Adjustment-Beyond-Observational Index = probe accuracy for the adjusted effect direction from the model representation minus what the observational association alone achieves. Report separately for the Simpson's-flip subset (the diagnostic cases where the two disagree). Also measure the angle between the recovered observational-association direction and the recovered adjusted-effect direction; a mechanism-representing model encodes them as distinct axes.", "4. Confounder-presence counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For each dataset, construct two matched inputs holding the data-generating process fixed: (a) confounder-in - the confounding variable Z is included as an observed column; (b) confounder-out - Z is withheld (so backdoor adjustment is impossible and only the confounded observational association is available). Measure the change in the model's internal effect signal (probed effect direction / the model's predicted X->Y relationship) between the two. Metric: Confounder-In-vs-Out Flip Asymmetry = fraction of Simpson's-flip cases where the internal effect signal moves toward the correct adjusted (sign-flipped) answer when Z is supplied, versus the near-zero change expected in confounder-free cases. A mechanistic (adjustment-representing) model flips its signal specifically when the confounder is present in a flip case; an association-only model does not.", "5. Interventional-fidelity dose-response: Vary the confounding strength (the coupling of Z to X and Y) continuously from zero (no confounding) through the sign-flip threshold, and test whether the model's internal adjusted-effect signal tracks the true do-calculus effect across the sign change - crossing zero at the correct confounding strength - rather than following the observational association. Metric: Spearman correlation and sign-crossing-location agreement between the internal signal and the true interventional effect; shuffled-confounder controls.", "6. Causal adjustment-direction steering: Recover an 'adjustment' direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between confounder-in and confounder-out states in flip cases). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's predicted X->Y effect moves toward the do-calculus answer, while matched-norm random directions and an observational-association direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of adjustment direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "7. Failure-prediction and safety link: Test whether an internal 'adjustment-representation strength' score (adjustment-beyond-observational confidence / confounder-counterfactual sensitivity) predicts, per dataset, where the model's effect estimate is confounded-wrong - especially in strong Simpson's-flip cases. Then apply to semi-realistic settings (e.g., confounding-by-indication in a drug-outcome table, batch confounding in an omics table) to test whether the internal signal flags confounded conclusions. Metric: AUROC for flagging confounded-wrong effect estimates from the internal signal, versus output-confidence and observational baselines.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where adjustment structure emerges across depth (hypothesizing TabPFN's mid layers, per prior causal-structure findings) and whether recovered adjustment subspaces align across TabPFN and the general tabular model (CKA), assessing whether backdoor-adjustment structure is a convergent internal property or specific to SCM-pretrained models. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) an explicit backdoor-adjustment estimator that computes P(Y|do(X)) by construction, and (lower bound) an observational-association regressor with no confounder access, placing foundation models on the observational-to-adjustment-mechanism spectrum."], "Risk Factors and Limitations": ["Observational association and adjusted effect coincide except in confounded cases, making the adjustment-beyond-observational separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with observational-residualization, the confounder-in-vs-out counterfactual (which holds the data-generating process fixed and varies only confounder availability), the Simpson's-flip diagnostic subset, and the dose-response sign-crossing test that an association-only model cannot reproduce.", "Withholding the confounder column changes the input dimensionality/schema, which for some tabular models is a structural edit that could confound the comparison with generic distribution shift; we mitigate by using confounder-free datasets (where withholding an equally-informative non-confounding column should NOT flip the effect) as a matched control, so any generic schema-change effect is shared, and report sensitivity.", "TabPFN and tabular transformers may implicitly perform some adjustment when all variables are supplied simply by conditioning, blurring 'representing the mechanism' from 'conditioning gives the right answer'; we distinguish these by requiring the correct SIGN FLIP specifically in Simpson's-flip cases, the distinct-axis probe, and the dose-response sign-crossing, none of which a naive conditioner passes without representing adjustment.", "Synthetic SCM data gives exact ground truth but may not reflect real confounding structure; we cross-validate on semi-realistic tables (confounding-by-indication, batch confounding) where the true adjusted effect is known or estimable, and report synthetic-vs-real agreement, treating synthetic as the decisive-counterfactual testbed and real as the transfer check.", "Linear probes recovering an adjustment direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/observational-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and delta-representations.", "A negative result (models encode observational association and fail to flip under confounder presence in Simpson's-flip cases) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant - it would warn against trusting tabular/scientific foundation models for causal-effect estimation in confounded domains (drug outcomes, clinical, omics) where confounding by indication or batch dominates; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining, representational test of whether foundation models represent the backdoor confounder-adjustment mechanism.", "All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models (TabPFN, a tabular baseline) plus small probes and synthetic/semi-realistic tabular datasets, keeping compute at academic scale."]}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'backdoor_confounder_flip_probe', 'Title': "Does the Model Adjust for Confounders? Testing Whether Tabular/Scientific Foundation Models Represent the Backdoor Simpson's-Flip Mechanism, Not Just Observational Association", 'Short Hypothesis': "The mechanism-defining feature that separates causation from correlation is confounder adjustment: the backdoor criterion says the interventional effect P(Y|do(X)) can differ in sign from the observational association P(Y|X) once a common cause (confounder) is adjusted for - the Simpson's-paradox sign flip. We hypothesize that if a tabular/scientific foundation model (e.g., TabPFN, pretrained over structural causal models) internally represents this adjustment mechanism rather than raw observational association, then (a) the adjusted (interventional) effect direction is decodable from internal representations beyond what the observational association predicts, (b) a confounder-presence counterfactual - supplying versus withholding the confounding variable, holding all else fixed - flips the model's internal effect signal in the Simpson's-flip cases but not in confounder-free cases, and (c) steering a recovered 'adjustment' direction causally moves predictions toward the do-calculus answer. The confounder-in-vs-out sign-flip is an exact, mechanism-defining test that predictive accuracy and observational correlation cannot pass.", 'Related Work': "TabPFN, pretrained on synthetic structural-causal-model datasets, is the natural testbed: 'Does TabPFN Understand Causal Structures?' (2025) shows its mid-layer embeddings contain decodable causal-graph information and beat statistical causal-discovery baselines, but it decodes the STATIC adjacency graph - it never tests whether the model represents the ADJUSTMENT mechanism (backdoor/Simpson's-flip) that distinguishes observational from interventional effects, nor uses a confounder-presence counterfactual. 'How Transformers Learn Causal Structure' (2024) and 'Causal Interpretation of Self-Attention' (2023) show transformers encode latent causal graphs in attention, again structural not adjustment-focused. Benchmark studies (CLadder 2023, CausalPitfalls 2025, 'Ice Cream Doesn't Cause Drowning' 2025, contingency-judgment 2025) show LLMs FAIL at confounding and Simpson's paradox at the OUTPUT/prompt level, but none probes internal representations or performs a causal confounder-in/out counterfactual. My own single-cell d-separation probe tested conditional-independence structure but not the sign-flipping backdoor-adjustment mechanism or a confounder-presence intervention. Because observational association and adjusted effect coincide in confounder-free data, correlation-based benchmarks cannot arbitrate this; the adjusted-effect-beyond-observational probe, confounder-in-vs-out sign-flip counterfactual, and causal adjustment-direction steering are the novel, mechanism-defining contribution.", 'Abstract': "The single most important distinction in causal inference - and the dominant failure mode of data-driven science - is confounder adjustment: an observed association between a variable X and outcome Y can vanish or even reverse sign once a common cause (confounder) is accounted for, the phenomenon known as Simpson's paradox, formalized by the backdoor criterion. Because a model can predict Y well while relying entirely on the confounded observational association, whether foundation models internally represent the adjustment mechanism is untested and safety-critical: confounding by indication, batch, or population dominates errors in drug-response, clinical, and materials data. We propose the first mechanism-defining, representational test. Using TabPFN - a tabular foundation model pretrained over structural causal models - and a general tabular baseline, we generate datasets with known causal graphs where the observational X-Y association and the true interventional (backdoor-adjusted) effect are controllably concordant or sign-discordant (Simpson's-flip). First, an adjusted-effect-beyond-observational probe: is the true interventional effect direction decodable from internal representations beyond what the observational association predicts, along a distinct axis? Second, and most decisively, a confounder-presence counterfactual: we supply versus withhold the confounding variable, holding all else fixed, and test whether the model's internal effect signal flips toward the adjusted answer specifically in Simpson's-flip cases, while confounder-free cases stay stable; an association-only model responds identically. Third, causal steering of a recovered adjustment direction. Metrics quantify an adjustment-beyond-observational index, a confounder-in-vs-out flip asymmetry, and an interventional-fidelity dose-response. Either outcome sharpens whether these models distinguish do(X) from X.", 'Experiments': ["1. Models and data: Load TabPFN (pretrained over structural causal models, with accessible per-layer embeddings) and a general tabular baseline (e.g., a supervised MLP/gradient-boosting embedding, or a tabular transformer). Generate synthetic datasets from known structural causal models spanning three regimes: (a) confounder-free (observational X-Y association equals the true interventional effect), (b) magnitude-confounded (adjustment shrinks but preserves the effect sign), and (c) Simpson's-flip (a confounder Z is a common cause of X and Y such that the observational X-Y association has the OPPOSITE sign to the true backdoor-adjusted P(Y|do(X)) effect). For each dataset record the true causal graph, the observational association, and the exact do-calculus interventional effect. Extract internal representations across layers. All experiments are inference-only plus small linear probes.", "2. Baseline decodability: Train linear probes to decode the observational X-Y association, the confounder's role, and the true interventional effect direction from internal representations across layers; confirm what is encoded and select best layers (replicating and extending TabPFN causal-structure findings). Establish an observational baseline: predict the interventional effect direction from the observational association alone (which is wrong in Simpson's-flip cases).", "3. Adjusted-effect-beyond-observational probe (primary probe): Test whether the true interventional (backdoor-adjusted) effect direction is decodable BEYOND the observational association. Regress out the observational-association-predictable component of the representation and re-decode the adjusted effect from the residual. Primary metric: Adjustment-Beyond-Observational Index = probe accuracy for the adjusted effect direction from the model representation minus what the observational association alone achieves. Report separately for the Simpson's-flip subset (the diagnostic cases where the two disagree). Also measure the angle between the recovered observational-association direction and the recovered adjusted-effect direction; a mechanism-representing model encodes them as distinct axes.", "4. Confounder-presence counterfactual (primary, decisive test): For each dataset, construct two matched inputs holding the data-generating process fixed: (a) confounder-in - the confounding variable Z is included as an observed column; (b) confounder-out - Z is withheld (so backdoor adjustment is impossible and only the confounded observational association is available). Measure the change in the model's internal effect signal (probed effect direction / the model's predicted X->Y relationship) between the two. Metric: Confounder-In-vs-Out Flip Asymmetry = fraction of Simpson's-flip cases where the internal effect signal moves toward the correct adjusted (sign-flipped) answer when Z is supplied, versus the near-zero change expected in confounder-free cases. A mechanistic (adjustment-representing) model flips its signal specifically when the confounder is present in a flip case; an association-only model does not.", "5. Interventional-fidelity dose-response: Vary the confounding strength (the coupling of Z to X and Y) continuously from zero (no confounding) through the sign-flip threshold, and test whether the model's internal adjusted-effect signal tracks the true do-calculus effect across the sign change - crossing zero at the correct confounding strength - rather than following the observational association. Metric: Spearman correlation and sign-crossing-location agreement between the internal signal and the true interventional effect; shuffled-confounder controls.", "6. Causal adjustment-direction steering: Recover an 'adjustment' direction (a supervised probe direction, or a delta-representation between confounder-in and confounder-out states in flip cases). Steer this direction with matched magnitude and test whether the model's predicted X->Y effect moves toward the do-calculus answer, while matched-norm random directions and an observational-association direction do not. Metric: mechanism-selectivity = |effect of adjustment direction| / |effect of random direction|.", "7. Failure-prediction and safety link: Test whether an internal 'adjustment-representation strength' score (adjustment-beyond-observational confidence / confounder-counterfactual sensitivity) predicts, per dataset, where the model's effect estimate is confounded-wrong - especially in strong Simpson's-flip cases. Then apply to semi-realistic settings (e.g., confounding-by-indication in a drug-outcome table, batch confounding in an omics table) to test whether the internal signal flags confounded conclusions. Metric: AUROC for flagging confounded-wrong effect estimates from the internal signal, versus output-confidence and observational baselines.", "8. Layer/emergence and cross-model universality: Report where adjustment structure emerges across depth (hypothesizing TabPFN's mid layers, per prior causal-structure findings) and whether recovered adjustment subspaces align across TabPFN and the general tabular model (CKA), assessing whether backdoor-adjustment structure is a convergent internal property or specific to SCM-pretrained models. Brackets: compare against (upper bound) an explicit backdoor-adjustment estimator that computes P(Y|do(X)) by construction, and (lower bound) an observational-association regressor with no confounder access, placing foundation models on the observational-to-adjustment-mechanism spectrum."], 'Risk Factors and Limitations': ["Observational association and adjusted effect coincide except in confounded cases, making the adjustment-beyond-observational separation statistically demanding; we mitigate with observational-residualization, the confounder-in-vs-out counterfactual (which holds the data-generating process fixed and varies only confounder availability), the Simpson's-flip diagnostic subset, and the dose-response sign-crossing test that an association-only model cannot reproduce.", 'Withholding the confounder column changes the input dimensionality/schema, which for some tabular models is a structural edit that could confound the comparison with generic distribution shift; we mitigate by using confounder-free datasets (where withholding an equally-informative non-confounding column should NOT flip the effect) as a matched control, so any generic schema-change effect is shared, and report sensitivity.', "TabPFN and tabular transformers may implicitly perform some adjustment when all variables are supplied simply by conditioning, blurring 'representing the mechanism' from 'conditioning gives the right answer'; we distinguish these by requiring the correct SIGN FLIP specifically in Simpson's-flip cases, the distinct-axis probe, and the dose-response sign-crossing, none of which a naive conditioner passes without representing adjustment.", 'Synthetic SCM data gives exact ground truth but may not reflect real confounding structure; we cross-validate on semi-realistic tables (confounding-by-indication, batch confounding) where the true adjusted effect is known or estimable, and report synthetic-vs-real agreement, treating synthetic as the decisive-counterfactual testbed and real as the transfer check.', 'Linear probes recovering an adjustment direction do not guarantee causal use; we address this with steering plus random/observational-direction controls, though distributed/superposed encodings may blur single-direction interventions - we use both probe directions and delta-representations.', "A negative result (models encode observational association and fail to flip under confounder presence in Simpson's-flip cases) is scientifically decisive and directly safety-relevant - it would warn against trusting tabular/scientific foundation models for causal-effect estimation in confounded domains (drug outcomes, clinical, omics) where confounding by indication or batch dominates; we frame either outcome as the first mechanism-defining, representational test of whether foundation models represent the backdoor confounder-adjustment mechanism.", 'All experiments are inference-only on public pretrained models (TabPFN, a tabular baseline) plus small probes and synthetic/semi-realistic tabular datasets, keeping compute at academic scale.']}
